Object.assign(LESSONS,{
 f2:{id:"f2",code:"F2 · Lesson 1",title:"Logic: the three acts of the mind",
  outcomes:["Name and distinguish the three acts of the intellect and the logical product of each.","Classify propositions as A, E, I or O and state their relations on the square of opposition.","Test a categorical syllogism for a valid middle term.","Identify the five predicables and a definition by genus and difference.","Spot equivocation and begging the question in an argument."],
  prereq:["univocal","distinction","science"],
  reading:{primary:[["ST I q.13 a.5 (univocal, equivocal, analogical)",NA("1013.htm#article5")],["ST I q.2 a.2 ad 2 (the middle term in demonstration)",NA("1002.htm#article2")]],
   secondary:["Kreeft, P. Socratic Logic. South Bend, IN: St Augustine's Press. [verify edition and year]","McInerny, R. (1996) Aquinas and Analogy. Washington, DC: Catholic University of America Press.","Copi, I.M., Cohen, C. and McMahon, K. (2014) Introduction to Logic. 14th edn. Harlow: Pearson."]},
  scripture:[["Romans 1:20, read as an argument from effects",null]],
  sections:[
   {id:"s1",h:"1. Three acts of the intellect",html:`<p>Thomas, following Aristotle, distinguishes three things the mind does. Logic gives rules for each.</p>
<div class="anatomy"><div class="part"><div class="label">Simple apprehension</div><div>Grasping what something is. Its product is a <strong>term</strong> or concept: "man", "mortal". Not true or false.</div></div>
<div class="part"><div class="label">Judgment</div><div>Joining or separating concepts: "man is mortal". Its product is a <strong>proposition</strong>, which is true or false.</div></div>
<div class="part"><div class="label">Reasoning</div><div>Moving from known judgments to a new one. Its product is an <strong>argument</strong>, valid or invalid.</div></div></div>
<p>This order governs Thomas's commentaries on Aristotle's logic: the <em>Categories</em> concern terms, <em>On Interpretation</em> propositions, the <em>Analytics</em> reasoning (In Peri Herm. I, lect. 1).</p>`,
    check:Q("\"Every man is mortal\" is the product of which act?",[["Simple apprehension","That yields a term such as \"man\"."],["Judgment","Correct: it joins two concepts and is true or false."],["Reasoning","Reasoning yields an argument from premises."]],1)},
   {id:"s2",h:"2. Propositions and the square of opposition",html:`<p>A categorical proposition joins a subject and predicate by quantity (all, some) and quality (affirm, deny):</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Form</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>A</td><td>Every S is P</td><td>Every man is mortal</td></tr><tr><td>E</td><td>No S is P</td><td>No man is a stone</td></tr>
<tr><td>I</td><td>Some S is P</td><td>Some man is wise</td></tr><tr><td>O</td><td>Some S is not P</td><td>Some man is not wise</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<ol><li><strong>Contradictories</strong> (A–O, E–I): one must be true and the other false.</li><li><strong>Contraries</strong> (A–E): cannot both be true, can both be false.</li><li><strong>Subcontraries</strong> (I–O): cannot both be false.</li><li><strong>Subalterns</strong> (A→I, E→O): the universal implies the particular (in traditional logic, where the subject exists).</li></ol>
<div class="box warn"><div class="label">Common misunderstanding</div><p>To refute "every philosopher is a sceptic" you need only the contradictory, "some philosopher is not a sceptic", not the contrary "no philosopher is a sceptic".</p></div>`,
    check:Q("The contradictory of \"No Thomist is a sceptic\" is…",[["Every Thomist is a sceptic","That is the contrary; both can be false."],["Some Thomist is a sceptic","Correct: E and I are contradictories."],["Some Thomist is not a sceptic","That is the subaltern (E → O)."]],1)},
   {id:"s3",h:"3. The syllogism and the middle term",html:`<p>A syllogism has three terms. The <strong>middle term</strong> appears in both premises and joins the other two; it does not appear in the conclusion.</p>
<div class="anatomy"><div class="part"><div class="label">Major premise</div><div>Every animal is mortal.</div></div><div class="part"><div class="label">Minor premise</div><div>Every man is an animal.</div></div><div class="part"><div class="label">Conclusion</div><div>Therefore every man is mortal.</div></div></div>
<p>Key rules: the middle term must be taken universally at least once; no term may be wider in the conclusion than in the premises; two negative premises prove nothing; and the middle term must mean the same thing in both premises.</p>
<p>In a <em>demonstration</em> the middle term is a cause or definition. When we demonstrate God's existence from effects, "the effect takes the place of the definition of the cause" as middle term (ST I q.2 a.2 ad 2).</p>`,
    check:Q("Which argument is invalid because the middle term is never taken universally?",[["Every dog is an animal; every cat is an animal; so every cat is a dog.","Correct: \"animal\" is undistributed in both premises."],["Every man is mortal; Socrates is a man; so Socrates is mortal.","Valid."],["No stone is alive; every granite is a stone; so no granite is alive.","Valid (Celarent)."]],0)},
   {id:"s4",h:"4. Predicables and definition",html:`<p>Porphyry lists five ways something general is said of something: <strong>genus</strong>, <strong>species</strong>, <strong>difference</strong>, <strong>property</strong> and <strong>accident</strong>. Of man: animal is the genus, rational the difference, able to laugh a property (always present but not part of the essence), pale an accident.</p>
<div class="def"><div class="label">Definition</div>A real definition states the essence by proximate genus and specific difference: "Man is a rational animal" (De ente c.2).</div>
<p>Thomas uses this in theology by showing where it fails: God is in no genus, so God cannot be defined (ST I q.3 a.5).</p>`,
    check:Q("\"Able to laugh\" (risibile) said of man is a…",[["genus","Too wide and part of the essence."],["specific difference","The difference is \"rational\"."],["property","Correct: it always follows the essence without being part of it."],["accident","An accident can come and go, like being pale."]],2)},
   {id:"s5",h:"5. Fallacies and the danger of equivocation",html:`<p>A fallacy is an argument that seems valid but is not. Two matter most in theology:</p>
<ol><li><strong>Equivocation</strong>: a word shifts meaning between premises. Thomas uses this to show why God-talk cannot be purely equivocal: "if that were so, it follows that from creatures nothing could be known or demonstrated about God at all; for the reasoning would always be exposed to the fallacy of equivocation" (ST I q.13 a.5).</li>
<li><strong>Begging the question</strong> (petitio principii): assuming what is to be proved.</li></ol>
<p>Thomas's solution is <strong>analogy</strong>: names said of God and creatures "according to proportion", neither in exactly the same sense nor in unrelated senses.</p>
<div class="box conn"><div class="label">Connections</div><p>RES 320-style methodology has the same worry under another name: a construct must be operationalised the same way across the premises of a study, or the inference fails.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("Why does Thomas reject purely equivocal names for God?",[["Because God is a creature.","No."],["Because then every argument from creatures to God would commit equivocation.","Correct: ST I q.13 a.5."],["Because Scripture uses metaphors.","Not his argument here."]],1)}
  ],
  quiz:[
   Q("Which act of the intellect produces something true or false?",[["Simple apprehension","Concepts are not true or false."],["Judgment","Correct."],["Reasoning","Arguments are valid or invalid."],["Imagination","Not an act of the intellect."]],1,"In Peri Herm. I, lect. 1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["three-acts"],diff:1}),
   Q("\"Some saints are not martyrs\" is an…",[["A proposition","A is universal affirmative."],["E proposition","E is universal negative."],["I proposition","I is particular affirmative."],["O proposition","Correct: particular negative."]],3,"Square of opposition",{bloom:"Apply",terms:["proposition"],diff:1}),
   Q("Contraries (A and E)…",[["can both be true","No."],["cannot both be true but can both be false","Correct."],["must differ in truth value","That is contradictories."],["are always both false","Not always."]],1,"Square of opposition",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["proposition"],diff:2}),
   Q("In \"Every animal is mortal; every man is an animal; so every man is mortal\", the middle term is…",[["man","The minor term."],["mortal","The major term."],["animal","Correct: in both premises, not the conclusion."],["every","A quantifier, not a term."]],2,"Syllogism",{bloom:"Apply",terms:["middle-term"],diff:1}),
   Q("In demonstrating that God exists from effects, what serves as middle term?",[["God's essence","Unknown to us."],["The meaning of the name \"God\" (with the effect)","Correct: ST I q.2 a.2 ad 2."],["The Trinity","Not reachable by reason."],["Faith","Not a term of a demonstration."]],1,"ST I q.2 a.2 ad 2",{bloom:"Analyse",terms:["middle-term","demonstration-quia"],diff:3}),
   Q("\"Rational\" in \"man is a rational animal\" is the…",[["genus","Animal is the genus."],["specific difference","Correct."],["property","No."],["accident","No."]],1,"Predicables",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["predicables","genus-difference"],diff:1}),
   Q("Why can God not be defined?",[["Because God is unknowable in every way","Too strong; we know that he is."],["Because God is in no genus","Correct: ST I q.3 a.5."],["Because definitions are only for words","No."],["Because God is a species","No."]],1,"ST I q.3 a.5",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["genus-difference"],diff:2}),
   Q("\"Every bank has money; the river has a bank; so the river has money\" commits…",[["begging the question","No."],["equivocation","Correct: \"bank\" shifts meaning."],["false cause","No."],["composition","No."]],1,"Fallacies",{bloom:"Apply",terms:["fallacy","univocal"],diff:1}),
   Q("Names said of God and creatures are, for Thomas…",[["univocal","Rejected in ST I q.13 a.5."],["purely equivocal","Rejected: knowledge of God would be impossible."],["analogous, according to proportion","Correct."],["meaningless","No."]],2,"ST I q.13 a.5",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["analogy"],diff:1}),
   Q("To refute \"Every psychologist is a determinist\", you need only show…",[["No psychologist is a determinist","The contrary; more than you need."],["Some psychologist is not a determinist","Correct: the contradictory."],["Some psychologist is a determinist","Consistent with the claim."],["Every determinist is a psychologist","Irrelevant conversion."]],1,"Square of opposition",{bloom:"Apply",terms:["proposition"],diff:2})
  ],
  construct:{q:"Whether names are said univocally of God and creatures?",ref:"ST I q.13 a.5",
   model:{obj:"It seems they are univocal: every equivocal term is reduced to a univocal one, as many are reduced to one; and we know God from creatures, which would be impossible unless names were common to both.",sc:"Damascene and Dionysius: whatever is said of God and creatures is not said in the same sense. More directly, God is not in a genus with creatures.",resp:"Univocal predication is impossible, because perfections divided in creatures exist in God undivided, so \"wise\" said of God exceeds what the name signifies in us. Nor are names purely equivocal, or nothing could be known of God from creatures. So they are said analogically, according to proportion: of creatures by their order to God as principle and cause.",rep:"The equivocal is reduced not to the univocal but to the analogical, as to its first principle."},
   rubric:["Objection argues for univocity.","Respondeo rejects univocity with a reason.","Respondeo rejects pure equivocity with a reason.","Respondeo concludes to analogy according to proportion.","Reply corrects the objection's principle."]},
  drill:{title:"Put a syllogism in order",parts:["Major premise: every animal is mortal.","Minor premise: every man is an animal.","Conclusion: every man is mortal.","Check: the middle term (animal) is taken universally in the major.","Check: no term is wider in the conclusion than in the premises."]},
  connections:`<p><strong>Psychology.</strong> Belief bias, the tendency to judge an argument valid because its conclusion is believable, is one of the best-documented effects in reasoning research (Evans, Barston and Pollard, 1983, <em>Memory &amp; Cognition</em>, 11(3), 295–306). <a class="chip ok" href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6621345/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PubMed 6621345</a> Training in form, as in this lesson, is the classical remedy.</p><p><strong>Other modules.</strong> F4 uses the predicables and categories; T3 uses demonstration quia; F6 uses analogy.</p>`},

 f3:{id:"f3",code:"F3 · Lesson 1",title:"Philosophy of nature: change, form and the four causes",
  outcomes:["State the three principles of change: subject, form and privation.","Distinguish substantial from accidental change.","Explain act and potency with examples.","Identify the four causes of a natural thing.","Explain why \"every agent, of necessity, acts for an end\" (ST I-II q.1 a.2)."],
  prereq:["act","potency","motion","four-causes"],
  reading:{primary:[["ST I-II q.1 a.2 (every agent acts for an end)",NA("2001.htm#article2")],["ST I q.2 a.3 (the First Way uses act and potency)",NA("1002.htm#article3")],["De principiis naturae (Latin, Corpus Thomisticum)","https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/opn.html"]],
   secondary:["Feser, E. (2019) Aristotle's Revenge: The Metaphysical Foundations of Physical and Biological Science. Neunkirchen-Seelscheid: Editiones Scholasticae.","Oderberg, D.S. (2007) Real Essentialism. London: Routledge.","Wallace, W.A. (1996) The Modeling of Nature. Washington, DC: Catholic University of America Press."]},
  scripture:[["Wisdom 11:21: \"thou hast ordered all things in measure, and number, and weight\"",null]],
  sections:[
   {id:"s1",h:"1. Parmenides' puzzle and the three principles of change",html:`<p>Parmenides argued that change is impossible: a thing cannot come from being (it already is) or from non-being (nothing comes from nothing). Aristotle's answer, which Thomas adopts, is that change comes from being <em>in potency</em>.</p>
<p>Every change has three principles (De principiis naturae c.1):</p>
<ol><li>A <strong>subject</strong> that persists (the bronze).</li><li>A <strong>form</strong> gained (the statue's shape).</li><li>A <strong>privation</strong>, the lack of that form before the change (unshaped bronze).</li></ol>`,
    check:Q("In water becoming hot, the privation is…",[["the water","That is the subject."],["the heat","That is the form gained."],["the water's lack of heat","Correct."]],2)},
   {id:"s2",h:"2. Act and potency",html:`<p><strong>Potency</strong> is a real capacity to be something; <strong>act</strong> is being it. Cold water is potentially hot and actually cold. Motion is defined through them: "the reduction of something from potentiality to actuality" (ST I q.2 a.3).</p>
<div class="def"><div class="label">Principle</div>Nothing is reduced from potency to act except by something already in act (ST I q.2 a.3). This principle drives the First Way.</div>
<div class="box warn"><div class="label">Common misunderstanding</div><p>Potency is not nothing and not mere logical possibility. A stone and an acorn are both "possibly an oak" in logic, but only the acorn has the real potency.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("Which statement is the principle behind the First Way?",[["Everything has a beginning in time.","Not Thomas's premise."],["Nothing passes from potency to act except by something in act.","Correct."],["Every change is locomotion.","Too narrow."]],1)},
   {id:"s3",h:"3. Matter and form; substantial and accidental change",html:`<p>Bodily substances are composed of <strong>matter</strong> (potential principle) and <strong>form</strong> (actual principle). This is hylomorphism (from Greek <em>hylē</em>, matter, and <em>morphē</em>, form).</p>
<ol><li><strong>Accidental change</strong>: the substance remains and gains or loses an accidental form (a man becoming tanned).</li><li><strong>Substantial change</strong>: one substance becomes another (a living dog becoming a corpse). The subject is <em>prime matter</em>, pure potency with no form of its own.</li></ol>
<p>Thomas holds that each substance has one substantial form (ST I q.76 a.4). In man that form is the soul, as F5 will show.</p>`,
    check:Q("A log burned to ash is an example of…",[["accidental change","The log does not remain."],["substantial change","Correct: one substance becomes another."],["locomotion only","Not only."]],1)},
   {id:"s4",h:"4. The four causes and nature",html:`<p>To explain a thing fully we name four causes (Physics II): <strong>material</strong> (what it is made of), <strong>formal</strong> (what it is), <strong>efficient</strong> (what made it), <strong>final</strong> (what it is for).</p>
<p>A <strong>nature</strong> is "a principle of motion and rest" within a thing. Natural things act regularly toward ends without knowing them. Thomas states the principle: "Every agent, of necessity, acts for an end… in other things, it is caused by their natural inclination, which is called the 'natural appetite'" (ST I-II q.1 a.2).</p>
<p><strong>Chance</strong> does not abolish ends: it is an unintended result of causes that were acting for ends.</p>`,
    check:Q("For an acorn, the final cause is…",[["the oak tree it tends to become","Correct."],["the soil","A condition, not a cause in this sense."],["the parent tree","The efficient cause."]],0)},
   {id:"s5",h:"5. Worked example: \"every agent acts for an end\"",html:`<div class="anatomy">
 <div class="part"><div class="label">Question</div><div>Whether it is proper to the rational nature to act for an end? (ST I-II q.1 a.2)</div></div>
 <div class="part"><div class="label">Respondeo</div><div>"Every agent, of necessity, acts for an end." The first of all causes is the final cause: an agent produces a determinate effect only if it is determined to it, and that determination has the nature of an end.</div></div>
 <div class="part"><div class="label">Distinction</div><div>Things tend to an end in two ways: moving themselves to it, as man by free will; or moved by another, "as an arrow tends to a determinate end through being moved by the archer".</div></div>
 <div class="part"><div class="label">Conclusion</div><div>Acting for an end belongs to all agents, but acting for an end <em>known as an end</em> belongs to rational nature.</div></div></div>`,
    check:Q("According to ST I-II q.1 a.2, which is the first of all causes?",[["The efficient cause","It moves only for an end."],["The material cause","No."],["The final cause","Correct."]],2)}
  ],
  quiz:[
   Q("The three principles of change are…",[["matter, energy, time","Modern physics terms."],["subject, form, privation","Correct."],["act, potency, motion","Related, but not the three principles."],["cause, effect, law","No."]],1,"De principiis naturae c.1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["privation","matter-form"],diff:1}),
   Q("Aristotle's answer to Parmenides is that change comes from…",[["absolute non-being","That is Parmenides' dilemma."],["being in act","It already is."],["being in potency","Correct."],["illusion","That is Parmenides' conclusion."]],2,"In Phys. I",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["potency"],diff:2}),
   Q("Motion is defined as…",[["any change of place","Too narrow."],["the reduction of something from potency to act","Correct: ST I q.2 a.3."],["energy in transit","Anachronistic."],["the passage of time","No."]],1,"ST I q.2 a.3",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["motion"],diff:1}),
   Q("Which is a substantial change?",[["A leaf turning red","Accidental."],["A man learning Latin","Accidental."],["Food becoming part of a living body","Correct."],["A ball rolling","Locomotion."]],2,"Matter and form",{bloom:"Apply",terms:["substantial-form"],diff:2}),
   Q("The subject of substantial change is…",[["a complete substance","That is the subject of accidental change."],["prime matter","Correct."],["the efficient cause","No."],["a privation","Privation is a principle, not the subject."]],1,"De principiis naturae c.2",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["matter-form"],diff:3}),
   Q("The sculptor is the statue's…",[["material cause","Bronze."],["formal cause","The shape."],["efficient cause","Correct."],["final cause","The purpose."]],2,"Four causes",{bloom:"Apply",terms:["four-causes","efficient-cause"],diff:1}),
   Q("Nature is…",[["the totality of living things","A modern usage."],["a principle of motion and rest in a thing","Correct."],["whatever is not man-made","Too loose."],["chance","No."]],1,"In Phys. II, lect. 1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["nature"],diff:1}),
   Q("Thomas says natural things act for an end by…",[["conscious choice","Only rational agents."],["natural appetite, or inclination","Correct: ST I-II q.1 a.2."],["chance","Chance presupposes ends."],["the will of the observer","No."]],1,"ST I-II q.1 a.2",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["final-cause","nature"],diff:2}),
   Q("Finding treasure while digging a grave is an example of…",[["final causality","Not intended."],["chance","Correct: an unintended outcome of causes acting for ends."],["substantial change","No."],["formal causality","No."]],1,"In Phys. II, lect. 8",{bloom:"Apply",terms:["chance"],diff:1}),
   Q("Why is potency not the same as logical possibility?",[["Because potency is real in a thing, grounded in its nature","Correct."],["Because potency is imaginary","No."],["Because only God has potency","God is pure act."],["They are the same","No."]],0,"Act and potency",{bloom:"Analyse",terms:["potency"],diff:3})
  ],
  construct:{q:"Whether every agent acts for an end?",ref:"ST I-II q.1 a.2",
   model:{obj:"It seems not: things without knowledge, like stones, cannot intend an end, and acting for an end requires intention.",sc:"The Philosopher (Physics II): nature acts for an end.",resp:"Every agent acts for an end, because an agent produces a determinate effect only if determined to it, and that determination has the nature of an end. In rational beings it comes from the will; in others from natural inclination. Some move themselves to the end, others are moved to it by another, as the arrow by the archer.",rep:"Things without knowledge do not intend the end themselves; they are directed to it by natural appetite, given by one who knows."},
   rubric:["Objection appeals to lack of knowledge.","Respondeo argues from the determinacy of effects.","Respondeo distinguishes rational and natural appetite.","The arrow and archer example appears.","Reply applies the distinction."]},
  drill:{title:"Put the account of change in order",parts:["A subject exists with a privation (bronze without shape).","An agent already in act acts on it (the sculptor).","The subject is reduced from potency to act.","The subject gains a new form (the statue's shape).","The privation is removed; the subject persists under the new form."]},
  connections:`<p><strong>Psychology.</strong> Thomas's \"natural appetite\" is a teleological concept. Modern biology and psychology often speak the same way of functions and goal-directed systems, while debating whether such language is literal (see Mayr's distinction between teleonomy and teleology). Keep the difference clear: a function is not a conscious purpose.</p><p><strong>Other modules.</strong> F5 applies matter and form to the human soul; T3 uses act and potency in the First Way.</p>`},

 f4:{id:"f4",code:"F4 · Lesson 1",title:"Metaphysics: being, essence and existence",
  outcomes:["Distinguish substance and accident and name the ten categories.","Explain the real distinction between essence and existence in creatures.","State why in God essence and existence are identical (ST I q.3 a.4).","List the transcendentals and say what each adds to being.","Explain participation and why God is \"subsistent being itself\"."],
  prereq:["essence","esse","participation","analogy"],
  reading:{primary:[["ST I q.3 a.4 (essence and existence in God)",NA("1003.htm#article4")],["De ente et essentia (Latin, Corpus Thomisticum)","https://www.corpusthomisticum.org/oee.html"],["ST I q.5 a.1 (good and being)",NA("1005.htm#article1")]],
   secondary:["Wippel, J.F. (2000) The Metaphysical Thought of Thomas Aquinas. Washington, DC: Catholic University of America Press, chs 5–6.","Feser, E. (2014) Scholastic Metaphysics: A Contemporary Introduction. Neunkirchen-Seelscheid: Editiones Scholasticae.","Gilson, É. (1952) Being and Some Philosophers. 2nd edn. Toronto: PIMS."]},
  scripture:[["Exodus 3:14, \"I am who am\"",null],["John 5:26, life \"in himself\"","j5-26"]],
  sections:[
   {id:"s1",h:"1. Substance and the categories",html:`<p>Metaphysics studies <strong>being as being</strong>. The first division of being is into <strong>substance</strong>, which exists in itself, and <strong>accident</strong>, which exists in another.</p>
<p>The ten categories: substance; and nine accidents: quantity, quality, relation, action, passion, where, when, position, state. Socrates is a substance; his height, paleness and being in the Agora are accidents.</p>
<div class="box warn"><div class="label">Common misunderstanding</div><p>"Accident" here does not mean a mishap. It means a real but dependent mode of being. The theology of the Eucharist (ST III q.75–77) depends on getting this right.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("\"Taller than Plato\" said of Socrates falls under which category?",[["Quantity","Height alone is quantity; \"taller than\" is a relation."],["Relation","Correct."],["Quality","No."]],1)},
   {id:"s2",h:"2. Essence and existence",html:`<p>The <strong>essence</strong> of a thing is what it is; its <strong>existence</strong> (esse) is that it is. In <em>De ente et essentia</em> (c.4), Thomas argues that in creatures these are really distinct:</p>
<ol><li>We can understand what a thing is (a man, a phoenix) without knowing whether it exists.</li><li>So existence is not part of the essence of such things.</li><li>What a thing has besides its essence comes either from its essential principles or from an outside cause.</li><li>Nothing can be the cause of its own existence.</li><li>So every thing whose essence differs from its existence has existence from another, and ultimately from one whose essence <em>is</em> its existence.</li></ol>
<p class="muted ui" style="font-size:.8rem">Chapter numbering of De ente differs between editions (c.3, c.4 or c.5); the Leonine is c.4. Scholars debate whether step 1 alone proves a real distinction (see Wippel 2000, ch. 5).</p>`,
    check:Q("The \"intellectus essentiae\" argument begins from the fact that…",[["we can know what something is without knowing that it exists","Correct."],["everything exists necessarily","No."],["essences are mental only","No."]],0)},
   {id:"s3",h:"3. God as subsistent being",html:`<p>In God, essence and existence are the same. Thomas's first argument (ST I q.3 a.4):</p>
<div class="quote">"Whatever a thing has besides its essence must be caused either by the constituent principles of that essence… or by some exterior agent… Therefore that thing, whose existence differs from its essence, must have its existence caused by another. But this cannot be true of God; because we call God the first efficient cause."<span class="src">ST I q.3 a.4 · Dominican tr. (1920), public domain</span></div>
<p>His second argument: existence is to essence as act to potency, and there is no potency in God. Hence God is <em>ipsum esse subsistens</em>, subsistent being itself. This is Thomas's reading of "I am who am" (Exodus 3:14).</p>`,
    check:Q("Why can God's existence not differ from his essence, in Thomas's first argument?",[["Because then his existence would be caused by another, but he is the first cause","Correct."],["Because God has no essence","He has; it is his existence."],["Because God is a body","No."]],0)},
   {id:"s4",h:"4. Participation and the transcendentals",html:`<p>Creatures <strong>participate</strong> in being: they have it, but are not it. "What is by essence is the cause of what is by participation", as fire is of what is hot (compare Super Ioan. cap. 5, lect. 5, read in the Reader at John 5:26).</p>
<p>Some features belong to every being, crossing all categories: the <strong>transcendentals</strong> (De veritate q.1 a.1):</p>
<ol><li><strong>Thing</strong> (res): being considered as having an essence.</li><li><strong>One</strong>: being as undivided.</li><li><strong>Something</strong> (aliquid): being as divided from others.</li><li><strong>True</strong>: being as related to intellect.</li><li><strong>Good</strong>: being as related to appetite.</li></ol>
<p>So "goodness and being are really the same, and differ only in idea" (ST I q.5 a.1).</p>`,
    check:Q("\"True\" as a transcendental names being as related to…",[["appetite","That is good."],["intellect","Correct."],["other beings","That is \"something\"."]],1)},
   {id:"s5",h:"5. Analogy of being",html:`<p>"Being" is not said in exactly the same sense of substance and accident, or of God and creatures, nor in unrelated senses. It is said <strong>analogically</strong>. Accidents are beings because they belong to substances; creatures are beings because they participate in the being of God, who is being by essence.</p>
<div class="box conn"><div class="label">Connections</div><p>The analogy of being is the bridge between metaphysics (F4) and the divine names (F6). Later Thomists (Cajetan, <em>De nominum analogia</em>, 1498) systematised types of analogy; whether Cajetan read Thomas rightly is still debated.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("How is \"being\" said of God and creatures?",[["Univocally","Rejected."],["Equivocally","Rejected."],["Analogically","Correct."]],2)}
  ],
  quiz:[
   Q("A substance is…",[["what exists in another","That is an accident."],["what exists in itself and not in another","Correct."],["any material","No."],["a property","No."]],1,"De ente c.1; ST I q.3 a.5 ad 1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["substance"],diff:1}),
   Q("Which is not one of the nine accidents?",[["Quality","It is an accident."],["Relation","It is an accident."],["Substance","Correct: substance is the first category, not an accident."],["Place","It is an accident."]],2,"Categories",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["categories","accident"],diff:1}),
   Q("In creatures, essence and existence are…",[["identical","Only in God."],["really distinct","Correct."],["both accidents","No."],["unknowable","No."]],1,"De ente c.4",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["essence","esse"],diff:1}),
   Q("\"Ipsum esse subsistens\" means…",[["being in general","No: esse commune is not God."],["subsistent being itself","Correct."],["the sum of all beings","Pantheism; rejected."],["a highest substance among others","God is not in a genus."]],1,"ST I q.4 a.2; q.3 a.4",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["esse"],diff:2}),
   Q("Thomas's second argument in ST I q.3 a.4 is that existence relates to essence as…",[["form to matter","Not his formulation."],["act to potency","Correct."],["accident to substance","No."],["part to whole","No."]],1,"ST I q.3 a.4",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["act","esse"],diff:2}),
   Q("Which is a transcendental?",[["Quantity","A category."],["Good","Correct."],["Animal","A genus."],["White","An accident."]],1,"De veritate q.1 a.1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["transcendentals"],diff:1}),
   Q("Good and being, according to ST I q.5 a.1, are…",[["really distinct","No."],["really the same, differing in idea","Correct."],["unrelated","No."],["opposed","No."]],1,"ST I q.5 a.1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["transcendentals"],diff:2}),
   Q("A creature has being by…",[["essence","Only God."],["participation","Correct."],["accident","No."],["convention","No."]],1,"ST I q.44 a.1",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["participation"],diff:1}),
   Q("The view that \"being\" means exactly the same in God and creatures is…",[["Thomas's","No."],["univocity, which Thomas rejects","Correct (later held by Scotus in a different form)."],["equivocity","No."],["analogy","No."]],1,"ST I q.13 a.5",{bloom:"Analyse",terms:["analogy","univocal"],diff:3}),
   Q("The Eucharistic doctrine of accidents without subject depends on which distinction?",[["Act and potency","Related, but not the key one here."],["Substance and accident","Correct: ST III q.77 a.1."],["Matter and form","Not the key one."],["Genus and species","No."]],1,"ST III q.77 a.1",{bloom:"Apply",terms:["substance","accident"],diff:2})
  ],
  construct:{q:"Whether essence and existence are the same in God?",ref:"ST I q.3 a.4",
   model:{obj:"It seems not: if God's essence were his existence, he would be being in general, which is predicated of all things, and so he would be in everything as part of it.",sc:"Hilary: in God, being is not an accident but subsisting truth. Also Exodus 3:14 read as God's proper name.",resp:"Whatever a thing has besides its essence is caused by its essential principles or by an exterior agent. Existence cannot be caused by a thing's own principles, so where existence differs from essence it is caused by another. God is the first cause, so his existence cannot differ from his essence. Also, existence is to essence as act to potency, and there is no potency in God.",rep:"Being in general (esse commune) is open to addition; God's being is not, because it is his very essence and admits nothing added."},
   rubric:["Objection confuses God's being with being in general.","Respondeo argues from causality.","Respondeo argues from act and potency.","Conclusion: God is his own existence.","Reply distinguishes esse commune from God's esse."]},
  drill:{title:"Put the essence–existence argument in order",parts:["We can know what a thing is without knowing whether it exists.","So in such things existence is other than essence.","What a thing has besides its essence is caused by its principles or by another.","Nothing causes its own existence, so such existence is caused by another.","The series ends in a being whose essence is its existence: God."]},
  connections:`<p><strong>Thomistic connection.</strong> Your Honours topic on moral responsibility will lean on F4 indirectly: the person as a <em>substance</em> of a rational nature (Boethius, ST I q.29 a.1) is what persists through change and so can be held responsible.</p><p><strong>Other modules.</strong> T3 (simplicity, ST I q.3) and T13 (transubstantiation) depend directly on this lesson.</p>`},

 f5:{id:"f5",code:"F5 · Lesson 1",title:"Philosophical anthropology: soul, intellect, will and passions",
  outcomes:["Explain why the intellectual soul is the form of the body (ST I q.76 a.1).","Describe how the intellect knows material things through phantasms and abstraction.","Explain the basis of free choice (ST I q.83 a.1).","List the eleven passions under the concupiscible and irascible powers.","Compare Thomas's account of the passions with a modern appraisal theory of emotion."],
  prereq:["soul-form","intellect","phantasm","will","passions"],
  reading:{primary:[["ST I q.76 a.1 (soul as form)",NA("1076.htm#article1")],["ST I q.84 a.7 (turning to phantasms)",NA("1084.htm#article7")],["ST I q.83 a.1 (free choice)",NA("1083.htm#article1")],["ST I-II q.23 a.4 (the eleven passions)",NA("2023.htm#article4")]],
   secondary:["Pasnau, R. (2002) Thomas Aquinas on Human Nature. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.","Miner, R. (2009) Thomas Aquinas on the Passions. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.","Brock, S.L. (2015) The Philosophy of Saint Thomas Aquinas: A Sketch. Eugene, OR: Cascade."]},
  scripture:[["Genesis 2:7, man formed from the dust and given the breath of life",null],["John 1:9, the light that enlightens every man","j1-9"]],
  sections:[
   {id:"s1",h:"1. The soul is the form of the body",html:`<p>The soul is the first principle of life in a living body. Thomas argues that the intellectual soul is the body's form:</p>
<div class="quote">"Each one is conscious that it is himself who understands."<span class="src">ST I q.76 a.1 · Dominican tr. (1920), public domain</span></div>
<ol><li>That by which a thing first acts is its form.</li><li>The soul is that by which we first live, sense, move and understand.</li><li>So the intellectual soul is the form of the body.</li><li>Otherwise understanding would not be <em>this man's</em> act, yet each of us knows it is himself who understands.</li></ol>
<p>So a human being is one substance, not a soul using a body. The Council of Vienne (1312) taught that the rational soul is of itself and essentially (per se et essentialiter) the form of the body; see CCC 365.</p>`,
    check:Q("Thomas's appeal to experience in ST I q.76 a.1 is that…",[["each one is conscious that it is himself who understands","Correct."],["we see our souls","No."],["the body thinks without a soul","No."]],0)},
   {id:"s2",h:"2. Intellect, phantasm and abstraction",html:`<p>The intellect grasps universals, so it does not operate through a bodily organ, and the soul is subsistent (ST I q.75 a.2). Yet in this life we understand only by turning to <strong>phantasms</strong>, images from the senses:</p>
<div class="quote">"In the present state of life in which the soul is united to a passible body, it is impossible for our intellect to understand anything actually, except by turning to the phantasms."<span class="src">ST I q.84 a.7</span></div>
<p>Thomas's evidence includes brain injury: when "the act of the imagination is hindered by a lesion of the corporeal organ… a man is hindered from actually understanding things of which he had a previous knowledge" (ST I q.84 a.7). The <strong>agent intellect</strong> abstracts the universal from the phantasm (ST I q.85 a.1).</p>
<div class="box conn"><div class="label">Connections</div><p>This is a striking point for a psychology student: Thomas makes intellect immaterial yet dependent, in this life, on bodily powers. Cognitive impairment after brain injury does not refute his view; he uses it as evidence.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("According to ST I q.84 a.7, a lesion that hinders imagination…",[["has no effect on understanding","Thomas says the opposite."],["hinders actual understanding even of what was known before","Correct."],["destroys the soul","No."]],1)},
   {id:"s3",h:"3. Will and free choice",html:`<p>The will is the rational appetite: it tends to the good as understood. Thomas's argument for free choice:</p>
<div class="quote">"Man has free-will: otherwise counsels, exhortations, commands, prohibitions, rewards, and punishments would be in vain."<span class="src">ST I q.83 a.1</span></div>
<ol><li>Stones act without judgment; animals act from judgment, but natural judgment (the sheep flees the wolf by instinct).</li><li>Man judges by comparison in reason.</li><li>In contingent matters reason can see opposite courses as good.</li><li>So man's judgment is free, and he can be inclined to various things.</li></ol>`,
    check:Q("Why does the sheep fleeing the wolf not act freely?",[["Because it acts from natural, not rational, judgment","Correct."],["Because it does not judge at all","Thomas says it does judge, naturally."],["Because wolves are evil","No."]],0)},
   {id:"s4",h:"4. The passions of the soul",html:`<p>The <strong>passions</strong> are movements of the sense appetite with bodily change. Thomas sorts eleven (ST I-II q.23 a.4):</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Power</th><th>Toward good</th><th>Away from evil</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Concupiscible (good or evil simply)</td><td>love · desire · joy</td><td>hatred · aversion · sorrow</td></tr>
<tr><td>Irascible (good or evil as arduous)</td><td>hope · daring</td><td>despair · fear · anger (no contrary)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The passions are not sins in themselves. They are good when ordered by reason, which, quoting Aristotle, governs them "by a politic and royal power", not a despotic one (ST I q.81 a.3 ad 2): the passions can resist, so reason must win them over, not simply command.</p>`,
    check:Q("Which passions belong to the irascible power?",[["Love, desire, joy","Concupiscible."],["Hope, despair, fear, daring, anger","Correct."],["Hatred, aversion, sorrow","Concupiscible."]],1)},
   {id:"s5",h:"5. Connections with emotion research",html:`<p><span class="voice">Later Thomists</span>Modern readers note that Thomas's passions are triggered by an <em>apprehension</em> of good or evil, and the irascible ones by its difficulty. That resembles cognitive appraisal theories, in which emotions follow evaluations of relevance, goal-congruence and coping potential (Lazarus, 1991, <em>Emotion and Adaptation</em>, Oxford University Press).</p>
<p>The fit is partial. Appraisal theories are empirical models of process; Thomas offers a philosophical account of the appetite's objects and its order to reason. Use the comparison to clarify both, not to equate them.</p>
<div class="box conn"><div class="label">Connections</div><p>Clinical link: CBT works on the appraisals that feed emotions, much as Thomas's \"political rule\" of reason persuades the passions rather than suppressing them. This is an analogy, not a historical claim about CBT's origins.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("In Thomas, the irascible passions concern good or evil as…",[["arduous, hard to attain or avoid","Correct."],["simply pleasant or painful","That is the concupiscible."],["imaginary","No."]],0)}
  ],
  quiz:[
   Q("Thomas's conclusion in ST I q.76 a.1 is that…",[["the intellect is a separate substance for all men","That is Averroism, which he rejects."],["the intellectual soul is the form of the human body","Correct."],["the soul is the body","Materialism; rejected."],["the soul uses the body as a sailor uses a ship","Platonic model; rejected."]],1,"ST I q.76 a.1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["soul-form"],diff:1}),
   Q("How many substantial forms does a human being have?",[["Three: vegetative, sensitive, rational","Thomas rejects plurality."],["One, the intellectual soul","Correct: ST I q.76 a.4."],["None","No."],["Two","No."]],1,"ST I q.76 a.4",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["substantial-form","soul-form"],diff:2}),
   Q("Why must the intellect operate without a bodily organ?",[["Because it grasps universal natures","Correct: ST I q.75 a.2."],["Because the brain is unimportant","Not his argument."],["Because angels have no bodies","Irrelevant."],["Because the senses deceive","Not his argument."]],0,"ST I q.75 a.2",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["intellect"],diff:2}),
   Q("In this life, the intellect understands only by…",[["direct intuition of essences","No."],["turning to phantasms","Correct."],["innate ideas","Rejected."],["divine illumination alone","No."]],1,"ST I q.84 a.7",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["phantasm"],diff:1}),
   Q("The agent intellect…",[["receives intelligible species","That is the possible intellect."],["abstracts intelligible species from phantasms","Correct."],["is a bodily organ","No."],["is the imagination","No."]],1,"ST I q.79 a.3; q.85 a.1",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["agent-intellect","abstraction"],diff:2}),
   Q("Thomas's opening reason for free will is that otherwise…",[["God would be cruel","Not his argument here."],["counsels, commands, rewards and punishments would be in vain","Correct."],["animals would be free","No."],["the passions would rule","No."]],1,"ST I q.83 a.1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["free-choice"],diff:1}),
   Q("Which passion has no contrary?",[["Fear","Contrary: daring."],["Hope","Contrary: despair."],["Anger","Correct: ST I-II q.23 a.3."],["Love","Contrary: hatred."]],2,"ST I-II q.23 a.3",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["passions"],diff:2}),
   Q("Reason rules the passions by…",[["despotic rule","Thomas says political, not despotic."],["political rule","Correct: ST I q.81 a.3 ad 2."],["no rule at all","No."],["suppression","No."]],1,"ST I q.81 a.3 ad 2",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["passions"],diff:2}),
   Q("Desire for a pleasant meal belongs to the…",[["irascible power","No difficulty involved."],["concupiscible power","Correct."],["will alone","It is a sense appetite."],["intellect","No."]],1,"ST I q.81 a.2",{bloom:"Apply",terms:["concupiscible"],diff:1}),
   Q("Thomas uses cases of brain injury to show that…",[["the soul is material","No."],["the intellect needs bodily powers to act in this life","Correct."],["understanding is impossible","No."],["the soul dies with the body","No."]],1,"ST I q.84 a.7",{bloom:"Analyse",terms:["phantasm","intellect"],diff:3})
  ],
  construct:{q:"Whether the intellectual principle is united to the body as its form?",ref:"ST I q.76 a.1",
   model:{obj:"It seems not: the intellect is separate (Aristotle, De anima III) and not the act of any body; so it cannot be the form of the body.",sc:"The Philosopher (Metaph. VIII): difference is derived from form; the difference constituting man is \"rational\", which is said of him by reason of the intellectual principle. So it is man's form.",resp:"That by which a thing first acts is its form. The soul is that by which we first live, sense, move and understand. So the intellectual soul is the form of the body. Otherwise understanding would not be this man's act, though each one is conscious that it is himself who understands.",rep:"The intellect is called separate because it is not the act of a bodily organ, not because the soul is not the form of the body: the soul exceeds the body in a power that does not use an organ."},
   rubric:["Objection invokes the intellect's separateness.","Sed contra argues from the difference \"rational\".","Respondeo argues from first principle of activity.","Respondeo uses the argument from self-awareness.","Reply distinguishes the soul from one of its powers."]},
  drill:{title:"Put the steps of human knowing in order",parts:["The external senses receive sensible forms from things.","The imagination forms a phantasm.","The agent intellect abstracts the intelligible species.","The possible intellect receives it and understands.","The intellect turns back to phantasms to know the individual thing."]},
  connections:`<p><strong>Psychology.</strong> Compare section 5 with your SLK and KRM work: appraisal-based models of emotion (Lazarus, 1991; Scherer, 2009, <em>Cognition and Emotion</em>, 23(7), 1307–1351 <span class="chip verify">verify citation</span>) and CBT's focus on appraisals sit close to Thomas's account of the passions as moved by apprehension. They are not identical: Thomas is giving a philosophy of the appetite, not a process model.</p><p><strong>Other modules.</strong> T6 builds the moral life on this anthropology (habits, virtues, the passions in moral acts).</p>`},

 f6:{id:"f6",code:"F6 · Lesson 1",title:"Natural theology: what reason can know of God",
  outcomes:["State what natural reason can and cannot know of God (ST I q.12 a.12).","Explain the three ways: causality, negation and eminence.","Explain why names like \"good\" signify God's substance, though imperfectly (ST I q.13 a.2).","Distinguish names said properly from names said metaphorically.","Relate these claims to Vatican I and CCC 36–43."],
  prereq:["analogy","five-ways","three-ways","divine-names"],
  reading:{primary:[["ST I q.12 a.12 (God known by natural reason)",NA("1012.htm#article12")],["ST I q.13 a.2–5 (the divine names)",NA("1013.htm")],["CCC 31–43",'https://www.vatican.va/archive/ENG0015/_INDEX.HTM']],
   secondary:["Davies, B. (2004) An Introduction to the Philosophy of Religion. 3rd edn. Oxford: Oxford University Press.","te Velde, R. (2006) Aquinas on God: The 'Divine Science' of the Summa Theologiae. Aldershot: Ashgate.","Kerr, F. (2002) After Aquinas: Versions of Thomism. Oxford: Blackwell, ch. 4."]},
  scripture:[["Romans 1:20",null],["Wisdom 13:5: \"by the greatness of the beauty… the creator of them may be seen\"",null],["John 1:18: no one has ever seen God","j1-18"]],
  sections:[
   {id:"s1",h:"1. How far reason reaches",html:`<div class="quote">"Our natural knowledge begins from sense… But because they are His effects and depend on their cause, we can be led from them so far as to know of God 'whether He exists,' and to know of Him what must necessarily belong to Him, as the first cause of all things, exceeding all things caused by Him."<span class="src">ST I q.12 a.12 · Dominican tr. (1920), public domain</span></div>
<p>Three things follow (same article): we know God's relation to creatures as their cause; that creatures differ from him, since he is no part of them; and that this is not by any defect in him, "but because He superexceeds them all".</p>
<p>Reason knows <em>that</em> God is, not <em>what</em> he is: "Reason cannot reach up to simple form, so as to know 'what it is'; but it can know 'whether it is'" (ad 1).</p>`,
    check:Q("By natural reason we can know of God…",[["his essence","No: ST I q.12 a.12."],["that he exists and what belongs to him as first cause","Correct."],["that he is three persons","No: revelation."]],1)},
   {id:"s2",h:"2. The three ways: causality, negation, eminence",html:`<p>The three ways named in ST I q.12 a.12 structure all of Thomas's natural theology:</p>
<ol><li><strong>Causality</strong>: God is the cause of all things.</li><li><strong>Negation</strong> (remotion): what belongs to creatures as creatures is removed from him: not material, not changing, not in time. Hence ST I q.3–11 (simplicity, immutability, eternity) mostly say what God is <em>not</em>.</li><li><strong>Eminence</strong>: every perfection in creatures is in God in a higher way.</li></ol>
<div class="box warn"><div class="label">Common misunderstanding</div><p>The negative way is not agnosticism. Saying God is not a body or not in time is real knowledge about him.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("\"God is eternal, not in time\" is mainly an instance of the way of…",[["causality","Not mainly."],["negation","Correct."],["eminence","Partly, but chiefly negation."]],1)},
   {id:"s3",h:"3. Do names signify God's substance?",html:`<p>Two views Thomas rejects: that names like "good" only deny defects (Rabbi Moses, Maimonides), or only mean "cause of goodness". Against both, he argues for example that then God could be called "a body", since he causes bodies (ST I q.13 a.2).</p>
<div class="quote">"Therefore we must hold a different doctrine—viz. that these names signify the divine substance, and are predicated substantially of God, although they fall short of a full representation of Him."<span class="src">ST I q.13 a.2</span></div>
<p>The reason: our intellect knows God from creatures, and every creature represents him so far as it has some perfection, "not as something of the same species or genus, but as the excelling principle".</p>`,
    check:Q("Thomas's objection to \"God is good = God causes goodness\" is that…",[["then God could equally be called a body, since he causes bodies","Correct."],["God does not cause goodness","No."],["goodness is not a perfection","No."]],0)},
   {id:"s4",h:"4. Proper and metaphorical names",html:`<p>Some names signify a perfection without any limitation in its meaning (being, good, wise, living). These are said of God <strong>properly</strong>, though in a higher way. Others include a creaturely mode in their very meaning (rock, lion, angry). These are said of God <strong>metaphorically</strong> (ST I q.13 a.3).</p>
<p>All proper names are said by <strong>analogy</strong>, as F2 and F4 showed (ST I q.13 a.5). And they are said first of God, as source, and only then of creatures, though we learn them the other way round (ST I q.13 a.6).</p>`,
    check:Q("\"God is my rock\" is said…",[["properly","\"Rock\" includes a bodily mode."],["metaphorically","Correct."],["univocally","No."]],1)},
   {id:"s5",h:"5. The Magisterium and the limits of reason",html:`<p><span class="voice">Magisterium</span>Vatican I teaches that God "can be known with certainty from the created world by the natural light of human reason" (Dei Filius ch. 2, as given in CCC 36). The Catechism adds that revelation is needed even for truths reason can reach, so that all can know them with ease, with firm certainty and without error (CCC 38). This echoes ST I q.1 a.1 (lesson F1).</p>
<p><span class="voice">Later Thomists</span>Modern debates press on the Kantian claim that reason cannot pass beyond experience. Thomists reply that causal reasoning from effects is not a leap beyond experience but an analysis of what experience contains (see Davies 2004).</p>
<div class="box conn"><div class="label">Connections</div><p>F6 completes Year 0. T3 now continues with the attributes: simplicity (q.3), perfection and goodness (q.4–6), infinity (q.7–8) and on through providence (q.22).</p></div>`,
    check:Q("CCC 38 says revelation is needed even for truths reason can reach, so that they are known…",[["by everyone, with ease, certainty and without error","Correct."],["only by philosophers","No."],["without reason","No."]],0)}
  ],
  quiz:[
   Q("Natural knowledge of God begins from…",[["innate ideas","No."],["sense","Correct: ST I q.12 a.12."],["mystical experience","No."],["Scripture","That is revelation."]],1,"ST I q.12 a.12",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["reason"],diff:1}),
   Q("Reason can know of God…",[["what he is","No: ad 1."],["whether he is","Correct."],["both equally","No."],["neither","No."]],1,"ST I q.12 a.12 ad 1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["three-ways"],diff:1}),
   Q("The three ways are…",[["faith, hope, charity","Theological virtues."],["causality, negation, eminence","Correct."],["motion, cause, contingency","The first three of the Five Ways."],["literal, moral, anagogical","Senses of Scripture."]],1,"ST I q.12 a.12",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["three-ways"],diff:1}),
   Q("Whom does Thomas name as holding that names of God only remove defects?",[["Augustine","No."],["Rabbi Moses (Maimonides)","Correct: ST I q.13 a.2."],["Aristotle","No."],["Dionysius","He is cited elsewhere."]],1,"ST I q.13 a.2",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["divine-names"],diff:2}),
   Q("According to ST I q.13 a.2, names like \"good\" said of God…",[["signify his substance, though imperfectly","Correct."],["signify nothing about him","No."],["signify only his effects","Rejected."],["fully comprehend him","No."]],0,"ST I q.13 a.2",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["divine-names"],diff:2}),
   Q("Which name is said of God properly, not metaphorically?",[["Lion","Metaphor."],["Rock","Metaphor."],["Wise","Correct."],["Shepherd","Metaphor."]],2,"ST I q.13 a.3",{bloom:"Apply",terms:["divine-names"],diff:1}),
   Q("Proper names are said first of…",[["creatures, in the order of the thing","Only in the order of our learning."],["God, in the order of the thing","Correct: ST I q.13 a.6."],["neither","No."],["angels","No."]],1,"ST I q.13 a.6",{bloom:"Analyse",terms:["analogy"],diff:3}),
   Q("The negative way is best described as…",[["agnosticism","No."],["real knowledge of what God is not","Correct."],["denying God exists","No."],["mystical silence only","No."]],1,"ST I q.3 prol.",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["three-ways"],diff:2}),
   Q("Dei Filius (1870) teaches that God…",[["cannot be known without faith","Opposite."],["can be known with certainty by natural reason from created things","Correct; CCC 36."],["is known by the Five Ways only","No."],["is known by feeling","No."]],1,"CCC 36",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["reason"],diff:1}),
   Q("CCC 38 on the moral necessity of revelation echoes which article?",[["ST I q.1 a.1","Correct."],["ST I q.2 a.3","The Five Ways."],["ST III q.1 a.1","The Incarnation."],["ST I-II q.94 a.2","Natural law."]],0,"CCC 38; ST I q.1 a.1",{bloom:"Analyse",terms:["revelation"],diff:2})
  ],
  construct:{q:"Whether any name can be applied to God substantially?",ref:"ST I q.13 a.2",
   model:{obj:"It seems not: Damascene says everything said of God signifies not his substance but what he is not, or a relation, or something following his nature.",sc:"Augustine (De Trin. VI): the being of God is the being strong or wise; so such names signify the divine substance.",resp:"Negative and relative names do not signify God's substance. But absolute names like good and wise do: our intellect knows God from creatures, and every creature represents him so far as it has some perfection, as the excelling principle. Views that reduce these names to negation or causality fail, since then God could be called a body because he causes bodies.",rep:"Damascene means these names do not signify what God is in a way that comprehends him, not that they signify nothing of his substance."},
   rubric:["Objection argues names signify only negation or relation.","Sed contra cites an authority for substantial predication.","Respondeo distinguishes negative/relative from absolute names.","Respondeo refutes the two rival views with the \"body\" argument.","Reply reinterprets the authority with a distinction."]},
  drill:{title:"Order the steps from creatures to divine names",parts:["We know creatures through the senses.","Creatures are effects that depend on a cause.","Reason concludes that the first cause exists.","It removes from that cause every creaturely limitation.","It attributes to it every perfection in a higher, analogical way."]},
  connections:`<p><strong>Thomistic connection.</strong> The three ways also govern how we speak of persons: we know another's mind only through effects (words, actions), never directly. Clinical assessment works the same way, inferring inner states from observable signs, which is why it must stay humble about what it cannot see.</p><p><strong>Other modules.</strong> T3 continues with the attributes; T4 (Trinity) shows what reason cannot reach.</p>`}
});

