Object.assign(LESSONS,{
 f7:{id:"f7",code:"F7 · Lesson 1",title:"Ethics: happiness, virtue and natural law",
  outcomes:["Explain why no created good can be man's happiness (ST I-II q.2 a.8).","State why perfect happiness is the vision of God (q.3 a.8).","Define virtue and explain why it is a habit (q.55 a.4).","Give the definition of law (q.90 a.4) and the four kinds of law.","State the first precept of natural law and the order of natural inclinations (q.94 a.2)."],
  prereq:["final-cause","habitus","synderesis","will"],
  reading:{primary:[["ST I-II q.2 a.8 (no created good is happiness)",NA("2002.htm#article8")],["ST I-II q.3 a.8 (vision of the divine essence)",NA("2003.htm#article8")],["ST I-II q.55 a.4 (definition of virtue)",NA("2055.htm#article4")],["ST I-II q.90 a.4; q.91 a.2; q.94 a.2 (law and natural law)",NA("2094.htm#article2")]],
   secondary:["Pinckaers, S. (1995) The Sources of Christian Ethics. Translated by M.T. Noble. Washington, DC: Catholic University of America Press.","McInerny, R. (1997) Ethica Thomistica. Rev. edn. Washington, DC: Catholic University of America Press.","Porter, J. (2005) Nature as Reason: A Thomistic Theory of the Natural Law. Grand Rapids, MI: Eerdmans."]},
  scripture:[["Matthew 5:3–12, the Beatitudes",null],["Romans 2:14–15, the law written on the heart",null],["John 4:13–14, water that ends thirst","j4-10"]],
  sections:[
   {id:"s1",h:"1. The last end and the search for happiness",html:`<p>Every agent acts for an end (F3), and so does every human life. Thomas asks what our final end is, and tests the usual candidates: wealth, honour, fame, power, bodily goods, pleasure (ST I-II q.2 a.1–6). Each fails because it is partial, can be lost, or serves something else.</p>
<div class="quote">"It is impossible for any created good to constitute man's happiness. For happiness is the perfect good, which lulls the appetite altogether… Wherefore God alone can satisfy the will of man."<span class="src">ST I-II q.2 a.8 · Dominican tr. (1920), public domain</span></div>
<p>The argument: the will's object is the universal good; every creature is good only by participation (F4); so only God, who is goodness itself, can fill the will.</p>`,
    check:Q("Why can no created good be our happiness, in ST I-II q.2 a.8?",[["Because created goods are evil","No: they are good, but partially."],["Because the will seeks the universal good, found in God alone","Correct."],["Because pleasure is sinful","Not his argument."]],1)},
   {id:"s2",h:"2. Happiness as the vision of God",html:`<p>Perfect happiness is an activity of the intellect: the vision of God's essence (ST I-II q.3 a.8). Thomas's argument runs from wonder:</p>
<ol><li>Man is not perfectly happy while anything remains to desire.</li><li>Knowing an effect, we naturally desire to know what its cause is. This desire is wonder.</li><li>From creatures we know <em>that</em> God is, not <em>what</em> he is (F6).</li><li>So the desire remains until the intellect reaches "the very Essence of the First Cause".</li></ol>
<p>Imperfect happiness is possible here through the virtues; perfect happiness only in the life to come.</p>
<div class="box conn"><div class="label">Connections</div><p>Psychology distinguishes hedonic well-being (pleasure, satisfaction) from eudaimonic well-being (meaning, growth, living well) (Ryan and Deci, 2001, <em>Annual Review of Psychology</em>, 52, 141–166). <a class="chip ok" href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11148302/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PubMed 11148302</a> Thomas's ethics is eudaimonist, but ends where empirical models cannot follow: in God.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("Thomas's argument for the vision of God starts from…",[["the desire to know what the cause is, once we know that it is","Correct: wonder."],["fear of punishment","No."],["the commands of Scripture alone","He argues from nature here."]],0)},
   {id:"s3",h:"3. Virtue",html:`<p>Happiness in this life comes through virtue. A virtue is a good <strong>habit</strong> (habitus), a stable disposition that makes good action prompt, easy and joyful. Thomas accepts the received definition, "a good quality of the mind, by which we live righteously, of which no one can make bad use, which God works in us, without us", while noting it "would be more suitable if for 'quality' we substitute 'habit,' which is the proximate genus" (ST I-II q.55 a.4).</p>
<ol><li><strong>Intellectual virtues</strong> perfect the intellect (wisdom, science, understanding, art, prudence).</li><li><strong>Moral virtues</strong> perfect the appetite; the cardinal virtues are prudence, justice, fortitude and temperance (q.61).</li><li>Moral virtue observes a <strong>mean</strong> fixed by reason (q.64 a.1): courage lies between cowardice and recklessness.</li></ol>
<p class="muted ui" style="font-size:.8rem">The last clause, \"which God works in us, without us\", fits infused virtue, which God causes in us \"without any action on our part, but not without our consent\" (q.55 a.4 ad 6). Acquired virtues are caused by our own repeated acts (q.63 a.2).</p>`,
    check:Q("What genus does Thomas prefer in the definition of virtue?",[["Quality","Accepted but not proximate."],["Habit","Correct: the proximate genus."],["Passion","No."]],1)},
   {id:"s4",h:"4. Law and its four kinds",html:`<div class="quote">"[A law] is nothing else than an ordinance of reason for the common good, made by him who has care of the community, and promulgated."<span class="src">ST I-II q.90 a.4</span></div>
<p>Four parts of the definition, four questions (q.90 a.1–4): law belongs to reason; it aims at the common good; it is made by the one responsible for the community; it must be made known.</p>
<div class="anatomy">
 <div class="part"><div class="label">Eternal law</div><div>God's wise plan directing all things (q.91 a.1).</div></div>
 <div class="part"><div class="label">Natural law</div><div>"The rational creature's participation of the eternal law" (q.91 a.2).</div></div>
 <div class="part"><div class="label">Human law</div><div>Particular determinations made by human authority, derived from natural law (q.95).</div></div>
 <div class="part"><div class="label">Divine law</div><div>Revealed law, Old and New, needed because our end is supernatural (q.91 a.4).</div></div></div>`,
    check:Q("Which is NOT part of Thomas's definition of law?",[["An ordinance of reason","Part of it."],["For the common good","Part of it."],["Enforced by punishment","Correct: not in the definition."],["Promulgated","Part of it."]],2)},
   {id:"s5",h:"5. The first precept of natural law",html:`<p>Natural law's precepts are self-evident to practical reason, as first principles are to speculative reason (F2). As "being" is first in the speculative order, "good" is first in the practical:</p>
<div class="quote">"Hence this is the first precept of law, that 'good is to be done and pursued, and evil is to be avoided.' All other precepts of the natural law are based upon this."<span class="src">ST I-II q.94 a.2</span></div>
<p>The order of precepts follows the order of natural inclinations:</p>
<ol><li>What we share with all substances: preserving our being (life).</li><li>What we share with animals: union of man and woman, raising children.</li><li>What is proper to reason: to know the truth about God and to live in society.</li></ol>
<div class="box warn"><div class="label">Common misunderstanding</div><p>Natural law is not "whatever is natural is right". It is reason's grasp of the goods our nature is ordered to. Inclinations are taken up and ordered by reason, not simply obeyed.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("The first precept of natural law is…",[["Love God above all","Divine law, not the first natural precept."],["Good is to be done and pursued, and evil avoided","Correct."],["Preserve your life","A precept of the first inclination."]],1)}
  ],
  quiz:[
   Q("Which goods does Thomas examine and reject as happiness in I-II q.2?",[["Wealth, honour, fame, power, bodily goods, pleasure","Correct."],["Faith, hope, charity","Theological virtues."],["Being, one, true, good","Transcendentals."],["Matter, form, privation","Principles of change."]],0,"ST I-II q.2 a.1–6",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["beatitude"],diff:1}),
   Q("Only God can satisfy the will because…",[["the will's object is the universal good, and creatures are good by participation","Correct: q.2 a.8."],["God commands it","Not the argument."],["the will is evil","No."],["happiness is a feeling","No."]],0,"ST I-II q.2 a.8",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["beatitude","participation"],diff:2}),
   Q("Perfect happiness consists in…",[["the vision of the divine essence","Correct: q.3 a.8."],["a long, healthy life","Imperfect at best."],["moral virtue alone","Imperfect happiness."],["pleasure","Rejected."]],0,"ST I-II q.3 a.8",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["beatitude","beatific-vision"],diff:1}),
   Q("Virtue is a…",[["passion","No."],["habit","Correct."],["power","No."],["act","Virtue is the source of good acts."]],1,"ST I-II q.55",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["virtue","habitus"],diff:1}),
   Q("Courage as a mean between cowardice and recklessness illustrates…",[["the mean of moral virtue fixed by reason","Correct: q.64 a.1."],["the mean of theological virtue","Theological virtues have no mean in this sense."],["mediocrity","A misunderstanding."],["the middle term","Logic, not ethics."]],0,"ST I-II q.64 a.1",{bloom:"Apply",terms:["virtue"],diff:2}),
   Q("Law is \"an ordinance of reason for the common good, made by him who has care of the community, and…\"",[["enforced","Not in the definition."],["promulgated","Correct."],["written","Not required: natural law is unwritten."],["obeyed","Not in the definition."]],1,"ST I-II q.90 a.4",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["law"],diff:1}),
   Q("Natural law is…",[["the laws studied by physics","No."],["the rational creature's participation in the eternal law","Correct: q.91 a.2."],["the customs of a society","No."],["revealed law","That is divine law."]],1,"ST I-II q.91 a.2",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["natural-law","eternal-law"],diff:1}),
   Q("Why is divine law needed besides natural law?",[["Because our end is supernatural, beyond natural powers","Correct: q.91 a.4."],["Because natural law is false","No."],["Because human law is enough","No."],["Because reason is useless","No."]],0,"ST I-II q.91 a.4",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["law"],diff:2}),
   Q("The inclination to know the truth about God belongs to…",[["what we share with all substances","That is self-preservation."],["what we share with animals","That is procreation and rearing."],["what is proper to reason","Correct."],["none of these","No."]],2,"ST I-II q.94 a.2",{bloom:"Apply",terms:["natural-law"],diff:2}),
   Q("A student says: \"Natural law means whatever comes naturally is right.\" What is wrong?",[["Nothing","It misreads Thomas."],["Natural law is reason's ordering of inclinations, not raw impulse","Correct."],["Natural law concerns only animals","No."],["Natural law is the same as divine law","No."]],1,"ST I-II q.94 a.2",{bloom:"Evaluate",terms:["natural-law"],diff:3})
  ],
  construct:{q:"Whether the natural law contains several precepts, or only one?",ref:"ST I-II q.94 a.2",
   model:{obj:"It seems it contains only one: law is a kind of precept, so if there were many natural precepts there would be many natural laws.",sc:"The precepts of natural law are to practical reason what first principles are to demonstration; there are several first principles, so there are several natural precepts.",resp:"As being is first in speculative reason, good is first in practical reason; so the first precept is that good is to be done and pursued and evil avoided. Whatever practical reason apprehends as man's good belongs to natural law. The order of precepts follows the order of natural inclinations: to preserve being, to what we share with animals, and to what is proper to reason.",rep:"All these precepts are one natural law in so far as they flow from one first precept."},
   rubric:["Objection argues for one precept.","Respondeo compares practical to speculative first principles.","Respondeo states the first precept.","Respondeo lists the three levels of inclination.","Reply reconciles many precepts with one law."]},
  drill:{title:"Put the argument for the vision of God in order",parts:["Man is not perfectly happy while anything remains to be desired.","Knowing an effect, we desire to know what its cause is.","From creatures we know that God is, not what he is.","So the desire to know remains while we know God only through effects.","Perfect happiness is reached only in seeing the divine essence."]},
  connections:`<p><strong>Psychology.</strong> Habit research shows that repeated behaviour in a stable context becomes automatic over weeks to months (median around 66 days in one study; Lally et al., 2010, <em>European Journal of Social Psychology</em>, 40(6), 998–1009). <span class="chip verify">verify citation</span> Thomas's <em>habitus</em> is richer: a habit of reason and choice, not only an automatic response. The comparison is useful in T6 and T8.</p><p><strong>Other modules.</strong> T6 builds on this lesson (human acts, passions, habits, law, grace).</p>`},

 f9:{id:"f9",code:"F9 · Lesson 1",title:"The Fathers, the councils and the life of Thomas",
  outcomes:["State what makes a writer a Father of the Church, and how this differs from a Doctor.","Place the four great early councils and the error each answered.","Name the main Fathers in the Catena aurea and their dates.","Outline the life of Thomas with key dates and places.","Explain the medieval setting: universities, mendicant orders, the recovery of Aristotle."],
  prereq:["catena","tradition","magisterium"],
  reading:{primary:[["Dei Verbum 8 (the Fathers as witnesses of Tradition)",'https://www.vatican.va/archive/hist_councils/ii_vatican_council/documents/vat-ii_const_19651118_dei-verbum_en.html'],["Catena aurea in Ioannem, Newman tr.","https://isidore.co/aquinas/CAJohn.htm"],["Leo XIII, Aeterni Patris (1879)","https://www.vatican.va/content/leo-xiii/en/encyclicals/documents/hf_l-xiii_enc_04081879_aeterni-patris.html"]],
   secondary:["Torrell, J.-P. (2005) Saint Thomas Aquinas, Volume 1: The Person and His Work. Rev. edn. Washington, DC: Catholic University of America Press.","Weisheipl, J.A. (1983) Friar Thomas d'Aquino: His Life, Thought and Works. Washington, DC: Catholic University of America Press.","Ramsey, B. (1985) Beginning to Read the Fathers. New York: Paulist Press."]},
  scripture:[["John 1:1–18 with the Fathers of the Catena","j1-1"]],
  sections:[
   {id:"s1",h:"1. Who the Fathers are",html:`<p>The <strong>Fathers of the Church</strong> are early Christian writers recognised by four marks: orthodoxy of doctrine, holiness of life, approval of the Church, and antiquity. The patristic age is usually closed with Isidore of Seville (d. 636) in the West and John Damascene (d. c. 749) in the East.</p>
<p><span class="voice">Magisterium</span>"The words of the holy fathers witness to the presence of this living tradition" (Dei Verbum 8).</p>
<p>A <strong>Doctor of the Church</strong> is a different title, given for eminent teaching at any period. Thomas is a Doctor (1567), not a Father.</p>`,
    check:Q("Which is NOT one of the traditional marks of a Father of the Church?",[["Antiquity","A mark."],["Holiness of life","A mark."],["Writing in Latin","Correct: Greek and Syriac Fathers count equally."],["Approval of the Church","A mark."]],2)},
   {id:"s2",h:"2. Four councils, four errors",html:`<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Council</th><th>Year</th><th>Error answered</th><th>Teaching</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Nicaea I</td><td>325</td><td>Arius: the Son is a creature</td><td>The Son is consubstantial (homoousios) with the Father</td></tr>
<tr><td>Constantinople I</td><td>381</td><td>Denial of the Spirit's divinity</td><td>The Holy Spirit is Lord and giver of life, worshipped with the Father and the Son</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ephesus</td><td>431</td><td>Nestorius: two persons in Christ</td><td>Mary is Theotokos, Mother of God</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chalcedon</td><td>451</td><td>Eutyches: one mixed nature</td><td>One person in two natures, without confusion or division</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>You met these errors in the Reader: Thomas reads John 1:1 against Arius and Sabellius, and John 1:14 against Nestorius and Eutyches.</p>`,
    check:Q("Which council taught that Mary is Theotokos?",[["Nicaea I","325, on the Son's divinity."],["Ephesus","Correct: 431."],["Chalcedon","451, on the two natures."]],1)},
   {id:"s3",h:"3. The Fathers of the Catena",html:`<p>In the Reader's chain on John 1–6, the most cited Fathers are:</p>
<ol><li><strong>Augustine</strong> (354–430), bishop of Hippo: Tractates on John. About 317 extracts.</li><li><strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (c. 349–407): Homilies on John, close to the literal sense. About 289.</li><li><strong>Theophylact</strong> (c. 1055–after 1107): Byzantine condenser of Chrysostom. About 90.</li><li><strong>Alcuin</strong> (c. 735–804) and <strong>Bede</strong> (673–735): Latin transmitters of the tradition.</li><li><strong>Origen</strong> (c. 185–c. 254): the first great exegete, used selectively.</li><li><strong>Hilary</strong> (c. 310–367): defender of Nicaea.</li></ol>
<p>Note that Thomas includes some writers after the patristic age, such as Theophylact and Alcuin. The <em>Catena</em> gathers the exegetical tradition, not only the Fathers in the strict sense.</p>`,
    check:Q("Which writer in the Catena is not a Father in the strict sense, being too late?",[["Augustine","A Father."],["Theophylact","Correct: 11th–12th century."],["Hilary","A Father."]],1)},
   {id:"s4",h:"4. The life of Thomas",html:`<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Event</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>c. 1225</td><td>Born at Roccasecca, near Aquino; later an oblate at Monte Cassino</td></tr>
<tr><td>1239–44</td><td>Studies at Naples; joins the Dominicans (1244); detained by his family</td></tr>
<tr><td>1245–52</td><td>Studies under Albert the Great in Paris and Cologne</td></tr>
<tr><td>1252–56</td><td>Bachelor of the Sentences at Paris; commentary on Lombard</td></tr>
<tr><td>1256–59</td><td>Master of theology at Paris</td></tr>
<tr><td>1259–68</td><td>Italy: Orvieto (1261–65), Rome (1265–68); begins the Summa; Catena aurea</td></tr>
<tr><td>1268–72</td><td>Second Paris regency; Lectura on John</td></tr>
<tr><td>1272–73</td><td>Naples; Psalms; stops writing around 6 December 1273</td></tr>
<tr><td>7 March 1274</td><td>Dies at Fossanova on the way to the Council of Lyon</td></tr>
<tr><td>1323 · 1567 · 1879</td><td>Canonised (John XXII) · Doctor of the Church (Pius V) · Aeterni Patris (Leo XIII)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="muted ui" style="font-size:.8rem">Dates follow Torrell (2005); some, such as his birth year and the Italian period, are approximate and debated.</p>`,
    check:Q("Under whom did Thomas study in Paris and Cologne?",[["Bonaventure","A contemporary, not his teacher."],["Albert the Great","Correct."],["Peter Lombard","Died a century earlier (1160)."]],1)},
   {id:"s5",h:"5. The medieval setting",html:`<ol><li><strong>Universities</strong>: Paris was the centre of theology. Teaching ran through <em>lectio</em> (reading a text), <em>quaestio</em> and <em>disputatio</em>: this is the scholastic method behind every article.</li>
<li><strong>Mendicant orders</strong>: Dominicans (1216) and Franciscans lived by begging and preaching, and their entry into the university was fiercely contested; Thomas defended it.</li>
<li><strong>Aristotle</strong>: his works reached the Latin West in the 12th–13th centuries, partly through Arabic translations and commentators (Avicenna, Averroes). Thomas's achievement was to receive Aristotle while correcting him in the light of faith.</li>
<li><strong>Peter Lombard's Sentences</strong> (c. 1150s): the standard theology textbook; every master commented on it.</li></ol>
<div class="box conn"><div class="label">Connections</div><p>Leo XIII's <em>Aeterni Patris</em> (1879) made Thomas the model for Catholic philosophical study, which is why this course exists in its present form.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("The standard medieval theology textbook was…",[["the Summa Theologiae","Thomas's own later work."],["Peter Lombard's Sentences","Correct."],["Aristotle's Ethics","Philosophy, not theology."]],1)}
  ],
  quiz:[
   Q("Thomas Aquinas is a…",[["Father of the Church","Too late for the patristic age."],["Doctor of the Church","Correct: 1567."],["Apostolic Father","No."],["Desert Father","No."]],1,"F9 s1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["doctor-church"],diff:1}),
   Q("The patristic age traditionally ends in the West with…",[["Augustine","He died in 430; the age continues."],["Isidore of Seville","Correct (d. 636)."],["Thomas Aquinas","Much later."],["Bede","Bede died 735; the usual marker is Isidore."]],1,"F9 s1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["father-church"],diff:2}),
   Q("Nicaea I (325) answered…",[["Nestorius","Ephesus."],["Arius","Correct."],["Eutyches","Chalcedon."],["Pelagius","Not an ecumenical council's main subject."]],1,"Councils",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["arianism","ecumenical-council"],diff:1}),
   Q("Chalcedon (451) taught that Christ is…",[["one nature after the union","That is Eutyches' view."],["one person in two natures","Correct."],["two persons","That is Nestorianism."],["a creature","That is Arianism."]],1,"Councils",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["hypostatic-union"],diff:1}),
   Q("Which Father is cited most often in the Catena on John 1–6?",[["Origen","About 83 times."],["Augustine","Correct, about 317."],["Bede","About 77."],["Basil","Only a few."]],1,"Catena data",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["catena"],diff:2}),
   Q("Thomas died in…",[["1274 at Fossanova","Correct."],["1225 at Roccasecca","His birth."],["1323 at Avignon","His canonisation."],["1256 at Paris","His inception."]],0,"Torrell 2005",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:1}),
   Q("The scholastic method is built on…",[["lectio, quaestio, disputatio","Correct."],["observation and experiment","Modern science."],["allegory alone","No."],["private revelation","No."]],0,"F9 s5",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["scholasticism"],diff:1}),
   Q("Aeterni Patris (1879) was issued by…",[["Pius V","He declared Thomas a Doctor."],["Leo XIII","Correct."],["John XXII","He canonised Thomas."],["Pius X","No."]],1,"Aeterni Patris",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:1}),
   Q("Why did Thomas include Theophylact and Alcuin in a chain of the Fathers?",[["He thought they were Fathers in the strict sense","Not the point."],["The Catena gathers the exegetical tradition, not only the strict patristic age","Correct."],["They were Greek","Alcuin was English."],["By mistake","No."]],1,"F9 s3",{bloom:"Analyse",terms:["catena","father-church"],diff:3}),
   Q("Thomas's characteristic achievement regarding Aristotle was to…",[["reject him entirely","No."],["adopt him uncritically","No."],["receive him while correcting him in the light of faith","Correct."],["translate him from Arabic","Not Thomas's work."]],2,"F9 s5",{bloom:"Understand",terms:[],diff:2})
  ],
  construct:{q:"Whether sacred doctrine may argue from the authority of the Fathers? (adapted)",ref:"ST I q.1 a.8 ad 2",
   model:{obj:"It seems not: argument from authority is the weakest form of proof, and theology is a science.",sc:"Theology properly uses authority, since its principles come by revelation.",resp:"Argument from authority is weakest in human sciences but most fitting in sacred doctrine, whose principles are revealed. Theology uses the authority of canonical Scripture properly and with necessity; the authority of the doctors of the Church properly but only with probability; and philosophers as extrinsic and probable arguments.",rep:"Authority founded on human reason is the weakest; authority founded on divine revelation is the strongest."},
   rubric:["Objection states that authority is the weakest argument.","Respondeo distinguishes human from divine authority.","Respondeo ranks Scripture, the doctors, and philosophers.","Scripture is said to give necessary proof; the doctors probable.","Reply applies the distinction."]},
  drill:{title:"Put Thomas's life in order",parts:["Born at Roccasecca and sent to Monte Cassino.","Joins the Dominicans at Naples and is detained by his family.","Studies under Albert the Great in Paris and Cologne.","Teaches as master in Paris, then in Italy, then in Paris again.","Stops writing in Naples and dies at Fossanova in 1274."]},
  connections:`<p><strong>South African connection.</strong> St John Vianney Seminary in Pretoria trains priests in this tradition, and St Augustine College in Johannesburg teaches it in a degree programme; both are named in the Phase 1 report.</p><p><strong>Other modules.</strong> Every Reader page uses the Fathers; T1 explains why their witness carries weight (Tradition).</p>`},

 t1:{id:"t1",code:"T1 · Lesson 1",title:"Fundamental theology: revelation, Tradition and faith",
  outcomes:["Explain how revelation is handed on through Scripture and Tradition (Dei Verbum 7–10).","Distinguish the material and formal object of faith (ST II-II q.1 a.1).","Explain faith as thinking with assent, moved by the will (II-II q.2 a.1).","Distinguish believing God, believing in a God, believing in God (q.2 a.2).","Explain how doctrine develops without changing (II-II q.1 a.7; Newman)."],
  prereq:["revelation","faith","tradition","magisterium","object-faith"],
  reading:{primary:[["Dei Verbum (1965) §§ 2–10",'https://www.vatican.va/archive/hist_councils/ii_vatican_council/documents/vat-ii_const_19651118_dei-verbum_en.html'],["ST II-II q.1 a.1, a.7",NA("3001.htm")],["ST II-II q.2 a.1–2",NA("3002.htm")],["ST II-II q.4 a.1 (definition of faith)",NA("3004.htm#article1")]],
   secondary:["Newman, J.H. (1878) An Essay on the Development of Christian Doctrine. 2nd edn. London: Longmans, Green. (Public domain.)","Dulles, A. (1992) Models of Revelation. Maryknoll, NY: Orbis.","Levering, M. (2014) Engaging the Doctrine of Revelation. Grand Rapids, MI: Baker Academic."]},
  scripture:[["Hebrews 11:1, faith as the substance of things hoped for",null],["John 3:33–36, receiving Christ's testimony","j3-33"],["John 4:39–42, faith from hearing","j4-39"]],
  sections:[
   {id:"s1",h:"1. Revelation and its transmission",html:`<p>God reveals himself "in deeds and words having an inner unity" (Dei Verbum 2), and fully in Christ. That revelation is handed on in two ways that form one whole:</p>
<div class="quote">"Sacred tradition and Sacred Scripture form one sacred deposit of the word of God, committed to the Church."<span class="src">Dei Verbum 10</span></div>
<p>The Magisterium "is not above the word of God, but serves it, teaching only what has been handed on" (Dei Verbum 10). This is the hinge between F1 and the rest of the theology years.</p>`,
    check:Q("According to Dei Verbum 10, Scripture and Tradition…",[["are two separate sources that can conflict","No."],["form one sacred deposit of the word of God","Correct."],["are both subordinate to theologians","No."]],1)},
   {id:"s2",h:"2. The object of faith",html:`<p>Every knowing habit has a <strong>material object</strong> (what is known) and a <strong>formal object</strong> (that by which it is known). In geometry, the conclusions are known through the demonstration.</p>
<div class="quote">"For the faith of which we are speaking, does not assent to anything, except because it is revealed by God. Hence the mean on which faith is based is the Divine Truth."<span class="src">ST II-II q.1 a.1</span></div>
<p>So the formal object of faith is the First Truth revealing. Miracles and the reasonableness of the faith are motives of credibility; they make belief reasonable, but we believe <em>because God has spoken</em>.</p>`,
    check:Q("The formal object of faith is…",[["the miracles of Christ","Motives of credibility, not the formal object."],["the First Truth revealing","Correct."],["the Church's tradition as such","It hands on what God reveals."]],1)},
   {id:"s3",h:"3. The act of faith",html:`<p>To believe is "to think with assent" (II-II q.2 a.1): the intellect assents, but not because it sees the truth evidently; the will moves it, under grace, because of God's authority.</p>
<p>Thomas, following Augustine, distinguishes three aspects of one act (q.2 a.2):</p>
<ol><li><strong>Believing in a God</strong> (credere Deum): the material object, what is believed.</li><li><strong>Believing God</strong> (credere Deo): the formal object, believing on his word.</li><li><strong>Believing in God</strong> (credere in Deum): the will tending to God as end.</li></ol>
<div class="quote">"Faith is a habit of the mind, whereby eternal life is begun in us, making the intellect assent to what is non-apparent."<span class="src">ST II-II q.4 a.1</span></div>`,
    check:Q("\"Believing God\" (credere Deo) concerns…",[["what is believed","That is credere Deum."],["believing on God's authority","Correct: the formal object."],["the will tending to God","That is credere in Deum."]],1)},
   {id:"s4",h:"4. How doctrine develops",html:`<p>Thomas asks whether the articles of faith have increased over time (II-II q.1 a.7):</p>
<div class="quote">"As regards the substance of the articles of faith, they have not received any increase as time went on: since whatever those who lived later have believed, was contained, albeit implicitly, in the faith of those Fathers who preceded them."<span class="src">ST II-II q.1 a.7</span></div>
<p>What grew was the number of articles believed explicitly. Dei Verbum 8 says the same: "there is a growth in the understanding of the realities and the words which have been handed down."</p>
<p><span class="voice">Later thought</span>Newman's <em>Essay on Development</em> (1845) gives seven notes of a true development: preservation of type, continuity of principles, power of assimilation, logical sequence, anticipation of its future, conservative action on its past, and chronic vigour.</p>`,
    check:Q("According to II-II q.1 a.7, the articles of faith increased…",[["in substance","Thomas denies this."],["in explicit statement","Correct."],["not at all, in any sense","Too strong."]],1)},
   {id:"s5",h:"5. Worked example: is faith reasonable?",html:`<div class="anatomy">
 <div class="part"><div class="label">Objection</div><div>Assent without evidence is credulity, unworthy of a rational being.</div></div>
 <div class="part"><div class="label">Distinction</div><div>Assent without <em>intrinsic</em> evidence (seeing the truth itself) differs from assent without <em>any</em> reason. Faith has a reason: the authority of God, who neither deceives nor is deceived.</div></div>
 <div class="part"><div class="label">Credibility</div><div>Signs such as miracles, prophecy and the Church's holiness show that belief is reasonable (compare John 5:31–40 in the Reader).</div></div>
 <div class="part"><div class="label">Conclusion</div><div>Faith is above reason but not against it (F1, F6). As Vatican I puts it, God gives external proofs of revelation together with the inner help of the Spirit (Dei Filius ch. 3; CCC 156).</div></div></div>`,
    check:Q("The key distinction in answering \"faith is credulity\" is between…",[["intrinsic evidence and any reason at all","Correct."],["Scripture and Tradition","Not the issue."],["Latin and Greek Fathers","No."]],0)}
  ],
  quiz:[
   Q("Revelation is given \"in deeds and words having an inner unity\" according to…",[["Dei Verbum 2","Correct."],["ST I q.2 a.3","The Five Ways."],["Aeterni Patris","No."],["CCC 1","No."]],0,"Dei Verbum 2",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["revelation"],diff:1}),
   Q("The Magisterium, according to Dei Verbum 10…",[["is above the word of God","Explicitly denied."],["serves the word of God","Correct."],["creates new revelation","No."],["replaces Scripture","No."]],1,"Dei Verbum 10",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["magisterium"],diff:1}),
   Q("In geometry, the formal aspect of the science is…",[["the conclusions","Material object."],["the mean of demonstration","Correct: II-II q.1 a.1."],["the textbook","No."],["the teacher","No."]],1,"ST II-II q.1 a.1",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["object-faith"],diff:2}),
   Q("Faith assents to something \"except because it is revealed by…\"",[["the Church","The Church hands it on."],["God","Correct."],["reason","No."],["experience","No."]],1,"ST II-II q.1 a.1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["object-faith"],diff:1}),
   Q("To believe is…",[["to see with evidence","That is science."],["to think with assent","Correct: q.2 a.1."],["to feel strongly","No."],["to guess","No."]],1,"ST II-II q.2 a.1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["faith"],diff:1}),
   Q("\"Credere in Deum\" names…",[["the material object","That is credere Deum."],["the formal object","That is credere Deo."],["the will's movement toward God as end","Correct."],["unbelief","No."]],2,"ST II-II q.2 a.2",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["faith"],diff:2}),
   Q("Thomas defines faith as \"a habit of the mind, whereby eternal life is begun in us, making the intellect assent to…\"",[["what is evident","No."],["what is non-apparent","Correct."],["what is probable","No."],["what the senses show","No."]],1,"ST II-II q.4 a.1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["faith"],diff:1}),
   Q("Have the articles of faith increased in substance over time?",[["Yes","Thomas denies it."],["No: only in explicit statement","Correct."],["Only after Vatican II","No."],["Only in the Old Testament","No."]],1,"ST II-II q.1 a.7",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["article-faith","development"],diff:2}),
   Q("Which is one of Newman's notes of a true development?",[["Preservation of type","Correct."],["Popularity","No."],["Novelty","No."],["Simplicity","No."]],0,"Newman 1845",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["development"],diff:2}),
   Q("Miracles and prophecies, in relation to faith, are…",[["the formal object of faith","No."],["motives of credibility","Correct."],["irrelevant","No."],["the material object","No."]],1,"CCC 156",{bloom:"Analyse",terms:["object-faith"],diff:3})
  ],
  construct:{q:"Whether the articles of faith have increased in course of time?",ref:"ST II-II q.1 a.7",
   model:{obj:"It seems they have not increased: faith is \"the substance of things to be hoped for\" (Hebrews 11:1), and the same things are hoped for at all times; so the same things are to be believed at all times.",sc:"Gregory (Hom. xvi in Ezech.): the knowledge of the holy fathers increased as time went on, and the nearer they were to the Saviour's coming, the more fully they received the mysteries of salvation.",resp:"The articles stand to the doctrine of faith as self-evident principles to a natural science: some are contained implicitly in others, and all in God's existence and his providence for our salvation. In substance they have not increased, since later believers believed nothing not contained implicitly in the faith of the earlier Fathers. But in explicit statement they increased.",rep:"The same things were always hoped for, but they were not always hoped for, or believed, with the same explicitness."},
   rubric:["Objection argues against any increase.","Sed contra cites an authority for increase.","Respondeo compares articles to first principles.","Respondeo distinguishes substance from explicit statement.","Reply applies the distinction."]},
  drill:{title:"Put the act of faith in order",parts:["God reveals himself in deeds and words.","The Church hands on what was revealed in Scripture and Tradition.","The truth is proposed to the believer.","The will, moved by grace, moves the intellect to assent.","The intellect assents on the authority of God revealing."]},
  connections:`<p><strong>Psychology.</strong> Research on epistemic trust studies how we rightly rely on testimony we cannot check ourselves (in developmental work, children's selective trust in informants). Thomas's account of faith is a theology of trust in the most reliable informant. This is an analogy for discussion, not evidence for the faith.</p><p><strong>Other modules.</strong> T7 treats faith as a theological virtue; T2 treats Scripture, the written form of revelation.</p>`},

 t2:{id:"t2",code:"T2 · Lesson 1",title:"Scripture and Thomas's way of reading it",
  outcomes:["State what the Church teaches about the canon, inspiration and inerrancy (Dei Verbum 11; CCC 120).","Explain why Scripture uses metaphors (ST I q.1 a.9).","Explain the four senses and why all rest on the literal (ST I q.1 a.10).","Describe Thomas's method: lectio, divisio textus, use of the Fathers.","Explain the possibility of several literal senses."],
  prereq:["inspiration","literal-sense","spiritual-sense","divisio","lectio"],
  reading:{primary:[["ST I q.1 a.9–10",NA("1001.htm#article9")],["Dei Verbum (1965) §§ 11–13",'https://www.vatican.va/archive/hist_councils/ii_vatican_council/documents/vat-ii_const_19651118_dei-verbum_en.html'],["Super Ioannem, prologue and cap. 1 (Latin)","https://isidore.co/aquinas/SSJohn.htm"]],
   secondary:["Levering, M., Vijgen, J. and Roszak, P. (eds) (2023) Handbook of Biblical Thomism. [verify publisher and year]","Dauphinais, M. and Levering, M. (eds) (2005) Reading John with St. Thomas Aquinas. Washington, DC: Catholic University of America Press.","Pontifical Biblical Commission (1993) The Interpretation of the Bible in the Church. Vatican City: Libreria Editrice Vaticana."]},
  scripture:[["John 1:1–2, Thomas's divisio of the Prologue","j1-1"],["John 2:1–11, literal and allegorical readings of Cana","j2-1"],["John 3:1–6, the literal sense of water and Spirit","j3-1"]],
  sections:[
   {id:"s1",h:"1. Canon, inspiration and inerrancy",html:`<p>The Catholic <strong>canon</strong> has 73 books: 46 in the Old Testament and 27 in the New, defined by the Council of Trent (1546) following earlier councils (CCC 120). This is the canon in the Reader's rail.</p>
<div class="quote">"In composing the sacred books, God chose men and while employed by Him they made use of their powers and abilities, so that with Him acting in them and through them, they, as true authors, consigned to writing everything and only those things which He wanted."<span class="src">Dei Verbum 11</span></div>
<p>So Scripture teaches "solidly, faithfully and without error that truth which God wanted put into sacred writings for the sake of salvation" (Dei Verbum 11).</p>`,
    check:Q("How many books are in the Catholic canon?",[["66","The Protestant count."],["73","Correct."],["81","No."]],1)},
   {id:"s2",h:"2. Why Scripture speaks in images",html:`<div class="quote">"It is befitting Holy Writ to put forward divine and spiritual truths by means of comparisons with material things. For God provides for everything according to the capacity of its nature. Now it is natural to man to attain to intellectual truths through sensible objects, because all our knowledge originates from sense."<span class="src">ST I q.1 a.9</span></div>
<p>This is the anthropology of F5 applied to Scripture: since we understand by turning to images, God teaches through images. Thomas adds that lowly images are sometimes better, because no one mistakes them for the reality.</p>`,
    check:Q("Why, according to ST I q.1 a.9, does Scripture use bodily images?",[["Because God is bodily","No."],["Because our knowledge begins from sense","Correct."],["To hide the truth from everyone","No."]],1)},
   {id:"s3",h:"3. The four senses, all founded on the literal",html:`<div class="quote">"The author of Holy Writ is God, in whose power it is to signify His meaning, not by words only (as man also can do), but also by things themselves."<span class="src">ST I q.1 a.10</span></div>
<p>Words signify things (the literal sense). The things themselves can signify further realities (the spiritual sense): allegorical, moral and anagogical. But "all the senses are founded on one — the literal — from which alone can any argument be drawn" (a.10 ad 1).</p>
<p>Thomas adds a striking point: "Since the literal sense is that which the author intends, and since the author of Holy Writ is God, Who by one act comprehends all things by His intellect, it is not unfitting… if, even according to the literal sense, one word in Holy Writ should have several senses."</p>`,
    check:Q("From which sense alone can a theological argument be drawn?",[["Allegorical","No."],["Literal","Correct: a.10 ad 1."],["Anagogical","No."]],1)},
   {id:"s4",h:"4. Thomas's method in his commentaries",html:`<p>You have watched this method in the Reader. Each lecture:</p>
<ol><li><strong>Divides the text</strong> (divisio textus) to show the passage's order: "First… secondly…". Each Reader unit opens with it.</li><li><strong>Expounds the letter</strong>, settling literal questions: harmonising the Gospels (John 2:12 with Matthew 4:12), explaining names and places.</li><li><strong>Weighs the Fathers</strong>, often giving two readings (Chrysostom and Augustine on \"the Spirit breatheth where he will\", John 3:8), sometimes judging one better.</li><li><strong>Answers heresies</strong> (Arius, Sabellius, Nestorius) from the text.</li><li><strong>Adds the spiritual senses</strong> where the things signified point beyond themselves.</li></ol>
<p>Thomas called himself a <em>magister in sacra pagina</em>, master of the sacred page. His inaugural lecture <em>Rigans montes</em> (1256) takes Psalm 103:13 (Vulgate): God waters the mountains (teachers) from his upper rooms, so that the earth (hearers) is filled.</p>`,
    check:Q("What does a divisio textus do?",[["Translates the text","No."],["Shows the order of the passage by dividing it into parts","Correct."],["Lists the Fathers","No."]],1)},
   {id:"s5",h:"5. Reading with the Church today",html:`<p><span class="voice">Magisterium</span>Dei Verbum 12 tells the interpreter to "investigate what meaning the sacred writers really intended, and what God wanted to manifest by means of their words", with attention to "literary forms". It also requires reading Scripture as a whole, within Tradition and the analogy of faith.</p>
<p>Thomas's literal sense and modern historical study are allies: both ask what the author meant. The Church adds what historical method alone cannot: the unity of Scripture, read in the Spirit in which it was written (CCC 111–114).</p>
<div class="box warn"><div class="label">Common misunderstanding</div><p>The spiritual sense is not a licence for any association a reader likes. For Thomas it rests on what the things themselves signify within God's plan, and it never overrides the literal.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("Dei Verbum 12 tells interpreters to attend to…",[["only the spiritual sense","No."],["the intention of the sacred writers and literary forms","Correct."],["private feelings","No."]],1)}
  ],
  quiz:[
   Q("The canon of Scripture was defined by…",[["Trent (1546), following earlier councils","Correct."],["Vatican II","No."],["Thomas Aquinas","No."],["Nicaea I","No."]],0,"CCC 120",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["canon"],diff:1}),
   Q("Dei Verbum 11 says the human writers were…",[["mere secretaries","No."],["true authors, with God acting in and through them","Correct."],["independent of God","No."],["unaware of what they wrote","No."]],1,"Dei Verbum 11",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["inspiration"],diff:1}),
   Q("Scripture teaches without error…",[["everything about science","No."],["the truth God wanted put into Scripture for the sake of salvation","Correct."],["only the New Testament","No."],["nothing","No."]],1,"Dei Verbum 11",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["inerrancy"],diff:2}),
   Q("Scripture uses metaphors because…",[["our knowledge originates from sense","Correct: I q.1 a.9."],["God is material","No."],["the authors were poets","Not Thomas's reason."],["truth is relative","No."]],0,"ST I q.1 a.9",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["inspiration"],diff:1}),
   Q("God can signify not only by words but by…",[["things themselves","Correct: a.10."],["dreams only","No."],["numbers only","No."],["silence","No."]],0,"ST I q.1 a.10",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["spiritual-sense"],diff:1}),
   Q("Why can one word have several literal senses, for Thomas?",[["Because the author is God, who comprehends all by one act","Correct."],["Because words are vague","No."],["Because readers decide","No."],["It cannot","He allows it."]],0,"ST I q.1 a.10",{bloom:"Analyse",terms:["literal-sense"],diff:3}),
   Q("In the Commentary on John, Thomas typically…",[["skips the letter for allegory","No."],["divides the text, expounds the letter, weighs the Fathers, adds spiritual senses","Correct."],["ignores the Fathers","No."],["follows only Augustine","He often compares Augustine and Chrysostom."]],1,"T2 s4",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["divisio","lectio"],diff:2}),
   Q("Thomas's inaugural lecture Rigans montes takes as its text…",[["John 1:1","No."],["Psalm 103:13 (Vulgate)","Correct."],["Isaiah 6:1","That is the prologue of Super Ioannem."],["Genesis 1:1","No."]],1,"Principium (1256)",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["principium"],diff:2}),
   Q("Dei Verbum 12 asks the interpreter to attend to…",[["literary forms","Correct."],["only Thomas","No."],["only the Vulgate","No."],["only the spiritual sense","No."]],0,"Dei Verbum 12",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["inerrancy"],diff:1}),
   Q("Reading Cana's six waterpots as the six ages of the world is…",[["the literal sense","No."],["the allegorical sense","Correct."],["a heresy","No."],["the moral sense","No."]],1,"Super Ioan. cap. 2, lect. 1",{bloom:"Apply",terms:["allegorical"],diff:2})
  ],
  construct:{q:"Whether in Holy Scripture a word may have several senses?",ref:"ST I q.1 a.10",
   model:{obj:"It seems not: many senses in one text produce confusion and deception and destroy all force of argument.",sc:"Gregory (Moral. xx, 1): Holy Writ by the manner of its speech transcends every science, because in one and the same sentence, while it describes a fact, it reveals a mystery.",resp:"The author of Holy Writ is God, who can signify not by words only but by things themselves. The signification of words is the literal sense; the signification of things signified by words is the spiritual sense, which rests on the literal. It is threefold: allegorical (Old Law signifying New), moral (what we should do), anagogical (eternal glory). And since the literal sense is what the author intends, and the author is God, even the literal sense of one word may be several.",rep:"No confusion results, for all the senses are founded on one, the literal, from which alone argument can be drawn."},
   rubric:["Objection fears confusion.","Sed contra cites Gregory.","Respondeo grounds the senses in God as author.","Respondeo divides the spiritual sense into three.","Reply: all senses rest on the literal, the only basis for argument."]},
  drill:{title:"Put Thomas's method in order",parts:["Divide the text to show its order (divisio textus).","Expound the letter and settle literal questions.","Compare the Fathers' readings and judge between them.","Answer errors from the text.","Draw out the spiritual senses where the things signify further."]},
  connections:`<p><strong>Method connection.</strong> Your RES 320 work on qualitative interpretation faces the same tension as biblical exegesis: authorial intention, the reader's horizon, and the community of interpreters. Thomas's rule, that every further sense must rest on the literal, plays the role of a coding frame that keeps interpretation accountable.</p><p><strong>Other modules.</strong> T2b introduces the Old and New Testaments book by book; the Reader is this lesson's workshop.</p>`}
});

YEARS[0].mods.forEach(m=>{const k={F7:"f7",F9:"f9"}[m[0]];if(k)m[3]=k;});
YEARS[1].mods.forEach(m=>{const k={T1:"t1",T2:"t2"}[m[0]];if(k)m[3]=k;});
(function(){function h(str){let x=2166136261;for(const c of str){x^=c.charCodeAt(0);x=Math.imul(x,16777619);}return x>>>0;}
 const shuf=q=>{const idx=q.opts.map((_,i)=>i);let seed=h(q.prompt);for(let i=idx.length-1;i>0;i--){seed=Math.imul(seed^(seed>>>15),2246822507)>>>0;const j=seed%(i+1);[idx[i],idx[j]]=[idx[j],idx[i]];}
  q.opts=idx.map(i=>q.opts[i]);q.ans=idx.indexOf(q.ans);};
 ["f7","f9","t1","t2"].forEach(k=>{const L=LESSONS[k];L.quiz.forEach(shuf);L.sections.forEach(s=>shuf(s.check));});})();

// ---------- Integration track: Psychology & Theology (P1–P4) ----------
// Evidence verified on PubMed 25 Oct 2026; Summa quotations checked against New Advent. DSM-5-TR text is paraphrased (APA copyright), never quoted at length.
TERMS.push(
 {id:"integration",t:"Integration (psychology and theology)",la:"—",d:"Integration",b:"Relating what psychology finds about people to what theology teaches about them, without letting either swallow the other.",p:"The disciplined relating of two knowledges of the human person that differ in method and formal object: empirical psychology and sacred doctrine, with philosophy of nature and anthropology as the bridge.",ex:"Reading research on habit formation alongside Thomas on habitus.",where:"ST I q.1 a.6 ad 2; a.8 ad 2",ccc:"159",conf:"Mixing the two, so that theology becomes therapy or therapy becomes religion.",rel:["formal-object-science","sacra-doctrina"],lesson:"p1",alias:["integration"]},
 {id:"formal-object-science",t:"Formal object of a science",la:"ratio formalis obiecti",d:"Logic",b:"The particular angle under which a discipline studies its subject.",p:"The aspect under which a science considers its material object; the same thing (a human person) can be studied by different sciences under different formal objects.",ex:"A person studied as a nervous system (neuroscience), as behaving (psychology), as ordered to God (theology).",where:"ST I q.1 a.1 ad 2",ccc:"",conf:"The topic or subject matter alone (the material object).",rel:["integration","object-faith"],lesson:"p1",alias:["formal object of a science"]},
 {id:"dsm",t:"DSM-5-TR",la:"—",d:"Psychology",b:"The American Psychiatric Association's manual of mental disorders, revised text published in 2022.",p:"Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, Fifth Edition, Text Revision (APA, 2022): a categorical classification with criteria sets, used with ICD codes.",ex:"Diagnosing major depressive disorder from criteria and clinical judgment.",where:"APA (2022)",ccc:"",conf:"ICD-11, the WHO classification used for official coding in many countries.",rel:["mental-disorder","icd"],lesson:"p2",alias:["DSM-5-TR","DSM-5","DSM"]},
 {id:"icd",t:"ICD-11",la:"—",d:"Psychology",b:"The World Health Organization's international classification of diseases, including mental disorders.",p:"International Classification of Diseases, 11th Revision, adopted by the World Health Assembly in 2019 and in effect from 1 January 2022; its chapter 6 covers mental, behavioural and neurodevelopmental disorders.",ex:"South African medical schemes still require ICD-10 codes on claims.",where:"WHO (2019)",ccc:"",conf:"DSM-5-TR, which is the APA's manual.",rel:["dsm"],lesson:"p2",alias:["ICD-11","ICD-10","ICD"]},
 {id:"mental-disorder",t:"Mental disorder (DSM sense)",la:"—",d:"Psychology",b:"A clinically significant disturbance in thinking, emotion regulation or behaviour that reflects a dysfunction in the processes underlying mental functioning, usually with distress or impairment.",p:"Paraphrase of the DSM-5-TR definition: a syndrome of clinically significant disturbance in cognition, emotion regulation or behaviour reflecting dysfunction in psychological, biological or developmental processes; usually with significant distress or disability; excluding expectable or culturally approved responses to loss, and deviance or conflict with society unless they result from dysfunction.",ex:"Grief after a death is not in itself a disorder; prolonged grief disorder may be.",where:"APA (2022), Introduction",ccc:"",conf:"Sin, vice, or simply unusual belief.",rel:["harmful-dysfunction","dsm"],lesson:"p2",alias:["mental disorder","mental disorders","mental illness"]},
 {id:"harmful-dysfunction",t:"Harmful dysfunction",la:"—",d:"Psychology",b:"Wakefield's view that a disorder is a breakdown of a natural function that also causes harm.",p:"A disorder is a harmful dysfunction: \"dysfunction\" is a scientific term (failure of a mechanism to perform its natural function) and \"harmful\" a value term judged by social standards (Wakefield, 1992).",ex:"Sorrow at a loss is a working function; persistent anhedonia without cause may be a dysfunction.",where:"Wakefield (1992)",ccc:"",conf:"Statistical rarity, or mere social disapproval.",rel:["mental-disorder","final-cause"],lesson:"p2",alias:["harmful dysfunction"]},
 {id:"imputability",t:"Imputability",la:"imputabilitas",d:"Moral theology",b:"How far an action can be counted as truly one's own for praise or blame.",p:"The attribution of an act to its agent as voluntary; it \"can be diminished or even nullified by ignorance, inadvertence, duress, fear, habit, inordinate attachments, and other psychological or social factors\" (CCC 1735).",ex:"A panic-driven act is less imputable than a calm, chosen one.",where:"ST I-II q.6; q.77 a.7; CCC 1735",ccc:"1735, 2282",conf:"The objective rightness or wrongness of the act, which imputability does not change.",rel:["passions","will","free-choice"],lesson:"p3",alias:["imputability","culpability","responsibility"]},
 {id:"acedia",t:"Acedia (sloth)",la:"acedia",d:"Moral theology",b:"A sadness about spiritual good that makes a person not want to do anything about God.",p:"\"An oppressive sorrow\" about spiritual good, a capital vice opposed to the joy of charity (ST II-II q.35 a.1); distinct from clinical depression, which is a disorder, not a choice.",ex:"Boredom and aversion toward prayer that one consents to.",where:"ST II-II q.35",ccc:"2094, 2733",conf:"Depression or ordinary tiredness.",rel:["passions","imputability"],lesson:"p3",alias:["acedia","sloth"]},
 {id:"scrupulosity",t:"Scrupulosity",la:"scrupulus",d:"Psychology",b:"Obsessive, distressing doubt about sin and religious duty.",p:"A pattern, often a presentation of obsessive-compulsive disorder, of intrusive moral or religious doubts with compulsive checking, confessing or reassurance-seeking.",ex:"Repeating a confession many times for fear something was left out.",where:"Ignatius, Spiritual Exercises, notes on scruples",ccc:"",conf:"A tender or well-formed conscience.",rel:["synderesis","imputability"],lesson:"p4",alias:["scrupulosity","scruples"]},
 {id:"sorrow",t:"Sorrow",la:"tristitia",d:"Anthropology",b:"The passion felt at a present evil; Thomas calls it the passion most harmful to the body.",p:"A passion of the concupiscible appetite regarding present evil; \"of all the soul's passions, sorrow is most harmful to the body\" (ST I-II q.37 a.4). Its remedies: pleasure, tears, the sympathy of friends, contemplation of truth, sleep and baths (q.38).",ex:"Grief after a death.",where:"ST I-II q.35–39",ccc:"",conf:"Depression, which is a disorder involving more than sorrow.",rel:["passions"],lesson:"p4",alias:["sorrow"]}
);
TERMS.forEach(t=>{TERM_BY_ID[t.id]=t;});

Object.assign(LESSONS,{
 p1:{id:"p1",code:"P1 · Integration",title:"Two sciences of one person: how psychology and theology relate",
  outcomes:["Distinguish the material and formal objects of psychology, philosophy and theology.","Describe five models of relating psychology and Christianity (Johnson, 2010).","State the Thomistic principle for integration: grace perfects nature; truth cannot contradict truth.","Summarise the evidence on religiousness and mental health, with effect sizes and limits.","Explain why correlation evidence cannot settle theological claims."],
  prereq:["formal-object-science","sacra-doctrina","science","soul-form"],
  reading:{primary:[["ST I q.1 a.1 ad 2 (the same thing, different sciences)",NA("1001.htm#article1")],["ST I q.1 a.8 ad 2 (grace perfects nature)",NA("1001.htm#article8")],["Fides et Ratio (1998) §§ 1, 34, 106",'https://www.vatican.va/content/john-paul-ii/en/encyclicals/documents/hf_jp-ii_enc_14091998_fides-et-ratio.html']],
   secondary:["Johnson, E.L. (ed.) (2010) Psychology and Christianity: Five Views. 2nd edn. Downers Grove, IL: IVP Academic.","Vitz, P.C., Nordling, W.J. and Titus, C.S. (eds) (2020) A Catholic Christian Meta-Model of the Person: Integration with Psychology and Mental Health Practice. Sterling, VA: Divine Mercy University Press.","Smith, T.B., McCullough, M.E. and Poll, J. (2003) 'Religiousness and depression', Psychological Bulletin, 129(4), pp. 614–636. doi:10.1037/0033-2909.129.4.614.","Bonelli, R.M. and Koenig, H.G. (2013) 'Mental disorders, religion and spirituality 1990 to 2010', Journal of Religion and Health, 52(2), pp. 657–673. doi:10.1007/s10943-013-9691-4."]},
  scripture:[["John 4:1–26, Christ meeting a wounded person","j4-10"],["John 5:1–9, the healing at Bethesda","j5-1"]],
  sections:[
   {id:"s1",h:"1. One person, several sciences",html:`<p>Thomas answers an objection that philosophy already covers everything, including God: "Sciences are differentiated according to the various means through which knowledge is obtained. For the astronomer and the physicist both may prove the same conclusion: that the earth, for instance, is round" (ST I q.1 a.1 ad 2).</p>
<p>So the same <strong>material object</strong> can be studied under different <strong>formal objects</strong>. A human person can be studied:</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Discipline</th><th>Formal object</th><th>Method</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Neuroscience</td><td>the person as a nervous system</td><td>measurement, imaging, experiment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Psychology</td><td>the person as behaving, thinking, feeling</td><td>observation, experiment, statistics</td></tr>
<tr><td>Philosophical anthropology (F5)</td><td>the person as a rational animal: soul, powers, ends</td><td>reasoning from what all experience shows</td></tr>
<tr><td>Theology</td><td>the person as ordered to God, fallen and redeemed</td><td>reasoning from revelation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>Philosophical anthropology is the hinge. Psychology does not answer "what is a person?"; it presupposes an answer. Thomas's account (F5: soul as form, intellect, will, passions) is the bridge on which the two other sciences can meet.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("Psychology and theology can study the same person without contradiction because…",[["they study different people","No."],["they consider the person under different formal objects","Correct."],["one of them is not a science","Both are sciences in their own way."]],1)},
   {id:"s2",h:"2. Five models of relating psychology and Christianity",html:`<p>Johnson (2010) collects five positions, each defended by a proponent:</p>
<ol><li><strong>Levels of explanation</strong> (Myers): psychology and theology answer different questions at different levels; keep them apart.</li>
<li><strong>Integration</strong> (Jones): bring findings from both into one coherent view.</li>
<li><strong>Christian psychology</strong> (Roberts and Watson): build psychology from the Christian tradition's own account of the soul.</li>
<li><strong>Transformational psychology</strong> (Coe and Hall): the psychologist's own spiritual formation shapes the science.</li>
<li><strong>Biblical counselling</strong> (Powlison): Scripture is sufficient for counselling; secular psychology is treated with suspicion.</li></ol>
<p><span class="voice">Catholic</span>The Catholic Christian Meta-Model of the Person (Vitz, Nordling and Titus, 2020) is a Catholic integration model built on Thomistic anthropology: the person as created, fallen, redeemed, and called to flourishing through virtue, relationships and vocation.</p>
<div class="co stance"><div class="co-h">[Stance] Top-band argument</div><p><strong>Position.</strong> A Thomist integration model is defensible: it keeps psychology's methods intact while placing its findings within a true account of the person.</p><p><strong>Strongest counter-argument.</strong> Levels-of-explanation theorists say any integration risks bending data to doctrine.</p><p><strong>Reply.</strong> Thomas agrees that each science keeps its own method (ST I q.1 a.1 ad 2). Integration happens at the level of interpretation, not data collection, and truth cannot contradict truth (Fides et Ratio 34).</p></div>`,
    check:Q("Which model holds that Scripture is sufficient for counselling and treats secular psychology with suspicion?",[["Levels of explanation","Keeps the two apart, but respects psychology."],["Biblical counselling","Correct."],["Integration","Seeks to combine both."]],1)},
   {id:"s3",h:"3. The principle: grace perfects nature",html:`<div class="quote">"Since therefore grace does not destroy nature but perfects it, natural reason should minister to faith as the natural bent of the will ministers to charity."<span class="src">ST I q.1 a.8 ad 2 · Dominican tr. (1920), public domain</span></div>
<p>Three consequences for clinical work:</p>
<ol><li><strong>Nature matters.</strong> Sleep, medication, therapy and social support are not \"unspiritual\". Thomas himself recommends sleep and baths for sorrow (ST I-II q.38 a.5; lesson P4).</li>
<li><strong>Grace is not a technique.</strong> Prayer and sacraments are not interventions to be measured like a drug; their primary end is union with God.</li>
<li><strong>No double truth.</strong> If a psychological finding seems to contradict doctrine, either the finding is being over-read or the doctrine misunderstood.</li></ol>
<div class="co warn"><div class="co-h">[Warn] Common error</div><p>"If I prayed enough, I would not be depressed." This confuses the order of grace with the order of nature, and can deepen guilt. Saints have suffered mental illness; holiness does not guarantee health.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("Which follows from \"grace perfects nature\" for a Catholic clinician?",[["Medication is unspiritual","No: nature matters."],["Natural remedies and grace are not rivals","Correct."],["Prayer replaces therapy","No."]],1)},
   {id:"s4",h:"4. What the evidence shows on religion and mental health",html:`<div class="co core"><div class="co-h">[Core] The evidence</div>
<ol><li><strong>Depression.</strong> Meta-analysis of 147 studies (N = 98,975): religiousness correlates with fewer depressive symptoms, r = −.096, small. The link is stronger under stress. Extrinsic religiousness and negative religious coping (e.g. blaming God) go with <em>more</em> symptoms (Smith, McCullough and Poll, 2003, doi:10.1037/0033-2909.129.4.614).</li>
<li><strong>Psychiatric outcomes broadly.</strong> Of 43 studies in top journals (1990–2010), 72% found religious involvement associated with less mental disorder; evidence was good for depression, substance abuse and suicide, insufficient for bipolar disorder and schizophrenia (Bonelli and Koenig, 2013, doi:10.1007/s10943-013-9691-4).</li>
<li><strong>Coping.</strong> Meta-analysis of 49 studies (105 effect sizes): positive religious coping relates to better adjustment, negative religious coping to worse (Ano and Vasconcelles, 2005, doi:10.1002/jclp.20049).</li>
<li><strong>Religious therapies.</strong> Religiously accommodated therapies help, but there is limited evidence that they outperform secular therapies; choice may rest on client preference (Hook et al., 2010, doi:10.1002/jclp.20626).</li></ol></div>
<div class="co extra"><div class="co-h">Limitations</div><p>Most studies are correlational and cross-sectional, mainly from the United States, and measure religiousness in many different ways. A small correlation says nothing about the truth of any religion.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("In Smith et al. (2003), the overall correlation between religiousness and depressive symptoms was about…",[["−.50","Far too large."],["−.10","Correct: r = −.096, small."],["+.10","The direction was negative."]],1)},
   {id:"s5",h:"5. Why the data cannot settle theology",html:`<p>A Thomist should neither over-use nor dismiss this evidence.</p>
<ol><li><strong>Different formal objects.</strong> Studies measure correlates of religious behaviour. They do not test whether God exists or whether grace is given.</li>
<li><strong>Ends differ.</strong> Faith aims at God, not at symptom reduction. A practice that brought comfort but no truth would fail on Thomas's terms; a true faith that brought suffering would still be true.</li>
<li><strong>Still useful.</strong> Findings like the harm of negative religious coping are pastorally important: they match Thomas's warning that a distorted image of God (as only a punisher) harms the soul.</li></ol>
<div class="co clinical"><div class="co-h">Clinical practicality (MClinPsych lens)</div><p>In South Africa most clients have a religious frame. Assess it respectfully (as the DSM-5-TR Cultural Formulation Interview does), use it when it helps the client, and never impose your own. The HPCSA's ethical rules require you to work within your competence and avoid imposing values. <span class="chip verify">verify current HPCSA wording</span></p></div>`,
    check:Q("Why can't correlational studies settle theological questions?",[["Because they are always wrong","No."],["Because they measure correlates of behaviour, not the truth of revelation","Correct."],["Because theology forbids research","No."]],1)}
  ],
  quiz:[
   Q("Thomas's astronomer–physicist example shows that…",[["only one science can study the earth","No."],["different sciences can prove the same conclusion by different means","Correct: ST I q.1 a.1 ad 2."],["physics is above astronomy","No."],["science contradicts faith","No."]],1,"ST I q.1 a.1 ad 2",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["formal-object-science"],diff:1}),
   Q("Which discipline is the hinge between psychology and theology in a Thomistic model?",[["Neuroscience","Too narrow."],["Philosophical anthropology","Correct."],["Statistics","A tool."],["Canon law","No."]],1,"P1 s1",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["integration"],diff:2}),
   Q("The \"levels of explanation\" model is associated with…",[["Myers","Correct."],["Powlison","Biblical counselling."],["Roberts","Christian psychology."],["Coe","Transformational."]],0,"Johnson (2010)",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["integration"],diff:2}),
   Q("The Catholic Christian Meta-Model of the Person draws chiefly on…",[["Freud","No."],["Thomistic anthropology","Correct."],["behaviourism alone","No."],["biblical counselling","No."]],1,"Vitz et al. (2020)",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["integration"],diff:1}),
   Q("\"If I prayed enough, I would not be depressed\" is an error because…",[["prayer is useless","No."],["it confuses the order of grace with the order of nature","Correct."],["depression is always a sin","No."],["saints never suffer","False."]],1,"P1 s3",{bloom:"Evaluate",terms:["integration"],diff:2}),
   Q("Smith et al. (2003) found which kind of religiousness associated with MORE depressive symptoms?",[["Intrinsic religiousness","No."],["Extrinsic religiousness and negative religious coping","Correct."],["Church attendance","No."],["Prayer","No."]],1,"doi:10.1037/0033-2909.129.4.614",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:2}),
   Q("Bonelli and Koenig (2013) found insufficient evidence for a religion–mental health link in…",[["depression","Good evidence there."],["bipolar disorder and schizophrenia","Correct."],["suicide","Good evidence there."],["substance abuse","Good evidence there."]],1,"doi:10.1007/s10943-013-9691-4",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:2}),
   Q("Hook et al. (2010) concluded that religious and spiritual therapies…",[["clearly outperform secular therapies","Limited evidence for that."],["are helpful, with limited evidence of superiority","Correct."],["are harmful","No."],["are untested","No."]],1,"doi:10.1002/jclp.20626",{bloom:"Understand",terms:[],diff:2}),
   Q("The main limitation of most religion–mental health studies is that they are…",[["experimental","Mostly they are not."],["correlational and cross-sectional","Correct."],["too large","No."],["theological","No."]],1,"P1 s4",{bloom:"Analyse",terms:[],diff:2}),
   Q("Grace does not destroy nature but…",[["replaces it","No."],["perfects it","Correct: ST I q.1 a.8 ad 2."],["ignores it","No."],["opposes it","No."]],1,"ST I q.1 a.8 ad 2",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["grace"],diff:1})
  ],
  construct:{q:"Whether psychology and sacred doctrine may treat the same human person? (adapted)",ref:"ST I q.1 a.1 ad 2",
   model:{obj:"It seems not: if psychology explains human behaviour fully, theology is superfluous; and if theology explains the soul, psychology is superfluous.",sc:"Thomas: the astronomer and the physicist may prove the same conclusion by different means.",resp:"Sciences differ by the means through which knowledge is obtained, that is, by formal object. Psychology studies the person as behaving and experiencing, through observation and measurement; sacred doctrine studies the person as ordered to God, through revelation. Philosophical anthropology links them. Since grace perfects nature, the findings of each can serve the other, and truth cannot contradict truth.",rep:"Neither explains the person fully, because each considers him under one formal object."},
   rubric:["Objection argues one discipline makes the other superfluous.","Sed contra uses the astronomer–physicist example.","Respondeo distinguishes formal objects.","Respondeo names philosophical anthropology as bridge.","Reply applies \"each under one formal object\"."]},
  drill:{title:"Order the steps of a Thomist integration",parts:["Identify the question: empirical, philosophical or theological.","Use the right method for that question.","Interpret findings within a sound account of the person.","Check against revealed truth: apparent conflicts signal over-reading.","Apply with prudence to this client, respecting freedom."]},
  connections:`<p><strong>Your modules.</strong> RES 320's distinction between levels of analysis and SLK 320's critique of individualism both meet here: the person is studied at many levels, and the community (Church, family) is part of the person's world.</p><p><strong>Other modules.</strong> F5 supplies the anthropology; P2–P4 apply it to diagnosis and disorders.</p>`},

 p2:{id:"p2",code:"P2 · Integration",title:"The DSM-5-TR: what it is, what it claims, and its limits",
  outcomes:["Describe what the DSM-5-TR is and how it relates to ICD-10 and ICD-11 in South African practice.","Paraphrase the DSM definition of mental disorder and its exclusions.","Explain Wakefield's harmful-dysfunction analysis and its Thomistic parallel.","Summarise reliability evidence and dimensional alternatives (HiTOP, RDoC).","Locate religion and spirituality in the DSM (Cultural Formulation; Religious or Spiritual Problem)."],
  prereq:["dsm","icd","mental-disorder","harmful-dysfunction","final-cause"],
  reading:{primary:[["American Psychiatric Association (2022) DSM-5-TR, Introduction and Section III (Cultural Formulation)","https://www.psychiatry.org/psychiatrists/practice/dsm"],["WHO ICD-11, chapter 6","https://icd.who.int/"],["Mental Health Care Act 17 of 2002 (South Africa)","https://www.gov.za/documents/mental-health-care-act"]],
   secondary:["American Psychiatric Association (2022) Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders. 5th edn, text revision. Washington, DC: APA.","Wakefield, J.C. (1992) 'The concept of mental disorder', American Psychologist, 47(3), pp. 373–388. doi:10.1037//0003-066x.47.3.373.","Regier, D.A. et al. (2013) 'DSM-5 field trials… Part II: test-retest reliability', American Journal of Psychiatry, 170(1), pp. 59–70. doi:10.1176/appi.ajp.2012.12070999.","Kotov, R. et al. (2017) 'The Hierarchical Taxonomy of Psychopathology (HiTOP)', Journal of Abnormal Psychology, 126(4), pp. 454–477.","Insel, T. et al. (2010) 'Research Domain Criteria (RDoC)', American Journal of Psychiatry, 167(7), pp. 748–751."]},
  scripture:[["John 5:1–9, illness named and healed","j5-1"]],
  sections:[
   {id:"s1",h:"1. What the DSM-5-TR is",html:`<p>The <strong>DSM-5-TR</strong> (American Psychiatric Association, March 2022) is a text revision of DSM-5 (2013). It is a <em>categorical</em> classification: each disorder has a criteria set, with specifiers and severity ratings. The revision updated text throughout, added <strong>prolonged grief disorder</strong>, and added codes for suicidal behaviour and non-suicidal self-injury.</p>
<p>The WHO's <strong>ICD-11</strong> took effect on 1 January 2022. In South Africa, clinicians diagnose with DSM-5-TR or ICD criteria, but medical schemes require <strong>ICD-10</strong> codes on claims. <span class="chip verify">check the current ICD-11 transition timeline</span></p>
<div class="co apply"><div class="co-h">[Apply] South African application</div><p>A Pretoria psychologist writes a DSM-5-TR diagnosis in the report, codes it in ICD-10 for the medical aid, and works within the Mental Health Care Act 17 of 2002 if involuntary care is involved.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("Which diagnosis was newly added in DSM-5-TR?",[["Prolonged grief disorder","Correct."],["Major depressive disorder","Long established."],["ADHD","Long established."]],0)},
   {id:"s2",h:"2. The DSM definition of mental disorder",html:`<div class="co def"><div class="co-h">Definition · Mental disorder (paraphrase of DSM-5-TR)</div><p>A syndrome of clinically significant disturbance in cognition, emotion regulation or behaviour that reflects a <strong>dysfunction</strong> in the psychological, biological or developmental processes underlying mental functioning, usually with significant <strong>distress or disability</strong>.</p></div>
<p>Two exclusions matter for theology:</p>
<ol><li>An expectable or culturally approved response to a common stressor or loss (such as grief after a death) is not a disorder.</li>
<li>Socially deviant behaviour (political, religious or sexual) and conflicts between the individual and society are not disorders unless they result from a dysfunction in the individual.</li></ol>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>Exclusion 2 protects religious belief from being pathologised merely for being unusual. A Thomist adds the reason: whether a belief is <em>true</em> is not a clinical question. Religious belief becomes clinically relevant only when its form shows dysfunction (for example, delusional fixity or compulsive scrupulosity), not because of its content.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("According to the DSM exclusions, religious deviance from social norms is…",[["always a disorder","No."],["not a disorder unless it results from a dysfunction","Correct."],["a personality disorder","No."]],1)},
   {id:"s3",h:"3. Harmful dysfunction and Thomas's natural ends",html:`<p>Wakefield (1992) argued that a disorder is a <strong>harmful dysfunction</strong>: "dysfunction" is a factual failure of a mechanism to do what it naturally does, and "harmful" is a value judgment.</p>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>"Natural function" is a teleological notion: Thomas's final cause (F3). Fear naturally serves avoidance of real danger; sorrow naturally responds to real loss. A disorder of the passions is, in Thomist terms, a passion detached from its proper object or measure: fear without real danger (panic), sorrow without proportion (melancholia). This gives Wakefield's "dysfunction" a metaphysical ground that evolutionary accounts lack.</p></div>
<div class="co analogy"><div class="co-h">Analogy · The smoke alarm</div><p>A smoke alarm that rings at fire is working. One that rings at toast every morning is dysfunctional, and harmful because you start ignoring it or never cook. Panic disorder is the alarm ringing at toast: the fear system is real and good, but detached from its proper object.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("In Wakefield's analysis, \"harmful\" is…",[["a purely factual term","That is \"dysfunction\"."],["a value term","Correct."],["a statistical term","No."]],1)},
   {id:"s4",h:"4. Reliability and the dimensional challenge",html:`<div class="co core"><div class="co-h">[Core] The evidence</div><p>In the DSM-5 field trials (11 academic centres), test-retest reliability (intraclass kappa) was very good for 5 diagnoses, good for 9, questionable for 6 and unacceptable for 3 (Regier et al., 2013, doi:10.1176/appi.ajp.2012.12070999). Major depressive disorder (κ ≈ .28) and generalised anxiety disorder (κ ≈ .20) were among the weaker results. <span class="chip verify">confirm kappas in the paper's table</span></p></div>
<p>Two alternatives challenge the categorical model:</p>
<ol><li><strong>HiTOP</strong> (Kotov et al., 2017): psychopathology as dimensions arranged hierarchically (e.g. internalising, externalising, thought disorder).</li>
<li><strong>RDoC</strong> (Insel et al., 2010): research framework built on functional domains (negative valence, cognitive systems, arousal) across levels from genes to behaviour.</li></ol>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>Thomas's passions are already dimensional in a sense: they are movements of degree within powers ordered to objects. HiTOP's \"internalising\" spectrum maps loosely onto sorrow and fear; \"externalising\" onto disordered concupiscible and irascible appetite. Treat this as a heuristic parallel, not an equivalence.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("HiTOP models psychopathology as…",[["discrete categories only","That is the DSM approach."],["hierarchical dimensions","Correct."],["moral failings","No."]],1)},
   {id:"s5",h:"5. Religion and spirituality inside the DSM",html:`<ol><li><strong>Cultural Formulation Interview</strong> (DSM-5-TR, Section III): asks about the client's understanding of the problem, cultural identity, sources of support, and religious and spiritual factors.</li>
<li><strong>Religious or Spiritual Problem</strong> (listed among other conditions that may be a focus of clinical attention): distress over loss or questioning of faith, conversion, or questioning spiritual values. It is not a mental disorder.</li>
<li><strong>Culture and delusion</strong>: a belief shared by one's religious community is not a delusion; clinicians must know the community's norms before judging.</li></ol>
<div class="co clinical"><div class="co-h">Clinical practicality (MClinPsych lens)</div><p>A Zion Christian Church member who reports dreams of ancestors, a Catholic who speaks of guilt after confession, and a Muslim client fasting in Ramadan all need assessment within their tradition's norms. The Cultural Formulation Interview gives a structure; your knowledge of the traditions (see the Apologetics track) gives content.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("\"Religious or Spiritual Problem\" in the DSM-5-TR is…",[["a mental disorder","No."],["a condition that may be a focus of clinical attention, not a disorder","Correct."],["a personality trait","No."]],1)}
  ],
  quiz:[
   Q("DSM-5-TR was published in…",[["2013","That was DSM-5."],["2022","Correct."],["1994","DSM-IV."],["2019","ICD-11 adoption year."]],1,"APA (2022)",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["dsm"],diff:1}),
   Q("South African medical schemes require diagnoses coded in…",[["DSM-5-TR codes only","No."],["ICD-10","Correct (verify transition status)."],["RDoC","A research framework."],["HiTOP","A research model."]],1,"P2 s1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["icd"],diff:1}),
   Q("The DSM definition requires that the disturbance reflect…",[["a sin","No."],["a dysfunction in underlying processes","Correct."],["social deviance","Explicitly excluded."],["a religious belief","No."]],1,"DSM-5-TR (paraphrase)",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["mental-disorder"],diff:1}),
   Q("Grief after the death of a loved one is, in itself,…",[["a mental disorder","Excluded as an expectable response."],["not a mental disorder","Correct."],["a personality disorder","No."],["always prolonged grief disorder","No."]],1,"DSM-5-TR (paraphrase)",{bloom:"Apply",terms:["mental-disorder"],diff:1}),
   Q("Wakefield's \"dysfunction\" corresponds most closely in Thomas to…",[["the efficient cause","No."],["failure of a power to attain its natural end","Correct: final cause."],["sin","No."],["grace","No."]],1,"Wakefield (1992); F3",{bloom:"Analyse",terms:["harmful-dysfunction","final-cause"],diff:3}),
   Q("In the DSM-5 field trials, how many diagnoses fell in the unacceptable reliability range?",[["None","No."],["3","Correct."],["9","That was the good range."],["15","No."]],1,"Regier et al. (2013)",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["dsm"],diff:2}),
   Q("RDoC was proposed by…",[["the WHO","No."],["the US National Institute of Mental Health (Insel et al., 2010)","Correct."],["the APA as DSM-6","No."],["the HPCSA","No."]],1,"Insel et al. (2010)",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:2}),
   Q("A belief shared by the client's religious community is…",[["by definition a delusion","No."],["not a delusion on that ground alone","Correct."],["a sign of psychosis","No."],["irrelevant to assessment","It is relevant context."]],1,"DSM-5-TR glossary (paraphrase)",{bloom:"Apply",terms:["mental-disorder"],diff:2}),
   Q("Which DSM tool structures assessment of cultural and religious factors?",[["The Cultural Formulation Interview","Correct."],["The PCL-R","A psychopathy checklist."],["The MMPI","A personality inventory."],["The GAF","Removed in DSM-5."]],0,"DSM-5-TR Section III",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["dsm"],diff:1}),
   Q("Why does the DSM's exclusion of religious deviance fit a Thomist view?",[["Because religion is irrelevant","No."],["Because the truth of a belief is not a clinical question; only dysfunction is","Correct."],["Because Thomas rejected psychology","Anachronistic."],["Because all beliefs are equal","Not Thomas's reason."]],1,"P2 s2",{bloom:"Evaluate",terms:["mental-disorder"],diff:3})
  ],
  construct:{q:"Whether every intense sorrow is a disorder? (adapted)",ref:"ST I-II q.37–38; DSM-5-TR definition",
   model:{obj:"It seems so: intense sorrow causes distress and impairment, which are the marks of disorder.",sc:"Christ wept at the tomb of Lazarus (John 11:35), and Thomas says tears naturally assuage sorrow (ST I-II q.38 a.2).",resp:"Sorrow is a passion ordered to a real evil; when proportionate to its object it is a working function, not a dysfunction. The DSM itself excludes expectable responses to loss. A disorder is present when sorrow is detached from its object or measure, persists beyond what the loss explains, and impairs life: a harmful dysfunction.",rep:"Distress alone does not make a disorder; dysfunction must also be present."},
   rubric:["Objection equates distress with disorder.","Sed contra uses Christ's tears or Thomas on tears.","Respondeo treats sorrow as a passion with a proper object.","Respondeo cites the DSM exclusion for expectable loss.","Reply: distress is necessary but not sufficient."]},
  drill:{title:"Order a culturally sensitive assessment",parts:["Build rapport and hear the client's own account.","Use the Cultural Formulation Interview, including religion and spirituality.","Assess symptoms against criteria and look for dysfunction, not mere difference.","Formulate: biological, psychological, social, spiritual factors.","Agree a plan the client understands and consents to."]},
  connections:`<p><strong>Your modules.</strong> KRM 320 assessments, forensic reports and future internship case write-ups all use DSM-5-TR language; this lesson gives you a way to use it without adopting a reductive view of the person.</p><p><strong>Other modules.</strong> P3 asks what a disorder is in Thomistic terms; P4 works through specific disorders.</p>`},

 p3:{id:"p3",code:"P3 · Integration",title:"Mental disorder in Thomistic anthropology: soul, powers and responsibility",
  outcomes:["Explain why mental disorders affect the bodily powers that intellect and will depend on, not the spiritual soul itself.","Use ST I-II q.10 a.3 and q.77 a.7 to explain how passion can reduce or remove responsibility.","Apply CCC 1735 and 2282 on imputability.","Distinguish illness, vice and sin in a case.","Distinguish acedia from depression."],
  prereq:["soul-form","phantasm","passions","imputability","acedia"],
  reading:{primary:[["ST I q.84 a.7 (lesion and understanding)",NA("1084.htm#article7")],["ST I-II q.10 a.3 (passion and the will)",NA("2010.htm#article3")],["ST I-II q.77 a.7 (whether passion excuses from sin)",NA("2077.htm#article7")],["ST II-II q.35 a.1 (acedia)",NA("3035.htm#article1")],["CCC 1735, 2282–2283",'https://www.vatican.va/archive/ENG0015/_INDEX.HTM']],
   secondary:["Nault, J.-C. (2015) The Noonday Devil: Acedia, the Unnamed Evil of Our Times. San Francisco: Ignatius Press.","Vitz, P.C., Nordling, W.J. and Titus, C.S. (eds) (2020) A Catholic Christian Meta-Model of the Person. Sterling, VA: Divine Mercy University Press.","Cuijpers, P. et al. (2020) 'A network meta-analysis of the effects of psychotherapies, pharmacotherapies and their combination in the treatment of adult depression', World Psychiatry, 19(1), pp. 92–107. doi:10.1002/wps.20701."]},
  scripture:[["John 5:14, \"sin no more\" said after the healing, not before it","j5-10"]],
  sections:[
   {id:"s1",h:"1. Where mental illness \"lives\" in the person",html:`<p>For Thomas the intellect and will are spiritual powers, but in this life they depend on bodily powers: imagination, memory, the cogitative power and the passions (F5).</p>
<div class="quote">"When the act of the imagination is hindered by a lesion of the corporeal organ, for instance in a case of frenzy; or when the act of the memory is hindered, as in the case of lethargy, we see that a man is hindered from actually understanding things of which he had a previous knowledge."<span class="src">ST I q.84 a.7 · Dominican tr. (1920), public domain</span></div>
<div class="co core"><div class="co-h">[Core] The Thomist account</div><ol><li>Mental disorders affect the bodily and sensitive powers (perception, imagination, memory, the passions) and so impede the exercise of intellect and will.</li><li>They do not corrupt the spiritual soul itself, which remains the form of the body and the image of God.</li><li>So a person with severe mental illness keeps full dignity and the capacity for grace, even when the use of reason is impaired.</li></ol></div>`,
    check:Q("According to Thomas, a brain lesion can…",[["destroy the spiritual soul","No."],["hinder the actual use of intellect by impairing the bodily powers it needs","Correct."],["have no effect on thinking","No: q.84 a.7."]],1)},
   {id:"s2",h:"2. Passion, reason and the will",html:`<p>Thomas distinguishes degrees of the passions' influence on the will (ST I-II q.10 a.3):</p>
<div class="quote">"First, so that his reason is wholly bound, so that he has not the use of reason: as happens in those who through a violent access of anger or concupiscence become furious or insane, just as they may from some other bodily disorder."<span class="src">ST I-II q.10 a.3</span></div>
<ol><li><strong>Reason wholly bound</strong>: the person acts like one without reason; the act is not a human act in the moral sense.</li>
<li><strong>Reason partly free</strong>: \"the movement of the will remains in a certain degree\"; responsibility is reduced but not removed.</li></ol>
<p>And on sin: "if the passion be such that it renders the subsequent act wholly involuntary, it entirely excuses from sin; otherwise, it does not excuse entirely" (ST I-II q.77 a.7). If someone wilfully caused the state (the drunkard), the acts are voluntary \"in their cause\".</p>`,
    check:Q("When passion wholly binds reason, the resulting act…",[["is fully culpable","No."],["is excused from sin, unless the state was voluntary in its cause","Correct: q.77 a.7."],["is always mortal sin","No."]],1)},
   {id:"s3",h:"3. The Catechism on imputability",html:`<div class="quote">"Imputability and responsibility for an action can be diminished or even nullified by ignorance, inadvertence, duress, fear, habit, inordinate attachments, and other psychological or social factors."<span class="src">CCC 1735</span></div>
<p>On suicide: "Grave psychological disturbances, anguish, or grave fear of hardship, suffering, or torture can diminish the responsibility of the one committing suicide" (CCC 2282), and "we should not despair of the eternal salvation of persons who have taken their own lives" (CCC 2283).</p>
<div class="co clinical"><div class="co-h">Clinical practicality (MClinPsych lens)</div><p>Bereaved Catholic families often fear for a loved one who died by suicide. CCC 2282–2283 gives you accurate, compassionate teaching to share, if the family wants it. If you or a client is struggling, the South African Depression and Anxiety Group suicide crisis line is 0800 567 567.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("CCC 1735 lists which of these as able to diminish imputability?",[["Psychological factors","Correct."],["Wealth","No."],["Education level only","Not listed so."]],0)},
   {id:"s4",h:"4. Illness, vice and sin: telling them apart",html:`<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Illness (disorder)</th><th>Vice</th><th>Sin</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>What it is</td><td>a dysfunction in powers the person did not choose</td><td>a bad habit acquired by repeated choices</td><td>a voluntary act against right reason and God's law</td></tr>
<tr><td>Voluntariness</td><td>not chosen; may reduce freedom</td><td>chosen in its formation; may now feel compulsive</td><td>voluntary, with knowledge and consent</td></tr>
<tr><td>Remedy</td><td>treatment, support, time</td><td>contrary acts building virtue, with grace</td><td>repentance and forgiveness</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Real cases mix them. Addiction may begin in chosen acts (vice), become a disorder (impaired control), and involve sins of diminished imputability. The confessor and the clinician each attend to their own order.</p>
<div class="co warn"><div class="co-h">[Warn] Common error</div><p>Treating every symptom as sin (moralising) or every sin as symptom (excusing). Both fail the person. Christ heals the man at Bethesda first and says \"sin no more\" afterwards (John 5:14): healing and moral call belong together but are not the same.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("A bad habit acquired by repeated choices is…",[["an illness","Not by definition."],["a vice","Correct."],["a passion","No."]],1)},
   {id:"s5",h:"5. Acedia is not depression",html:`<div class="quote">"Sloth, according to Damascene (De Fide Orth. ii, 14) is an oppressive sorrow, which, to wit, so weighs upon man's mind, that he wants to do nothing."<span class="src">ST II-II q.35 a.1</span></div>
<p>The description sounds like depression, but the object differs. Acedia is sorrow <em>about spiritual good</em>, consented to; it is a capital vice opposed to the joy of charity. Major depressive disorder is a syndrome affecting mood, interest, sleep, appetite, energy and cognition, often regardless of what the person values.</p>
<div class="co confused"><div class="co-h">If this confused you</div><p>Ask two questions. <strong>What is the sorrow about?</strong> Acedia: specifically God and spiritual things, while other interests may remain. Depression: loss of interest across life. <strong>Is it consented to?</strong> Acedia involves the will; depression happens to a person. They can coexist, and a depressed person may experience prayer as dry without any sin.</p></div>
<div class="co core"><div class="co-h">[Core] Treatment evidence</div><p>For adult depression, combined psychotherapy and medication outperformed either alone in a network meta-analysis of 101 trials (N = 11,910; response RR ≈ 1.25–1.27), while psychotherapy and medication alone did not differ (Cuijpers et al., 2020, doi:10.1002/wps.20701).</p></div>`,
    check:Q("The key difference between acedia and depression is…",[["acedia is always more severe","No."],["acedia is consented sorrow about spiritual good; depression is a disorder","Correct."],["they are the same","No."]],1)}
  ],
  quiz:[
   Q("Mental disorders, on a Thomist view, primarily affect…",[["the spiritual soul's substance","No."],["the bodily and sensitive powers that intellect and will rely on","Correct."],["God's image in man","It remains."],["nothing real","No."]],1,"ST I q.84 a.7",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["soul-form","phantasm"],diff:2}),
   Q("Thomas compares a person whose reason is wholly bound by passion to…",[["a saint","No."],["irrational animals, acting from the impulse of passion","Correct: q.10 a.3."],["an angel","No."],["a judge","No."]],1,"ST I-II q.10 a.3",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["passions"],diff:2}),
   Q("A drunkard's acts while drunk are, for Thomas,…",[["wholly excused","Not if he chose to get drunk."],["voluntary in their cause","Correct: q.77 a.7."],["not acts at all","No."],["acts of the passions only","No."]],1,"ST I-II q.77 a.7",{bloom:"Apply",terms:["imputability"],diff:2}),
   Q("CCC 1735 says imputability can be…",[["only increased","No."],["diminished or even nullified","Correct."],["never affected by psychology","No."],["decided by doctors","No."]],1,"CCC 1735",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["imputability"],diff:1}),
   Q("CCC 2283 teaches that regarding those who died by suicide we should…",[["despair of their salvation","Opposite."],["not despair of their eternal salvation","Correct."],["refuse prayer","No."],["assume they were insane","No."]],1,"CCC 2283",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["imputability"],diff:1}),
   Q("Acedia is defined by Thomas (after Damascene) as…",[["an oppressive sorrow about spiritual good","Correct."],["physical laziness","Too narrow."],["anger","No."],["a disorder of the brain","No."]],0,"ST II-II q.35 a.1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["acedia"],diff:1}),
   Q("A client has lost interest in all activities, sleeps poorly and cannot concentrate, and prayer feels dry. Best first reading?",[["Acedia: rebuke for spiritual sloth","Moralising."],["Possible depression: assess, and do not assume sin","Correct."],["Demonic possession","No basis."],["Laziness","Moralising."]],1,"P3 s5",{bloom:"Apply",terms:["acedia"],diff:2}),
   Q("A vice differs from an illness in that a vice…",[["is never voluntary","No."],["is acquired through repeated choices","Correct."],["cannot be changed","No."],["is a passion","No."]],1,"P3 s4",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["virtue"],diff:2}),
   Q("In Cuijpers et al. (2020), which was more effective for adult depression?",[["Psychotherapy alone","No difference from medication alone."],["Combined psychotherapy and medication","Correct."],["Medication alone","No difference from psychotherapy alone."],["No treatment","No."]],1,"doi:10.1002/wps.20701",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:2}),
   Q("Christ at Bethesda says \"sin no more\"…",[["before healing","No."],["after healing","Correct: John 5:14."],["never","No."],["to the crowd","To the healed man."]],1,"John 5:14",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:1})
  ],
  construct:{q:"Whether passion excuses from sin altogether?",ref:"ST I-II q.77 a.7",
   model:{obj:"It seems so: whatever causes an act to be involuntary excuses from sin, and passion, by binding reason, makes acts involuntary.",sc:"The Apostle calls the passions \"passions of sins\" (Romans 7:5), so they do not wholly excuse.",resp:"An evil act is excused altogether only if it is rendered altogether involuntary. If passion binds reason wholly, it excuses entirely, unless the passion itself was voluntary, as with one who wilfully gets drunk: then the act is voluntary in its cause. If reason is not wholly bound, the passion diminishes but does not wholly remove sin.",rep:"Passion causes involuntariness only when it takes away the use of reason; otherwise the act remains partly voluntary."},
   rubric:["Objection: passion makes acts involuntary.","Sed contra cites Scripture.","Respondeo: excuse requires total involuntariness.","Respondeo: voluntary in its cause (drunkenness).","Reply: partial binding gives partial excuse."]},
  drill:{title:"Order a pastoral-clinical reading of a case",parts:["Describe the behaviour without judging it.","Assess for disorder: dysfunction, distress, impairment.","Assess freedom: knowledge, consent, passion, habit.","Distinguish what needs treatment from what needs repentance.","Refer: clinician for illness, confessor for sin, both where both apply."]},
  connections:`<p><strong>Your Honours project.</strong> This lesson is the theological spine of a psychopathy-and-responsibility argument: ST I-II q.10 a.3 and q.77 a.7 give degrees of responsibility; P4 adds Thomas's claim that the first principles of natural law cannot be blotted out (I-II q.94 a.6).</p><p><strong>Other modules.</strong> T6 develops human acts and the voluntary in full.</p>`},

 p4:{id:"p4",code:"P4 · Integration",title:"Specific disorders read through Thomas: sorrow, fear, scruples, attention, trauma, psychopathy",
  outcomes:["Use ST I-II q.38 (remedies of sorrow) alongside evidence-based treatment of depression.","Relate anxiety disorders to the passion of fear (I-II q.41–44).","Distinguish scrupulosity (OCD) from a well-formed conscience, and name first-line treatment.","Map ADHD onto the integral parts of prudence without moralising.","Apply I-II q.94 a.6 to the question of psychopathy and moral responsibility."],
  prereq:["sorrow","passions","scrupulosity","synderesis","imputability"],
  reading:{primary:[["ST I-II q.37 a.4; q.38 a.1–5 (sorrow and its remedies)",NA("2038.htm")],["ST I-II q.41–44 (fear)",NA("2041.htm")],["ST II-II q.49 (parts of prudence)",NA("3049.htm")],["ST I-II q.94 a.6 (can natural law be blotted out?)",NA("2094.htm#article6")]],
   secondary:["NICE (2005) Obsessive-compulsive disorder and body dysmorphic disorder: treatment (CG31). London: NICE.","NICE (2018) Post-traumatic stress disorder (NG116). London: NICE.","NICE (2018) Attention deficit hyperactivity disorder: diagnosis and management (NG87). London: NICE.","World Health Organization (2013) Guidelines for the Management of Conditions Specifically Related to Stress. Geneva: WHO.","Hare, R.D. (2003) The Hare Psychopathy Checklist–Revised. 2nd edn. Toronto: Multi-Health Systems."]},
  scripture:[["John 6:16–21, \"It is I, fear not\"","j6-14"],["John 5:1–9, thirty-eight years of illness","j5-1"]],
  sections:[
   {id:"s1",h:"1. Depression and Thomas's remedies for sorrow",html:`<div class="quote">"Of all the soul's passions, sorrow is most harmful to the body."<span class="src">ST I-II q.37 a.4 · Dominican tr. (1920), public domain</span></div>
<p>Thomas then gives five remedies (q.38), each with a modern echo:</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Thomas's remedy</th><th>Modern parallel</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>a.1</td><td>Any pleasure assuages sorrow</td><td>Behavioural activation: scheduling rewarding activity</td></tr>
<tr><td>a.2</td><td>Tears and groans</td><td>Emotional expression rather than suppression</td></tr>
<tr><td>a.3</td><td>The sympathy of friends</td><td>Social support; the therapeutic relationship</td></tr>
<tr><td>a.4</td><td>Contemplation of truth</td><td>Meaning-making; cognitive work on beliefs</td></tr>
<tr><td>a.5</td><td>Sleep and baths</td><td>Sleep hygiene; bodily care; exercise</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="co extra"><div class="co-h">Limits of the parallel</div><p>Thomas is describing the passion of sorrow, not major depressive disorder. His remedies fit well with first-line psychological care, but severe depression may also need medication; combined treatment was most effective in Cuijpers et al. (2020).</p></div>`,
    check:Q("Which of Thomas's remedies corresponds most closely to behavioural activation?",[["Sleep and baths","Bodily care."],["Any pleasure assuages sorrow","Correct: a.1."],["Tears","Expression."]],1)},
   {id:"s2",h:"2. Anxiety and the passion of fear",html:`<p>Fear is an irascible passion about a future evil that is hard to resist (I-II q.41–42). Its natural function is to prepare us to avoid real danger.</p>
<ol><li><strong>Generalised anxiety</strong>: fear spread over many possible evils, with worry as an attempt to control them.</li>
<li><strong>Panic</strong>: fear without a proportionate object (F5, P2: the alarm ringing at toast).</li>
<li><strong>Phobia</strong>: fear fixed on an object beyond its real danger.</li></ol>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>Exposure therapy works in a way Thomas would recognise: reason governs the passions \"politically\" (ST I q.81 a.3 ad 2). The passion is not commanded away; it is retrained by repeated experience until it fits its real object. Fortitude, the virtue that moderates fear (II-II q.123), grows the same way.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("Fear, for Thomas, belongs to which power?",[["Concupiscible","No."],["Irascible","Correct: it regards an evil hard to avoid."],["Intellect","No."]],1)},
   {id:"s3",h:"3. Scrupulosity: when conscience becomes a compulsion",html:`<p>Scrupulosity is often a presentation of obsessive-compulsive disorder: intrusive doubts about sin or sacrilege, with compulsions such as repeated confession, checking prayers, or seeking reassurance.</p>
<div class="co case"><div class="co-h">Case from the tradition</div><p>St Ignatius of Loyola suffered severe scruples after his conversion and wrote rules on scruples in the <em>Spiritual Exercises</em>. St Alphonsus Liguori, patron of moral theologians, was also tormented by them. Confessors have long treated scruples by obedience to a director and refusing to re-confess, which looks much like response prevention.</p></div>
<div class="co core"><div class="co-h">[Core] Treatment</div><p>First-line psychological treatment for OCD is CBT including exposure and response prevention (ERP), with SSRIs as an option (NICE CG31).</p></div>
<div class="co warn"><div class="co-h">[Warn] Common error</div><p>Treating scrupulosity as holiness or as a well-formed conscience. A well-formed conscience judges calmly by objective norms; scrupulosity doubts endlessly regardless of evidence. It needs both pastoral direction and clinical treatment.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("First-line psychological treatment for OCD, including scrupulosity, is…",[["repeated confession","That feeds the compulsion."],["CBT with exposure and response prevention","Correct: NICE CG31."],["psychoanalysis","Not first-line."]],1)},
   {id:"s4",h:"4. ADHD and the parts of prudence; trauma and memory",html:`<p>Thomas lists eight integral parts of prudence (II-II q.49): memory, understanding, docility, shrewdness, reason, foresight, circumspection and caution. Several (memory, foresight, circumspection, caution) overlap with what psychology calls executive functions.</p>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>ADHD can impair the instruments of prudence (working memory, planning, inhibition) without any defect of will or virtue. The person may want the good and struggle to execute it. Moral formation should support the instruments (external structure, routines, medication where indicated: NICE NG87) rather than blame the person.</p></div>
<p><strong>Trauma.</strong> PTSD involves intrusive memory, avoidance, negative mood and hyperarousal. Trauma-focused CBT and EMDR are recommended (WHO, 2013; NICE NG116). In Thomas's terms, a memory joined to overwhelming fear and sorrow keeps returning to the imagination; therapy helps reason re-order the memory's meaning, in line with \"contemplation of truth\" (q.38 a.4).</p>`,
    check:Q("Which part of prudence overlaps most with planning ahead?",[["Docility","Teachability."],["Foresight","Correct."],["Memory","Recall of the past."]],1)},
   {id:"s5",h:"5. Psychopathy and moral responsibility",html:`<p>Psychopathy (measured by the PCL-R) involves callousness, lack of remorse, shallow affect and antisocial behaviour. Are psychopaths morally responsible? Thomas gives a principled answer:</p>
<div class="quote">"As to those general principles, the natural law, in the abstract, can nowise be blotted out from men's hearts. But it is blotted out in the case of a particular action, in so far as reason is hindered from applying the general principle to a particular point of practice, on account of concupiscence or some other passion… But as to the other, i.e. the secondary precepts, the natural law can be blotted out from the human heart, either by evil persuasions… or by vicious customs and corrupt habits."<span class="src">ST I-II q.94 a.6</span></div>
<div class="co stance"><div class="co-h">[Stance] Top-band argument</div><p><strong>Position.</strong> Psychopaths retain synderesis (the first principles) and are therefore in principle responsible, though their responsibility for particular acts may be diminished where emotional deficits hinder applying principles to cases.</p><p><strong>Strongest counter-argument.</strong> Some philosophers argue that without moral emotions a person cannot grasp moral reasons as reasons, and so is not fully responsible.</p><p><strong>Reply.</strong> Thomas locates knowledge of first principles in reason, not feeling; affect assists application. So the deficit is in application (diminished imputability), not in knowledge of good and evil as such. This remains contested; it is your Honours question.</p></div>`,
    check:Q("According to ST I-II q.94 a.6, the most general principles of natural law…",[["can be blotted out by bad habits","Only secondary precepts can."],["can nowise be blotted out in the abstract","Correct."],["are unknown to most people","No."]],1)}
  ],
  quiz:[
   Q("Thomas says the passion most harmful to the body is…",[["anger","No."],["sorrow","Correct: q.37 a.4."],["fear","No."],["love","No."]],1,"ST I-II q.37 a.4",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["sorrow"],diff:1}),
   Q("Which is NOT one of Thomas's remedies for sorrow?",[["Tears","Remedy (a.2)."],["Sympathy of friends","Remedy (a.3)."],["Solitude and silence","Correct: not among the five."],["Sleep and baths","Remedy (a.5)."]],2,"ST I-II q.38",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["sorrow"],diff:1}),
   Q("Fear concerns…",[["a present good","No."],["a future evil hard to resist","Correct."],["a past evil","No."],["a present evil simply","That is sorrow."]],1,"ST I-II q.41–42",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["passions"],diff:2}),
   Q("Exposure therapy fits Thomas's view that reason rules the passions…",[["despotically","No."],["politically, by persuasion and retraining","Correct."],["not at all","No."],["only through grace","Grace helps, but this is natural."]],1,"ST I q.81 a.3 ad 2",{bloom:"Analyse",terms:["passions"],diff:2}),
   Q("Which saint wrote rules on scruples in the Spiritual Exercises?",[["Ignatius of Loyola","Correct."],["Thomas Aquinas","No."],["Augustine","No."],["Jerome","No."]],0,"Spiritual Exercises",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["scrupulosity"],diff:1}),
   Q("Repeatedly re-confessing the same sins out of doubt is best treated as…",[["a sign of holiness","No."],["a compulsion to be addressed with direction and ERP","Correct."],["a new sin each time","Moralising."],["irrelevant","No."]],1,"NICE CG31",{bloom:"Apply",terms:["scrupulosity"],diff:2}),
   Q("Executive functions overlap most with which parts of prudence?",[["Docility and shrewdness","Less so."],["Memory, foresight, circumspection, caution","Correct."],["Justice and temperance","Those are other virtues."],["Faith and hope","Theological virtues."]],1,"ST II-II q.49",{bloom:"Analyse",terms:["virtue"],diff:3}),
   Q("Recommended psychological treatments for PTSD include…",[["trauma-focused CBT and EMDR","Correct: WHO 2013; NICE NG116."],["psychoanalysis only","No."],["avoidance","Maintains symptoms."],["exorcism","No."]],0,"WHO (2013); NICE NG116",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:1}),
   Q("According to Thomas, secondary precepts of natural law can be blotted out by…",[["nothing","No."],["evil persuasions and corrupt habits","Correct."],["study","No."],["grace","No."]],1,"ST I-II q.94 a.6",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["natural-law"],diff:2}),
   Q("A Thomist argument on psychopathy locates the deficit chiefly in…",[["knowledge of first moral principles","Thomas says these cannot be blotted out."],["applying principles to particular cases","Correct."],["the spiritual soul's existence","No."],["the body only","No."]],1,"P4 s5",{bloom:"Evaluate",terms:["synderesis","imputability"],diff:3})
  ],
  construct:{q:"Whether the law of nature can be abolished from the heart of man?",ref:"ST I-II q.94 a.6",
   model:{obj:"It seems it can: Romans 2:14 implies some live without the law; and many have lost all moral sense through vice.",sc:"Augustine (Confess. ii): \"Thy law is written in the hearts of men, which iniquity itself effaces not.\"",resp:"The most general precepts cannot be blotted out in the abstract, but can be blotted out in a particular action when reason is hindered by passion. The secondary precepts can be blotted out by evil persuasions or by vicious customs and corrupt habits.",rep:"Those who seem to have lost all moral sense have lost the application or the secondary precepts, not the first principles themselves."},
   rubric:["Objection argues the law can be wholly lost.","Sed contra cites Augustine.","Respondeo distinguishes general from secondary precepts.","Respondeo: passion hinders application in particular acts.","Reply applies the distinction to apparent amorality."]},
  drill:{title:"Order a Thomist reading of a depressed client's week",parts:["Name the sorrow and its object without judgment.","Allow expression: tears and words (q.38 a.2).","Strengthen support: friends, family, the therapeutic relationship (a.3).","Restore the body: sleep, exercise, care (a.5); assess for medication.","Work on meaning and truth (a.4), and schedule rewarding activity (a.1)."]},
  connections:`<p><strong>Your interests.</strong> This lesson touches ADHD, anxiety, depression, bipolar disorder (impaired reason in mania: q.10 a.3), trauma and EMDR, and psychopathy: your stated clinical interests. Each section ends where empirical work must take over.</p><p><strong>Care note.</strong> Some topics here are sensitive. If any touch your own life, the SADAG helpline (0800 567 567) and UP's Student Counselling Unit are good first steps.</p>`}
});

Object.assign(EXTRAS,{
 p1:{plain:{version:"Psychology and theology study the same person from different angles. Thomas's rule is that different sciences can reach the same truth by different means, and that grace perfects nature rather than replacing it.",question:"How can a Catholic be a rigorous psychologist without splitting faith from work?",one:"Keep each science's method, unite them in a true account of the person, and expect no contradiction between truths."},
  hook:"\"M-F-B\": Material object the same, Formal objects different, Bridge is anthropology.",
  analogy:{title:"Three maps of one city",text:"A road map, a geological map and a parish map of Pretoria are all true and all different. None replaces the others; you choose by the question you are asking. Neuroscience, psychology and theology are maps of one person."},
  sort:{title:"Which integration model?",cats:["Levels of explanation","Integration","Christian psychology","Biblical counselling"],items:[["Keep the questions separate",0],["Combine findings into one view",1],["Build psychology from the tradition's account of the soul",2],["Scripture is sufficient for counselling",3]]}},
 p2:{plain:{version:"The DSM-5-TR is a working tool, not a philosophy of the person. It defines disorder by dysfunction plus distress, explicitly excludes grief and religious difference as such, and is challenged by dimensional models.",question:"What exactly is a DSM diagnosis, and what can it not tell us?",one:"A disorder is a harmful dysfunction; the truth of a belief is never a clinical question."},
  hook:"\"D + D – E\": Dysfunction plus Distress, minus the Exclusions (expectable loss, social or religious deviance).",
  analogy:{title:"The smoke alarm",text:"An alarm ringing at a real fire is working. One ringing every time you make toast is dysfunctional and harmful. Panic disorder is the alarm ringing at toast."},
  sort:{title:"Disorder or not (by the DSM definition alone)?",cats:["Possibly a disorder","Not a disorder as such"],items:[["Fear so intense the client cannot leave home",0],["Grief in the weeks after a death",1],["Holding an unusual religious belief shared by one's church",1],["Repeated checking that consumes hours a day",0]]}},
 p3:{plain:{version:"Mental illness affects the bodily powers that thinking and choosing rely on, not the spiritual soul. So it can reduce or even remove responsibility for acts, while leaving dignity and the capacity for grace intact.",question:"When is a person responsible for what they do while unwell?",one:"Illness can bind reason wholly (no fault), partly (reduced fault), or not at all; only the person's will makes sin."},
  hook:"\"I-V-S\": Illness happens, Vice is built, Sin is chosen.",
  analogy:{title:"The pianist and the broken piano",text:"A master pianist on a piano with broken keys plays badly through no lack of skill. Severe illness is the broken instrument: the soul's powers remain, but their bodily instruments fail."},
  sort:{title:"Illness, vice or sin?",cats:["Illness","Vice","Sin"],items:[["Panic attacks that come without warning",0],["A habit of gossip built over years",1],["Choosing to lie to gain an advantage",2],["Persistent low mood with loss of all interest",0]]}},
 p4:{plain:{version:"Thomas's psychology of the passions gives a language for sorrow, fear and conscience that sits well beside modern treatments, as long as we do not confuse a passion with a disorder.",question:"What does Thomas add to how we understand and treat specific conditions?",one:"Name the passion, find its proper object, help reason retrain it, and treat what is illness as illness."},
  hook:"\"Pleasure, Tears, Friends, Truth, Sleep\": Thomas's five remedies for sorrow (I-II q.38).",
  analogy:{title:"Training a horse",text:"A rider does not hit a frightened horse into calm; he lets it approach the feared thing again and again until it settles. Exposure therapy, and Thomas's \"political\" rule of reason over passion, work the same way."},
  sort:{title:"Match the condition to its Thomistic lens",cats:["Sorrow","Fear","Conscience","Prudence"],items:[["Major depression",0],["Panic disorder",1],["Scrupulosity",2],["ADHD",3]]}}
});

YEARS.push({y:"Integration track · Psychology & Theology",mods:[["P1","Two sciences of one person",4,"p1"],["P2","The DSM-5-TR and its limits",4,"p2"],["P3","Mental disorder in Thomistic anthropology",4,"p3"],["P4","Specific disorders read through Thomas",4,"p4"]]});
(function(){function h(str){let x=2166136261;for(const c of str){x^=c.charCodeAt(0);x=Math.imul(x,16777619);}return x>>>0;}
 const shuf=q=>{const idx=q.opts.map((_,i)=>i);let seed=h(q.prompt);for(let i=idx.length-1;i>0;i--){seed=Math.imul(seed^(seed>>>15),2246822507)>>>0;const j=seed%(i+1);[idx[i],idx[j]]=[idx[j],idx[i]];}
  q.opts=idx.map(i=>q.opts[i]);q.ans=idx.indexOf(q.ans);};
 ["p1","p2","p3","p4"].forEach(k=>{const L=LESSONS[k];L.quiz.forEach(shuf);L.sections.forEach(s=>shuf(s.check));});})();

// ---------- Year 1 completion: T4, T5, T9a (T2b and F8 in 17b) ----------
// Summa quotations: Dominican translation (1920, public domain), each checked by script against the New Advent text.
function setKey(code,key){YEARS.forEach(y=>y.mods.forEach(m=>{if(m[0]===code)m[3]=key;}));}
function shuffleLesson(L){function h(str){let x=2166136261;for(const c of str){x^=c.charCodeAt(0);x=Math.imul(x,16777619);}return x>>>0;}
 const shuf=q=>{const idx=q.opts.map((_,i)=>i);let seed=h(q.prompt);for(let i=idx.length-1;i>0;i--){seed=Math.imul(seed^(seed>>>15),2246822507)>>>0;const j=seed%(i+1);[idx[i],idx[j]]=[idx[j],idx[i]];}q.opts=idx.map(i=>q.opts[i]);q.ans=idx.indexOf(q.ans);};
 L.quiz.forEach(shuf);L.sections.forEach(s=>shuf(s.check));}
function addTerms(arr){arr.forEach(t=>{if(!TERM_BY_ID[t.id]){TERMS.push(t);TERM_BY_ID[t.id]=t;}});}

addTerms([
 {id:"relation-divine",t:"Divine relation",la:"relatio divina",d:"Trinity",b:"A real reference of one divine person to another, arising from the processions.",p:"A real relation in God, founded on an immanent procession; really identical with the divine essence, really distinct from its opposite relation (ST I q.28 aa.1–3). There are four: paternity, filiation, active spiration, passive spiration.",ex:"Paternity: the Father's relation to the Son.",where:"ST I q.28",ccc:"252, 255",conf:"A relation of reason, like 'to the left of', which exists only in the mind.",rel:["procession","person"],lesson:"t4",alias:["divine relation","divine relations","subsisting relation"]},
 {id:"perichoresis",t:"Perichoresis (circumincession)",la:"circumincessio",d:"Trinity",b:"The mutual indwelling of the three divine persons in one another.",p:"Each divine person is wholly in the others, by unity of essence, relation and origin (John 14:10; ST I q.42 a.5; Council of Florence).",ex:"\"I am in the Father, and the Father in me\" (John 14:10).",where:"ST I q.42 a.5",ccc:"255",conf:"Mixing or merging of persons.",rel:["person","relation-divine"],lesson:"t4",alias:["perichoresis","circumincession"]},
 {id:"appropriation",t:"Appropriation",la:"appropriatio",d:"Trinity",b:"Attributing to one person something common to all three, because it resembles what is proper to that person.",p:"The manifestation of the persons by means of essential attributes, e.g. power to the Father, wisdom to the Son, goodness to the Spirit (ST I q.39 aa.7–8).",ex:"Creation is appropriated to the Father, though all three create.",where:"ST I q.39 aa.7–8",ccc:"258",conf:"A property proper to one person only, such as being unbegotten.",rel:["person"],lesson:"t4",alias:["appropriation","appropriated"]},
 {id:"mission",t:"Divine mission",la:"missio",d:"Trinity",b:"A divine person's being sent: his eternal procession together with a new way of being present in creatures.",p:"Mission includes the eternal procession and a temporal effect: visible (the Incarnation, the dove, tongues of fire) or invisible (by sanctifying grace) (ST I q.43).",ex:"The Spirit is sent at Pentecost and into the soul at baptism.",where:"ST I q.43",ccc:"689–690",conf:"A change in God; the change is in the creature.",rel:["procession","grace"],lesson:"t4",alias:["mission","missions","indwelling"]},
 {id:"conservation",t:"Conservation",la:"conservatio",d:"Creation",b:"God's continuous giving of being to creatures, without which they would cease to exist.",p:"The continuation of the act of creation: the being of every creature depends on God at every moment (ST I q.104 a.1).",ex:"A creature is like light in the air, not like a house left behind by its builder.",where:"ST I q.104 a.1",ccc:"301",conf:"Deism: God made the world and left it.",rel:["creation","esse"],lesson:"t5",alias:["conservation","preservation"]},
 {id:"secondary-cause",t:"Secondary cause",la:"causa secunda",d:"Creation",b:"A creature that truly causes effects, while depending on God as first cause.",p:"A created agent with real causal power received from God, who works in every agent without replacing it (ST I q.105 a.5).",ex:"Fire really heats; God gives fire its being and power.",where:"ST I q.105 a.5",ccc:"306–308",conf:"Occasionalism (only God causes).",rel:["providence","efficient-cause"],lesson:"t5",alias:["secondary cause","secondary causes"]},
 {id:"angel",t:"Angel",la:"angelus",d:"Creation",b:"A purely spiritual creature with intellect and will.",p:"An incorporeal intellectual substance; each angel is its own species (ST I q.50 aa.1, 4); knows by infused species; was tested by one free choice (q.62–63).",ex:"Gabriel, Michael, Raphael; each person's guardian angel.",where:"ST I qq.50–64",ccc:"328–336",conf:"Departed human souls.",rel:["intellect"],lesson:"t5",alias:["angel","angels","angelic"]},
 {id:"devotion",t:"Devotion",la:"devotio",d:"Spiritual theology",b:"Readiness of will to give oneself to God's service.",p:"The first and principal act of the virtue of religion: \"the will to give oneself readily to things concerning the service of God\" (ST II-II q.82 a.1).",ex:"Rising early to pray, gladly.",where:"ST II-II q.82",ccc:"2096",conf:"Feelings of sweetness, which may or may not accompany it.",rel:["contemplation"],lesson:"t9a",alias:["devotion"]},
 {id:"contemplation",t:"Contemplation",la:"contemplatio",d:"Spiritual theology",b:"A simple, loving gaze of the mind on divine truth.",p:"The act of the contemplative life, principally the contemplation of divine truth (ST II-II q.180 a.4); in its infused form, a gift of grace.",ex:"Resting silently before the Blessed Sacrament.",where:"ST II-II qq.179–182",ccc:"2709–2719",conf:"Discursive meditation, which reasons step by step.",rel:["devotion"],lesson:"t9a",alias:["contemplation","contemplative"]}
]);

Object.assign(LESSONS,{
t4:{id:"t4",code:"T4 · Lesson 1",title:"De Deo Trino: processions, relations and persons (ST I qq.27–43)",
 outcomes:["Explain why the Trinity cannot be proved by reason but can be shown not to be impossible (I q.32 a.1).","Derive the two processions from the two immanent acts of an intellectual nature (q.27).","Name the four real relations and explain why only three are persons (qq.28, 30).","Define a divine person as a subsisting relation (q.29 a.4).","Distinguish what is proper to a person from what is appropriated, and explain the missions (qq.39, 43)."],
 prereq:["person","procession","consubstantial","simplicity","arianism","sabellianism"],
 reading:{primary:[["ST I q.27 aa.1–5 (processions)",NA("1027.htm")],["ST I q.28 aa.1–4 (relations)",NA("1028.htm")],["ST I q.29 a.4; q.30 a.2 (person, three persons)",NA("1029.htm#article4")],["ST I q.32 a.1 (can reason know the Trinity?)",NA("1032.htm#article1")],["ST I q.43 a.3 (invisible mission)",NA("1043.htm#article3")],["CCC 232–267",'https://www.vatican.va/archive/ENG0015/__P17.HTM']],
  secondary:["Emery, G. (2007) The Trinitarian Theology of Saint Thomas Aquinas. Oxford: Oxford University Press.","Emery, G. (2011) The Trinity: An Introduction to Catholic Doctrine on the Triune God. Washington, DC: CUA Press.","Augustine, De Trinitate, books VIII–XV (the psychological analogy).","Garrigou-Lagrange, R. (1952) The Trinity and God the Creator. St Louis: Herder."]},
 scripture:[["John 1:1–18, the Word with God and God","j1-1"],["John 1:29–34, the Spirit descending on the Son","j1-29"],["John 5:19–30, the Son's equality and origin","j5-19"]],
 sections:[
 {id:"s1",h:"1. Can reason prove the Trinity?",html:`<p>Thomas's answer is a clear no.</p>
<div class="quote">"It is impossible to attain to the knowledge of the Trinity by natural reason."<span class="src">ST I q.32 a.1 · Dominican tr. (1920), public domain</span></div>
<p><strong>The argument.</strong> Reason knows God only from creatures, as a cause from its effects. But creating is common to all three persons, so it shows the unity of the essence, not the distinction of persons. So we can know by reason what belongs to the one God (T3), but not that God is three.</p>
<p><strong>Two dangers in trying to prove it</strong> (same article): it lowers the dignity of faith, which concerns \"things that appear not\" (Heb 11:1); and weak \"proofs\" expose the faith to ridicule from unbelievers, who think we believe for those reasons.</p>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">The key distinction (ad 2)</div><p>Reason can be used in two ways: to <em>prove</em> a principle (as we prove God is one), or to <em>confirm</em> an accepted principle by showing that its consequences fit, as a hypothesis in astronomy is confirmed by saving the appearances. Arguments for the Trinity (Augustine's analogy of the mind, Richard of St Victor's argument from love) work only in the second way: \"when assumed to be true, such reasons confirm it\".</p></div>
<div class="co analogy"><div class="co-h">Analogy · The key and the lock</div><p>You cannot work out the shape of a key by staring at a lock from outside. But once you have the key, you see how well it fits. Revelation gives the key; theology shows the fit.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Why can't natural reason prove the Trinity, according to Thomas?",[["Because the Trinity is irrational","No: it is above reason, not against it."],["Because reason knows God only through creation, which is the common work of all three persons","Correct: I q.32 a.1."],["Because Thomas distrusted philosophy","No: he uses philosophy throughout."]],1)},
 {id:"s2",h:"2. The two processions",html:`<p>Scripture speaks of the Son proceeding (John 8:42) and the Spirit proceeding (John 15:26). How can anything proceed in God without being a creature?</p>
<ol><li><strong>Not an outward procession.</strong> Arius took procession as an effect from a cause, making the Son a creature; Sabellius as a cause going out to its effects, making the persons mere modes. Both misread procession as outward (q.27 a.1).</li>
<li><strong>An inward procession.</strong> Some actions stay within the agent, above all understanding: \"whenever we understand, by the very fact of understanding there proceeds something within us, which is a conception of the object understood\" (q.27 a.1). This inner word does not leave the mind.</li>
<li><strong>Two immanent acts, two processions.</strong> An intellectual nature has two acts that remain within: understanding and willing. So there are two processions in God: of the Word, by intellect, and of Love, by will (q.27 a.3), and no more (q.27 a.5).</li>
<li><strong>Why the first is \"generation\" and the second is not.</strong> Generation is the origin of a living being from a living principle by way of likeness. The intellect's word is a likeness of what is known, so the Word is begotten: Son. Love does not proceed as a likeness but as an impulse toward the beloved, so the Spirit is not a second Son (q.27 aa.2, 4).</li></ol>
<div class="co core"><div class="co-h">[Core] The psychological analogy</div><p>Augustine (De Trinitate IX–XV) and Thomas find the image of the Trinity in the mind that knows and loves itself. It is an analogy, not a proof, and it limps: in us the word and love are accidents; in God they are subsistent persons.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Why is the procession of the Word called \"generation\" but the procession of Love is not?",[["Because the Spirit is less than the Son","No: they are equal."],["Because the intellect's word proceeds as a likeness, and love does not","Correct: q.27 aa.2, 4."],["Because Scripture never mentions the Spirit's procession","It does: John 15:26."]],1)},
 {id:"s3",h:"3. Real relations in God",html:`<p>Processions give rise to relations: whatever comes from a principle is related to it. Because the processions are real, the relations are real (q.28 a.1).</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Procession</th><th>Relation of the source</th><th>Relation of the one proceeding</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Word (generation)</td><td>Paternity</td><td>Filiation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Love (spiration)</td><td>Active spiration (Father and Son)</td><td>Passive spiration, or \"procession\" (Spirit)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Relation and essence.</strong> In creatures, a relation is an accident added to a substance. In God there are no accidents (T3, simplicity), so: \"relation really existing in God is really the same as His essence and only differs in its mode of intelligibility\" (q.28 a.2).</p>
<p><strong>Relation and relation.</strong> But relations that are opposed are really distinct from each other: \"The very nature of relative opposition includes distinction\" (q.28 a.3). A father is not his own son.</p>
<div class="co def"><div class="co-h">Definition · The rule of Florence</div><p>\"In God all is one where there is no opposition of relation\" (Council of Florence, 1442, as cited in CCC 255). Everything in God is one, except what is distinguished by opposed relations.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("According to ST I q.28 a.2, a relation in God is really…",[["an accident added to the essence","God has no accidents."],["the same as the divine essence, differing only in mode of understanding","Correct."],["a separate substance","That would be tritheism."]],1)},
 {id:"s4",h:"4. Person as subsisting relation; why three",html:`<p>Boethius defined a person as \"an individual substance of a rational nature\". How can God be three persons and one substance? Thomas's answer is his most original contribution to Trinitarian theology:</p>
<div class="quote">"Therefore a divine person signifies a relation as subsisting."<span class="src">ST I q.29 a.4</span></div>
<p>Because a relation in God is identical with the essence, it does not inhere in a subject: it subsists. The Father is subsisting paternity, the Son subsisting filiation, the Spirit subsisting procession.</p>
<p><strong>Why three, not four?</strong> \"A real distinction between the divine relations can come only from relative opposition\" (q.30 a.2). Paternity and filiation are opposed: two persons. Procession is opposed to active spiration. But active spiration is not opposed to paternity or filiation, so it belongs to both Father and Son as one principle, and makes no fourth person (q.30 a.2).</p>
<div class="co warn"><div class="co-h">[Warn] Three errors</div><ul><li><strong>Modalism</strong> (Sabellius): one person in three roles. Denies real relations.</li><li><strong>Arianism / subordinationism</strong>: the Son and Spirit are less than the Father. Denies identity of essence.</li><li><strong>Tritheism</strong>: three gods. Denies that the relations are identical with the one essence.</li></ul></div>`,
  check:Q("Why does active spiration not constitute a fourth person?",[["Because it is not real","It is real."],["Because it is not opposed to paternity or filiation, so it belongs to Father and Son together","Correct: q.30 a.2."],["Because the Spirit spirates himself","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s5",h:"5. Equality, appropriation and the missions",html:`<p><strong>Equality.</strong> \"We must needs admit equality among the divine persons\" (q.42 a.1): each has the whole divine nature. There is an order of origin (the Father is the principle without principle) but no order of greatness.</p>
<p><strong>Appropriation.</strong> We attribute power to the Father, wisdom to the Son and goodness to the Spirit, although all three are equally powerful, wise and good. This is \"for the manifestation of our faith\" (q.39 a.7): the common attribute helps us see what is proper to each person.</p>
<p><strong>Missions.</strong> To be sent is to proceed eternally and to be present in a new way in a creature. The Son is sent visibly in the Incarnation; the Spirit visibly at the Jordan and Pentecost; both are sent invisibly whenever grace is given. In grace, God is present \"as the object known is in the knower, and the beloved in the lover\", and so dwells in the soul \"as in His own temple\" (q.43 a.3).</p>
<div class="co apply"><div class="co-h">[Apply] Why this matters for prayer</div><p>The Christian prays <em>to</em> the Father, <em>through</em> the Son, <em>in</em> the Spirit (Eph 2:18). The life of grace is not a relationship with a distant God but a share in the Son's relation to the Father, by the Spirit dwelling within.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("When we say \"the Father is almighty\", the attribute of power is…",[["proper to the Father alone","All three are almighty."],["appropriated to the Father","Correct: q.39 a.7."],["denied of the Son","No."]],1)}
 ],
 quiz:[
  Q("Thomas holds that the Trinity can be known…",[["by natural reason from creation","No: I q.32 a.1."],["only by revelation, though reason can show it is not impossible","Correct."],["by mystical experience alone","Not his position."],["not at all","It is known by faith."]],1,"ST I q.32 a.1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["procession"],diff:1}),
  Q("Which heresy read divine procession as an effect proceeding from a cause, making the Son a creature?",[["Sabellianism","That read it as the cause going out to its effects."],["Arianism","Correct: q.27 a.1."],["Nestorianism","A Christological heresy."],["Pelagianism","About grace."]],1,"ST I q.27 a.1",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["arianism"],diff:2}),
  Q("The two processions in God correspond to which two acts?",[["Creating and redeeming","Outward acts common to all three."],["Understanding and willing","Correct: q.27 a.3."],["Sensing and moving","Not in God."],["Judging and saving","No."]],1,"ST I q.27 a.3",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["procession"],diff:1}),
  Q("Which of these is NOT one of the four real relations in God?",[["Paternity","It is."],["Filiation","It is."],["Creation","Correct: creation is a relation of creatures to God, not within God."],["Passive spiration","It is."]],2,"ST I q.28",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["relation-divine"],diff:2}),
  Q("A divine person is defined by Thomas as…",[["an individual substance of a rational nature, without qualification","Boethius's definition, which needs adapting."],["a relation as subsisting","Correct: q.29 a.4."],["a mode of the one person","Modalism."],["a part of God","God has no parts."]],1,"ST I q.29 a.4",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["person","relation-divine"],diff:2}),
  Q("Real distinction in God arises only from…",[["different natures","There is one nature."],["relative opposition","Correct: q.28 a.3; q.30 a.2."],["different attributes","Attributes are one in God."],["different operations ad extra","These are common to all three."]],1,"ST I q.30 a.2",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["relation-divine"],diff:2}),
  Q("\"In God all is one where there is no opposition of relation\" is a principle from…",[["Nicaea (325)","No."],["Florence (1442)","Correct; cited in CCC 255."],["Trent (1546)","No."],["Vatican I (1870)","No."]],1,"CCC 255",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["relation-divine"],diff:2}),
  Q("Attributing wisdom to the Son, though all three are wise, is an example of…",[["a personal property","Proper to one only."],["appropriation","Correct."],["modalism","No."],["a mission","No."]],1,"ST I q.39 a.7",{bloom:"Apply",terms:["appropriation"],diff:1}),
  Q("According to I q.43 a.3, the invisible mission of a divine person takes place by…",[["any natural gift","No: only grace."],["sanctifying grace","Correct."],["miracles only","Those can be signs of visible missions."],["the sacraments only","Grace may also be given outside them."]],1,"ST I q.43 a.3",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["mission","grace"],diff:2}),
  Q("Why is the Holy Spirit not a second Son?",[["Because he is created","No."],["Because love does not proceed by way of likeness, as the word does","Correct: q.27 a.4."],["Because Scripture forbids the name","Not Thomas's reason."],["Because he proceeds from the Father alone","Not the reason, and the Latin tradition holds the Filioque."]],1,"ST I q.27 a.4",{bloom:"Analyse",terms:["procession"],diff:3}),
  Q("Reason's role regarding the Trinity, in I q.32 a.1 ad 2, is like…",[["a demonstration in geometry","That proves a principle."],["a hypothesis confirmed by fitting the observations","Correct: showing congruity."],["a vote","No."],["a feeling","No."]],1,"ST I q.32 a.1 ad 2",{bloom:"Analyse",terms:[],diff:3}),
  Q("Which council defined the divinity of the Holy Spirit (\"Lord and giver of life\")?",[["Nicaea I (325)","Defined the Son's divinity."],["Constantinople I (381)","Correct."],["Ephesus (431)","Defined Theotokos."],["Chalcedon (451)","Defined the two natures."]],1,"Creed of 381",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:1})
 ],
 construct:{q:"Whether the trinity of the divine persons can be known by natural reason?",ref:"ST I q.32 a.1",
  model:{obj:"It seems it can: philosophers spoke of a threefold God, and Richard of St Victor argued that infinite goodness must share itself, so that love requires a beloved and a co-beloved.",sc:"Hilary: \"Let no man think to reach the sacred mystery of generation by his own mind.\"",resp:"Natural reason knows God only from creatures, as cause from effect. But creative power is common to the whole Trinity and belongs to the unity of the essence, not the distinction of persons. Therefore by reason we can know what belongs to the one essence, but not the Trinity. To try to prove it lessens the dignity of faith and exposes it to ridicule.",rep:"Arguments like Richard's do not prove the Trinity; they confirm it once believed, by showing its fittingness, as a hypothesis is confirmed by saving the appearances."},
  rubric:["Objection gives a real philosophical argument (e.g. from love).","Sed contra cites Hilary or Ambrose.","Respondeo: reason knows God only as cause, and creation is common to the three.","Respondeo names both dangers (dignity of faith; ridicule).","Reply distinguishes proving from confirming."]},
 drill:{title:"Order Thomas's treatise on the Trinity",parts:["Processions (q.27)","Relations (q.28)","Persons (qq.29–43, beginning with the definition of person)","The persons considered individually: Father, Son, Holy Spirit (qq.33–38)","The persons compared, and their missions (qq.39–43)"]},
 connections:`<p><strong>Back to T3.</strong> Divine simplicity (q.3) is what makes relations identical with the essence. Without it, three relations would be three accidents or three gods.</p><p><strong>Forward.</strong> T11 (Christology) applies the doctrine of person to the Incarnation: one divine person, two natures. T13 (sacraments) and the Apologetics disputation on the Filioque use q.36.</p><p><strong>Psychology.</strong> Thomas's model of the person as intrinsically relational has been drawn on by Catholic psychologists (Vitz et al., 2020) to criticise purely individualistic models of the self, a theme you meet in SLK 320.</p>`},

t5:{id:"t5",code:"T5 · Lesson 1",title:"Creation, conservation, angels and providence (ST I qq.44–49, 50–64, 103–105)",
 outcomes:["Define creation as the production of being from nothing and explain why it is not a change.","Explain why the world's beginning in time is held by faith, not proved (I q.46 a.2).","Explain conservation and God's action in every agent without occasionalism (q.104 a.1; q.105 a.5).","Set out Thomas's doctrine of angels: incorporeal, each its own species, tested once.","Explain providence and secondary causes, and how contingency is preserved (q.22 a.3; q.19 a.8)."],
 prereq:["creation","esse","providence","efficient-cause","final-cause"],
 reading:{primary:[["ST I q.44 a.4 (God's end in creating)",NA("1044.htm#article4")],["ST I q.45 a.1 (creation from nothing)",NA("1045.htm#article1")],["ST I q.46 a.2 (the beginning of the world)",NA("1046.htm#article2")],["ST I q.47 a.1 (why many creatures)",NA("1047.htm#article1")],["ST I q.50 aa.1, 4 (angels)",NA("1050.htm")],["ST I q.22 a.3; q.104 a.1; q.105 a.5",NA("1022.htm#article3")],["CCC 279–354",'https://www.vatican.va/archive/ENG0015/__P18.HTM']],
  secondary:["Feser, E. (2017) Five Proofs of the Existence of God. San Francisco: Ignatius Press, ch. 7 (God and the world).","Garrigou-Lagrange, R. (1937) Providence. St Louis: Herder.","Baldner, S.E. and Carroll, W.E. (1997) Aquinas on Creation. Toronto: PIMS.","Carroll, W.E. (2000) 'Creation and science in the Middle Ages', New Blackfriars, 81(950), pp. 101–111. [verify pages]"]},
 scripture:[["John 1:3, all things were made by him","j1-1"],["John 1:47–51, angels ascending and descending","j1-47"],["John 5:17, my Father works until now","j5-10"]],
 sections:[
 {id:"s1",h:"1. What creation is",html:`<p>Ordinary making presupposes material: a sculptor needs marble, a parent gives life from what already exists. Creation is different in kind:</p>
<div class="quote">"For nothing is the same as no being."<span class="src">ST I q.45 a.1</span></div>
<p>Thomas's argument: when we consider \"the emanation of all being from the universal cause\", nothing can be presupposed, since that would itself be a being. So to create is to make something from nothing (ex nihilo): not out of \"nothing\" as a kind of stuff, but with nothing presupposed.</p>
<p><strong>Not a change.</strong> \"Creation is not change, except according to a mode of understanding\" (q.45 a.2 ad 2). Change needs a subject that is first one way and then another. In creation there is no prior subject. What remains is a relation: the creature's total dependence on God.</p>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge · Science and creation</div><p>Physics studies change: how one state of the universe becomes another. Creation is not a change, so physics, including Big Bang cosmology, neither proves nor refutes it. The question \"why is there anything at all?\" belongs to metaphysics and theology (Baldner and Carroll, 1997).</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Why is creation not a change, according to Thomas?",[["Because it happened too fast to observe","No."],["Because change needs a pre-existing subject, and creation presupposes nothing","Correct: q.45 a.2 ad 2."],["Because only angels were created","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s2",h:"2. Did the world have a beginning?",html:`<p>Thomas's position surprised many contemporaries, Christian and Muslim:</p>
<div class="quote">"By faith alone do we hold, and by no demonstration can it be proved, that the world did not always exist."<span class="src">ST I q.46 a.2</span></div>
<p><strong>Why not provable?</strong> Demonstration proceeds from essences, and essences abstract from \"here\" and \"now\", so nothing about what a thing is shows it must have begun. Nor can we prove it from God's will, since God creates freely, and we know his free choices only by revelation (q.46 a.2).</p>
<p><strong>Two opposite errors.</strong> Against Aristotle's followers, who held the world must be eternal, Thomas shows their arguments are not demonstrative (q.46 a.1). Against Bonaventure and others, who thought reason could prove a beginning, he warns that weak proofs expose the faith to ridicule (q.46 a.2).</p>
<div class="co core"><div class="co-h">[Core] The key point</div><p>Even an eternal world would be created: totally dependent on God for its being at every moment. Creation is about dependence, not about a first moment (De aeternitate mundi, 1270).</p></div>`,
  check:Q("According to I q.46 a.2, the world's beginning in time is known…",[["by demonstration","No."],["by faith alone","Correct."],["by modern physics alone","Physics studies change, not creation."]],1)},
 {id:"s3",h:"3. Why so many creatures? Why angels?",html:`<p>God does not create to gain anything: \"He intends only to communicate His perfection, which is His goodness\" (q.44 a.4). And because no single creature can represent that goodness adequately, he makes many:</p>
<div class="quote">"For goodness, which in God is simple and uniform, in creatures is manifold and divided and hence the whole universe together participates the divine goodness more perfectly, and represents it better than any single creature whatever."<span class="src">ST I q.47 a.1</span></div>
<p><strong>Angels.</strong> The perfection of the universe requires intellectual creatures, and \"intelligence cannot be the action of a body\", so there must be purely spiritual creatures (q.50 a.1). Because angels have no matter, which is what distinguishes individuals within a species, \"it is impossible for two angels to be of one species\" (q.50 a.4): each angel is like a species of its own.</p>
<p><strong>The fall of the angels.</strong> Angels decide by one complete act, since they see the whole of a choice at once; their choice is therefore fixed (q.64 a.2). The first sin of the angel was pride: refusing subjection where it was due, and seeking to be like God by his own power rather than by grace (q.63 aa.2–3).</p>`,
  check:Q("Why does Thomas hold that no two angels share a species?",[["Because angels are too proud to share","No."],["Because individuals within a species are distinguished by matter, and angels have none","Correct: q.50 a.4."],["Because Scripture says so","He argues from reason."]],1)},
 {id:"s4",h:"4. Conservation and God's action in every agent",html:`<p>Creation is not only a past event: \"the being of every creature depends on God, so that not for a moment could it subsist, but would fall into nothingness were it not kept in being by the operation of the Divine power\" (q.104 a.1).</p>
<p><strong>But creatures really act.</strong> Some (later called occasionalists) held that God alone causes: fire does not heat, God heats in the fire. Thomas rejects this: \"this is impossible\" (q.105 a.5), because it takes away the order of cause and effect, \"and this would imply lack of power in the Creator\": a greater cause gives real power to its effects.</p>
<p><strong>How both are true.</strong> God works in every agent by giving it being, conserving its power, applying it to act, and as the end for which it acts. The creature is a real cause, but a secondary one, as the pen truly writes in the writer's hand. God and creature are not two causes competing on the same level.</p>
<div class="co analogy"><div class="co-h">Analogy · The author and the characters</div><p>In a good novel the characters act from their own motives, yet every line exists because the author writes it. The author does not compete with the characters; he is on a different level. God's causality is more intimate still: he gives existence itself.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Thomas rejects the view that God alone acts in nature because…",[["it would imply lack of power in the Creator, who gives real power to creatures","Correct: q.105 a.5."],["fire is stronger than God","No."],["creatures do not need God","They do, at every moment."]],0)},
 {id:"s5",h:"5. Providence, secondary causes and contingency",html:`<p>Providence has two parts: the plan of the order of things to their end, and its execution, called government (q.22 a.3). God plans everything immediately, \"even the smallest\". But he executes the plan through creatures:</p>
<div class="quote">"He governs things inferior by superior, not on account of any defect in His power, but by reason of the abundance of His goodness; so that the dignity of causality is imparted even to creatures."<span class="src">ST I q.22 a.3</span></div>
<p><strong>Contingency.</strong> If God's will is always fulfilled, is everything necessary? No: \"God wills some things to be done necessarily, some contingently\" (q.19 a.8). His will is so efficacious that things happen not only as he wills but in the mode he wills: necessary things necessarily, free choices freely.</p>
<div class="co stance"><div class="co-h">[Stance] A contested question</div><p><strong>How exactly does God's causality relate to free choice?</strong> Thomists in the Báñez–Garrigou-Lagrange line hold that God moves the will by a \"physical premotion\" that infallibly yet freely brings about the choice. Molinists (Luis de Molina, 1588) hold that God knows what free creatures would do in any situation (\"middle knowledge\") and arranges providence accordingly. In 1607 Paul V ended the <em>de auxiliis</em> controversy by allowing both schools and forbidding them to condemn each other. Present both fairly.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("In I q.22 a.3, why does God govern lower things through higher ones?",[["Because he lacks power","Explicitly denied."],["To give creatures the dignity of causality, out of abundance of goodness","Correct."],["Because angels insisted","No."]],1)}
 ],
 quiz:[
  Q("Thomas defines God's end in creating as…",[["acquiring glory he lacked","God lacks nothing."],["communicating his perfection, which is his goodness","Correct: q.44 a.4."],["necessity of his nature","He creates freely."],["defeating chaos","Mythological, not Thomist."]],1,"ST I q.44 a.4",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["creation"],diff:1}),
  Q("\"Ex nihilo\" means…",[["out of a stuff called nothing","No."],["with no pre-existing material or subject","Correct: q.45 a.1."],["out of God's substance","That is emanationism."],["out of chaos","No."]],1,"ST I q.45 a.1",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["creation"],diff:1}),
  Q("Can reason prove the world began in time, according to Thomas?",[["Yes, by the impossibility of an infinite past","Bonaventure's view, not Thomas's."],["No; it is held by faith alone","Correct: q.46 a.2."],["Yes, by physics","No."],["No, because the world is eternal","He does not say the world is eternal."]],1,"ST I q.46 a.2",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["creation"],diff:2}),
  Q("Why did God make many kinds of creatures?",[["By accident","No."],["Because no single creature can adequately represent his goodness","Correct: q.47 a.1."],["Because matter required it","Thomas rejects that explanation."],["Because angels made them","Avicenna's view, rejected."]],1,"ST I q.47 a.1",{bloom:"Understand",terms:[],diff:2}),
  Q("Each angel is its own species because…",[["angels are infinite","No."],["they have no matter to distinguish individuals of one species","Correct: q.50 a.4."],["they choose their species","No."],["God dislikes repetition","No."]],1,"ST I q.50 a.4",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["angel"],diff:2}),
  Q("The first sin of the angel was…",[["lust","Impossible for pure spirits."],["pride","Correct: q.63 a.2."],["gluttony","No."],["sloth","No."]],1,"ST I q.63 a.2",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["angel"],diff:1}),
  Q("Conservation means…",[["God made the world and left it","That is deism."],["God continuously gives being to creatures","Correct: q.104 a.1."],["creatures preserve themselves","No."],["God preserves only species","No."]],1,"ST I q.104 a.1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["conservation"],diff:1}),
  Q("Occasionalism holds that…",[["creatures are real causes","That is Thomas's view."],["God alone causes, and creatures are mere occasions","Correct; Thomas rejects it (q.105 a.5)."],["chance governs all","No."],["angels move the stars","A medieval view, not occasionalism."]],1,"ST I q.105 a.5",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["secondary-cause"],diff:2}),
  Q("According to I q.19 a.8, contingent things…",[["happen against God's will","No."],["happen contingently because God wills them to happen in that mode","Correct."],["do not exist","They do."],["are necessary after all","No."]],1,"ST I q.19 a.8",{bloom:"Analyse",terms:["providence"],diff:3}),
  Q("Which pope ended the de auxiliis controversy by permitting both Thomists and Molinists?",[["Pius V","No."],["Paul V (1607)","Correct."],["Leo XIII","No."],["Pius X","No."]],1,"de auxiliis",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["providence"],diff:2})
 ],
 construct:{q:"Whether it is an article of faith that the world began?",ref:"ST I q.46 a.2",
  model:{obj:"It seems that the newness of the world can be demonstrated: an infinite series of past days could not have been traversed, so the world must have begun.",sc:"The unity of the Trinity cannot be demonstrated (I q.32 a.1), and the same holds of the newness of the world, which is held by faith.",resp:"The newness of the world cannot be demonstrated from the world itself, since demonstration proceeds from essences, which abstract from here and now; nor from the efficient cause, since God acts by free will, which reason cannot investigate except where he wills necessarily. Therefore that the world began is held by faith alone. It is useful to consider this, lest anyone, presuming to demonstrate what is of faith, bring forward unconvincing reasons and expose the faith to ridicule.",rep:"An infinite past series is not traversed from a first term, since there would be no first term, so the objection assumes what it should prove."},
  rubric:["Objection gives a real argument (e.g. from traversing the infinite).","Sed contra draws the parallel with the Trinity.","Respondeo: not demonstrable from the world (essences abstract from time).","Respondeo: not demonstrable from God's will, which is free.","Reply answers the infinite-traversal argument."]},
 drill:{title:"Order Thomas's treatment of God and creatures",parts:["The first cause of beings (q.44)","The mode of their emanation: creation (q.45)","The beginning of their duration (q.46)","The distinction of creatures, including angels and man (qq.47–102)","Government and conservation of creatures (qq.103–119)"]},
 connections:`<p><strong>Back to F3 and F4.</strong> Creation applies the real distinction of essence and existence: what receives existence must receive it from what is existence itself.</p><p><strong>Forward.</strong> T6 needs providence and secondary causality to explain human freedom and grace. T15 returns to the angels' fixed choice when explaining why mortal sin after death cannot be repented.</p><p><strong>Science.</strong> Use section 1 when you meet the claim, common in popular science writing, that cosmology has made creation unnecessary.</p>`},

t9a:{id:"t9a",code:"T9a · Lesson 1",title:"Spiritual theology I: devotion, prayer and contemplation (ST II-II qq.82–83, 179–182)",
 outcomes:["Define devotion as an act of the virtue of religion and distinguish it from feelings (q.82).","Explain why prayer is fitting, given an unchanging providence (q.83 a.2).","Analyse the order of the Lord's Prayer (q.83 a.9).","Explain the role of attention and the three effects of prayer (q.83 a.13).","Compare the active and contemplative lives, and explain the Dominican ideal of handing on contemplation (qq.182, 188 a.6)."],
 prereq:["devotion","contemplation","charity","providence"],
 reading:{primary:[["ST II-II q.82 a.1 (devotion)",NA("3082.htm#article1")],["ST II-II q.83 aa.1, 2, 9, 13, 14 (prayer)",NA("3083.htm")],["ST II-II q.180 a.4; q.182 a.1; q.188 a.6",NA("3180.htm#article4")],["CCC 2558–2758",'https://www.vatican.va/archive/ENG0015/__P8X.HTM']],
  secondary:["Garrigou-Lagrange, R. (1947–48) The Three Ages of the Interior Life. 2 vols. St Louis: Herder.","Garrigou-Lagrange, R. (1937) Christian Perfection and Contemplation. St Louis: Herder.","Teresa of Avila, The Interior Castle (1577).","Torrell, J.-P. (2003) Saint Thomas Aquinas, vol. 2: Spiritual Master. Washington, DC: CUA Press."]},
 scripture:[["John 4:23–24, worship in spirit and truth","j4-10"],["John 1:35–39, \"Come and see\": the first disciples stay with Jesus","j1-35"]],
 sections:[
 {id:"s1",h:"1. Devotion: the will to serve",html:`<p>We often say we \"feel devout\". Thomas locates devotion elsewhere:</p>
<div class="quote">"Hence devotion is apparently nothing else but the will to give oneself readily to things concerning the service of God."<span class="src">ST II-II q.82 a.1</span></div>
<p>Devotion is the first act of the virtue of <strong>religion</strong>, the part of justice that gives God the worship due to him (II-II q.81). It is an act of the will, so it can be present when feelings are absent. Its causes are meditation on God's goodness (which moves love) and on our own weakness (which removes presumption) (q.82 a.3).</p>
<div class="co confused"><div class="co-h">If this confused you</div><p>Feelings of sweetness in prayer are gifts and can help, but they are not the measure of devotion. A tired student who still prays because God deserves it is devout in Thomas's sense.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("For Thomas, devotion is chiefly…",[["a feeling of sweetness","Feelings may accompany it."],["the will to give oneself readily to God's service","Correct: q.82 a.1."],["a type of prayer","It is an act of religion that animates prayer."]],1)},
 {id:"s2",h:"2. Why pray if God does not change?",html:`<p>Thomas names three ancient errors that make prayer pointless: that providence does not rule human affairs; that everything happens by necessity; and that providence can be changed by our prayers (q.83 a.2). Against all three:</p>
<div class="quote">"We pray not that we may change the Divine disposition, but that we may impetrate that which God has disposed."<span class="src">ST II-II q.83 a.2</span></div>
<p><strong>The logic.</strong> Providence includes not only which effects happen, but through which causes. God has disposed that certain goods be given through prayer, as he disposed that crops grow through farming. Prayer is a real secondary cause (T5).</p>
<p><strong>Prayer as an act of reason.</strong> \"Prayer, as we speak of it now, is an act of reason\" (q.83 a.1): it orders and expresses desire before God. It presupposes the will's desire, especially charity.</p>`,
  check:Q("According to II-II q.83 a.2, we pray in order to…",[["change God's mind","Explicitly denied."],["obtain what God has disposed to give through prayer","Correct."],["inform God of our needs","He already knows them."]],1)},
 {id:"s3",h:"3. The Lord's Prayer: the order of desire",html:`<p>\"The Lord's Prayer is most perfect\" (q.83 a.9), because \"not only do we ask for all that we may rightly desire, but also in the order wherein we ought to desire them, so that this prayer not only teaches us to ask, but also directs all our affections\".</p>
<ol><li><strong>The end:</strong> God's glory (hallowed be thy name) and our enjoyment of it (thy kingdom come).</li>
<li><strong>The means:</strong> obedience (thy will be done) and what we need (daily bread, bodily and sacramental).</li>
<li><strong>Removing obstacles:</strong> sin (forgive us), temptation (lead us not), and penalties (deliver us from evil).</li></ol>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge · Prayer forms desire</div><p>Because prayer interprets desire before God, praying the Our Father daily is a training of the affections: it teaches us to want first what is first. This links T9a to the doctrine of the passions (F5) and of the last end (F7).</p></div>`,
  check:Q("What makes the Lord's Prayer \"most perfect\" for Thomas?",[["It is the shortest","No."],["It asks for everything rightly desired, in the right order","Correct: q.83 a.9."],["It was composed by the apostles","It was taught by Christ."]],1)},
 {id:"s4",h:"4. Attention, distraction and perseverance",html:`<p>Must prayer be attentive throughout to be worth anything? Thomas distinguishes three effects of prayer (q.83 a.13):</p>
<ol><li><strong>Merit</strong>, common to all acts done in charity: here \"the force of the original intention with which one sets about praying renders the whole prayer meritorious\", even if attention wanders involuntarily.</li>
<li><strong>Impetration</strong> (obtaining what is asked): the first intention also suffices.</li>
<li><strong>Spiritual refreshment</strong> of the mind: this requires actual attention.</li></ol>
<p><strong>How long?</strong> The desire of charity, which is the cause of prayer, should be continual; prayer itself should last as long as it stirs fervour, and not so long as to cause weariness (q.83 a.14).</p>
<div class="co clinical"><div class="co-h">Clinical practicality</div><p>For a person with ADHD (P4), Thomas's distinction is consoling: wandering attention does not cancel the prayer's merit if the intention was set. Shorter, more frequent prayer fits q.83 a.14 well.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Which effect of prayer requires actual attention, according to q.83 a.13?",[["Merit","The first intention suffices."],["Spiritual refreshment","Correct."],["Impetration","The first intention suffices."]],1)},
 {id:"s5",h:"5. Active and contemplative lives",html:`<p>\"The contemplative life is simply more excellent than the active\" (q.182 a.1), because it engages what is best in us (the intellect) with its highest object (God), and it begins here what will be perfect in heaven. That which belongs principally to the contemplative life \"is the contemplation of the divine truth, because this contemplation is the end of the whole human life\" (q.180 a.4).</p>
<p><strong>But the highest life combines both.</strong> In the work of teaching and preaching that flows from contemplation:</p>
<div class="quote">"For even as it is better to enlighten than merely to shine, so is it better to give to others the fruits of one's contemplation than merely to contemplate."<span class="src">ST II-II q.188 a.6</span></div>
<p>This became the Dominican motto <em>contemplari et contemplata aliis tradere</em>. It is the ideal behind this whole course: study that becomes prayer, and prayer that becomes teaching.</p>
<div class="co analogy"><div class="co-h">Analogy · The well and the irrigation channel</div><p>A well that is never drawn on is clean but useless to the fields; a channel with no well runs dry. The Dominican life is the well feeding the channel.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("In II-II q.188 a.6, what is better than merely contemplating?",[["Merely acting","No."],["Giving to others the fruits of contemplation","Correct."],["Studying without prayer","No."]],1)}
 ],
 quiz:[
  Q("Devotion is the first act of which virtue?",[["Faith","No."],["Religion","Correct: q.82."],["Temperance","No."],["Prudence","No."]],1,"ST II-II q.82",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["devotion"],diff:1}),
  Q("Prayer, as Thomas discusses it, is an act of…",[["the senses","No."],["reason","Correct: q.83 a.1."],["the irascible appetite","No."],["the body","No."]],1,"ST II-II q.83 a.1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:1}),
  Q("Which is NOT one of the three ancient errors about prayer in q.83 a.2?",[["Providence does not rule human affairs","It is one."],["All happens by necessity","It is one."],["God's disposition is changed by prayer","It is one."],["Prayer is a secondary cause in God's plan","Correct: that is Thomas's own view."]],3,"ST II-II q.83 a.2",{bloom:"Analyse",terms:["providence"],diff:2}),
  Q("The first petitions of the Our Father concern…",[["our daily needs","Those come later."],["the end: God's glory and our share in it","Correct: q.83 a.9."],["temptation","Later."],["enemies","No."]],1,"ST II-II q.83 a.9",{bloom:"Understand",terms:[],diff:1}),
  Q("Involuntary distraction during vocal prayer…",[["makes the prayer worthless","No."],["does not remove merit if the initial intention was right","Correct: q.83 a.13."],["is always a mortal sin","No."],["is impossible for saints","No."]],1,"ST II-II q.83 a.13",{bloom:"Apply",terms:[],diff:2}),
  Q("According to q.83 a.14, prayer should last…",[["as long as possible regardless of fervour","No."],["as long as it is useful in arousing fervour, without causing weariness","Correct."],["exactly one hour","No."],["only five minutes","No."]],1,"ST II-II q.83 a.14",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:2}),
  Q("The contemplative life is simply more excellent because…",[["it is easier","No."],["it engages the intellect with its highest object, God","Correct: q.182 a.1."],["it avoids people","No."],["it earns more merit in every case","Merit depends on charity."]],1,"ST II-II q.182 a.1",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["contemplation"],diff:2}),
  Q("\"Contemplata aliis tradere\" means…",[["to keep contemplation private","Opposite."],["to hand on to others the things contemplated","Correct."],["to contemplate others","No."],["to trade in contemplation","No."]],1,"ST II-II q.188 a.6",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["contemplation"],diff:1}),
  Q("Which of these is a cause of devotion, according to q.82 a.3?",[["Meditation on God's goodness and our weakness","Correct."],["Good weather","No."],["Feelings alone","No."],["Avoiding all study","No."]],0,"ST II-II q.82 a.3",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["devotion"],diff:2}),
  Q("Garrigou-Lagrange's \"three ages\" of the interior life are…",[["childhood, youth, age","No."],["purgative, illuminative, unitive","Correct."],["faith, hope, charity","Those are virtues."],["Advent, Lent, Easter","No."]],1,"Garrigou-Lagrange (1947–48)",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:1})
 ],
 construct:{q:"Whether it is becoming to pray?",ref:"ST II-II q.83 a.2",
  model:{obj:"It seems not: prayer seems to inform God of our needs, but he knows all; and prayer seems to change his will, but his will is unchangeable.",sc:"\"Ask, and it shall be given you\" (Luke 11:9).",resp:"Three ancient errors made prayer useless: that providence does not rule human affairs; that everything happens of necessity; and that providence is changed by prayer. Against these: providence disposes not only which effects happen but by what causes, and among these causes are human acts. So we pray not to change God's disposition but to obtain what he has disposed to be fulfilled through our prayers.",rep:"We do not inform God; we pray so that we may consider that we need his help, and so grow in trust and humility."},
  rubric:["Objection uses God's knowledge or unchangeability.","Sed contra cites Christ's command.","Respondeo names the three errors.","Respondeo: prayer is a cause disposed by providence.","Reply explains what prayer does for us."]},
 drill:{title:"Order the petitions of the Lord's Prayer as Thomas groups them",parts:["The end itself: God's glory (hallowed be thy name)","Our attaining the end (thy kingdom come)","The means: obedience (thy will be done)","What we need for the way (daily bread)","Removal of obstacles (forgive us; lead us not; deliver us)"]},
 connections:`<p><strong>Back to T5.</strong> Prayer is a real secondary cause in providence.</p><p><strong>Forward.</strong> T9 continues with states of life and the stages of growth (Garrigou-Lagrange). The Prayers tab puts this lesson into practice.</p><p><strong>Psychology.</strong> Attention research distinguishes sustained from selective attention; Thomas's distinction between initial intention and actual attention maps onto that and is pastorally helpful for anxious or distractible students.</p>`}
});
