Object.assign(EXTRAS,{
 t4:{plain:{version:"God is one, and in him there are three persons. Reason cannot prove this, but once it is revealed, Thomas shows how it holds together: in God there are two inner acts, knowing and loving; from these come relations; and each relation, being identical with God's being, is a person.",question:"How can God be one and three without contradiction?",one:"One in essence, three by opposed relations of origin; each person is a relation that subsists."},
  hook:"\"2-4-3\": two processions, four relations, three persons.",
  analogy:{title:"Mind, word and love",text:"When you understand something, a word forms in your mind; when you love it, love proceeds from you. These are inside you, not outside. In God the Word and Love are not passing acts but persons, each wholly God."},
  sort:{title:"Proper or appropriated?",cats:["Proper to one person","Appropriated"],items:[["Being unbegotten (Father)",0],["Power (Father)",1],["Being begotten (Son)",0],["Wisdom (Son)",1],["Proceeding from Father and Son (Spirit)",0],["Goodness (Spirit)",1]]}},
 t5:{plain:{version:"God makes everything from nothing, keeps it in being at every moment, and works through creatures as real causes. Angels are pure spirits. Providence plans everything and carries it out through creatures, without making free choices unfree.",question:"How does God relate to the world, moment by moment?",one:"Creation is total dependence, not just a first moment; God gives creatures real causality."},
  hook:"\"C-C-C\": Creation (from nothing), Conservation (every moment), Concurrence (in every act).",
  analogy:{title:"The singer and the song",text:"A song exists only while the singer sings it; stop singing and it stops. Creatures depend on God like that, not like a statue left behind by a sculptor."},
  sort:{title:"Thomist position or error?",cats:["Thomas","Error he rejects"],items:[["The world's beginning is known by faith",0],["Reason proves the world is eternal",1],["Creatures are real causes",0],["God alone causes in nature",1],["Each angel is its own species",0],["Providence is changed by our prayers",1]]}},
 t9a:{plain:{version:"Devotion is a ready will to serve God; prayer is reason's expression of desire before God. We do not change God by praying; we receive what he planned to give through our prayer. Contemplation is the highest act of life, and handing it on to others is better still.",question:"What is prayer, and why does it work?",one:"Pray to receive what God disposed to give through prayer; let the Our Father order your desires."},
  hook:"\"Merit, Impetration, Refreshment\": the three effects of prayer (q.83 a.13).",
  analogy:{title:"The farmer and the rain",text:"God gives the harvest, but through the farmer's sowing. He gives some goods through our asking in the same way."},
  sort:{title:"Which effect of prayer?",cats:["Merit","Impetration","Spiritual refreshment"],items:[["Pleasing God by an act of charity",0],["Obtaining what we ask",1],["The mind's consolation, which needs attention",2]]}}
});
Object.assign(DEPTH,{
 t4:{cases:[["\"Three in one doesn't add up\"","A friend says the Trinity is bad arithmetic. Answer: it would be if we said one God and three Gods, or one person and three persons. We say one in essence and three in relation: different respects, so no contradiction."],["The baptism of a child","At a baptism the priest says \"in the name\" (singular) \"of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit\". One name, three persons: the formula itself contains the doctrine."],["Muslim objection","A Muslim colleague says Christians worship three gods. Explain the difference between three relations in one simple essence and three beings (see Apologetics: \"The Word\")."]],
  allegory:["The sun, its light and its warmth","Patristic writers used the sun, its radiance and its heat: one sun, the light from it, and the warmth from both. Thomas would add the warning: every image limps, because light and heat are not persons and are not each the whole sun."],
  slow:{title:"ST I q.29 a.4: \"person\" signifies relation",steps:[["\"Person\" in God is said of each of the three, unlike essential names, but does not obviously express relation either.","The puzzle: is \"person\" an essence-word like \"God\" or a relation-word like \"Father\"?"],["Some said it signifies the essence, and only by Church usage a relation.","Thomas finds this unsatisfactory: usage cannot change what the word means."],["Distinction in God is only by relations of origin, and relation in God is not an accident but the essence itself.","Hence a relation in God subsists."],["\"Therefore a divine person signifies a relation as subsisting.\"","Person signifies relation directly and essence indirectly."]]},
  confusions:[["Are the persons parts of God?","No. Each person is the whole divine essence; they differ only by relation."],["Is the Father older or greater?","No. He is the principle without principle, but not prior in time or greater in nature (q.42)."]],
  further:[["Emery, G. (2011) The Trinity: An Introduction to Catholic Doctrine on the Triune God.","The best short introduction, written by a leading Thomist; read chapters 3–5."],["Garrigou-Lagrange, R. (1952) The Trinity and God the Creator.","Article-by-article commentary on I qq.27–43; dense, use with the Summa open."]]},
 t5:{cases:[["\"The Big Bang replaces God\"","A lecturer says cosmology explains the universe without God. Thomas: cosmology explains how one physical state follows another; creation is the giving of being to the whole, which is not a physical process."],["Natural disaster and providence","An earthquake kills many. Providence does not mean God wills every evil directly; he permits natural causes to act and draws good from evil (I q.22 a.2 ad 2; q.48)."],["Guardian angels","A child asks whether angels are real. CCC 328 calls their existence a truth of faith, and Thomas teaches each person has a guardian angel (I q.113)."]],
  allegory:["The pen and the writer","A letter is wholly written by the writer and wholly written by the pen, but in different ways. Nobody says the pen did half. God and creatures cause in this way."],
  slow:{title:"ST I q.105 a.5: Does God work in every agent?",steps:[["Some held that God alone works, and created things have no effect.","Occasionalism, then held by some Muslim theologians (the Ash'arites)."],["\"But this is impossible.\" First, it removes the order of cause and effect, \"and this would imply lack of power in the Creator\".","A better cause makes things that are themselves causes."],["Second, powers in things would be given for nothing, if they did nothing.","God does nothing in vain."],["So God works in every agent: as end, as giving the power, as applying it, and as keeping it in being.","Both God and creature truly cause, on different levels."]]},
  confusions:[["If God causes my choice, am I free?","Thomas: God moves each thing according to its nature, so he moves the will freely. Thomists and Molinists explain this differently; the Church allows both."],["Are angels like ghosts?","No: they are a different kind of creature, never embodied; ghosts would be human souls."]],
  further:[["Baldner, S.E. and Carroll, W.E. (1997) Aquinas on Creation.","Translation and study of Thomas's commentary on the Sentences on creation, with an excellent introduction on science and creation."],["Garrigou-Lagrange, R. (1937) Providence.","Part 1 is doctrinal, part 2 practical: abandonment to providence. Good spiritual reading after this lesson."]]},
 t9a:{cases:[["The busy professional","A lawyer prays only on Sundays because her days are full. Thomas's q.83 a.14: the desire of charity can be continual, and short prayers through the day sustain it."],["The prayer of petition","A student prays to pass an exam. Is that selfish? No: it is right to ask for goods we may rightly desire, and to work for them too."],["Dry prayer","A man feels nothing for months. Devotion is in the will; keep the time (see Questions: dryness)."]],
  allegory:["The two sisters","Martha served, Mary sat at Jesus' feet (Luke 10:38–42). The tradition reads them as the active and contemplative lives. Jesus does not condemn Martha's work; he says Mary chose \"the best part\". The best Christian life lets Mary's listening feed Martha's serving."],
  slow:{title:"ST II-II q.83 a.2: Whether it is becoming to pray",steps:[["Three errors: no providence; everything necessary; providence changeable by prayer.","Each makes prayer useless or misunderstands it."],["Providence disposes not only effects, but by what causes and in what order they come.","The key premise."],["Human acts are among these causes.","We truly cause things by acting."],["\"We pray not that we may change the Divine disposition, but that we may impetrate that which God has disposed.\"","Prayer is a cause God built into his plan."]]},
  confusions:[["Does unanswered prayer mean God ignored it?","Thomas: God gives what is best for salvation; sometimes the answer is something better than what was asked (q.83 a.15 discusses merit and impetration)."]],
  further:[["Garrigou-Lagrange, R. (1947–48) The Three Ages of the Interior Life.","Start with vol. 1, part 1 (the sources of the interior life). Long but foundational."],["Torrell, J.-P. (2003) Saint Thomas Aquinas, vol. 2: Spiritual Master.","Shows that Thomas's theology is itself a spirituality."]]}
});

