Object.assign(EXTRAS,{
 t11:{plain:{version:"The eternal Son became man without ceasing to be God: one person, two natures. Because he is one person, we can say \"God was born\" and \"God died\". His Passion saves by merit, satisfaction, sacrifice and redemption, all flowing from love and obedience, and his Resurrection is the cause and pattern of ours.",question:"Who is Jesus, and how does he save us?",one:"One divine person in two natures; saved by love made obedient to death."},
  hook:"\"M-S-S-R\": Merit, Satisfaction, Sacrifice, Redemption.",
  analogy:{title:"The king who became a citizen",text:"A king who becomes a citizen of his own besieged city, sharing its hunger and fighting on its walls, can speak for the city before himself as king. Christ, God and man, offers in our nature what we could not offer."},
  sort:{title:"Orthodox or heresy?",cats:["Orthodox","Heresy"],items:[["One person in two natures",0],["Two persons morally united (Nestorius)",1],["One nature after the union (Eutyches)",1],["Two wills in harmony",0],["Christ had no human mind (Apollinaris)",1]]}},
 t12:{plain:{version:"Mary is Mother of God because her Son is God. She remained a virgin. She was preserved from original sin by Christ's merits and taken body and soul into heaven. Every Marian teaching is really about Christ.",question:"Why does the Church say these things about Mary?",one:"Every Marian dogma protects a truth about Christ."},
  hook:"\"M-V-I-A\": Mother of God, Virgin, Immaculate, Assumed.",
  analogy:{title:"The moon and the sun",text:"The moon has no light of its own; it reflects the sun. Mary's glory is reflected glory, wholly from Christ."},
  sort:{title:"Which dogma and when?",cats:["431","1854","1950"],items:[["Mother of God",0],["Immaculate Conception",1],["Assumption",2]]}},
 t13:{plain:{version:"Sacraments are signs that Christ uses to give grace. They work by his power, not the minister's holiness, but their fruit depends on our openness. The Eucharist is the greatest because it is Christ himself.",question:"How do sacraments give grace?",one:"Christ acts through signs, as a writer through a pen."},
  hook:"\"Past, Present, Future\": Passion, grace, glory.",
  analogy:{title:"The pipe and the water",text:"Water reaches the house through a pipe, whether silver or lead. Grace reaches us through the sacraments, whether the minister is holy or not."},
  sort:{title:"Matter of which sacrament?",cats:["Baptism","Eucharist","Confirmation","Anointing"],items:[["Water",0],["Wheat bread",1],["Chrism",2],["Oil of the sick",3]]}},
 t14:{plain:{version:"The Church is Christ's body, with him as Head and the Spirit as soul. The one Church of Christ subsists in the Catholic Church, while much that is good exists in other communities. The pope can define doctrine infallibly under strict conditions.",question:"What is the Church and where is it found?",one:"One Church of Christ, subsisting in the Catholic Church; elements outside belong to it by right."},
  hook:"\"One, Holy, Catholic, Apostolic\".",
  analogy:{title:"The vine",text:"Branches grafted firmly onto the vine bear fruit; branches partly cut off still carry some sap. The elements outside the Church's visible bounds are real sap from the same vine (John 15:5)."},
  sort:{title:"Which mark of the Church?",cats:["One","Holy","Catholic","Apostolic"],items:[["One faith, one baptism",0],["Means of sanctification",1],["Universal in time and place",2],["Succession from the apostles",3]]}},
 t15:{plain:{version:"At death each person is judged. Those who die in grace but are not fully purified are purified; heaven is seeing God; hell is the loss of God chosen by unrepentant mortal sin. At the end the body is raised and all is renewed.",question:"What happens after death?",one:"Death, judgment, heaven or hell (with purgatory for some), resurrection."},
  hook:"\"Death, Judgment, Heaven, Hell\": the Four Last Things.",
  analogy:{title:"Gravity of the soul",text:"Thomas: as a stone falls to its place unless held, so the soul goes to its reward or punishment unless delayed by an obstacle."},
  sort:{title:"Which last thing?",cats:["Heaven","Purgatory","Hell"],items:[["Vision of God",0],["Final purification of the saved",1],["Loss of God, chosen",2]]}},
 t16:{plain:{version:"The Church has laws because she is a community with a common good: salvation. The 1983 Code organises them in seven books. Laws are read by their words and purpose, penalties strictly, and the salvation of souls is the supreme law.",question:"Why does the Church have law, and how does it work?",one:"Law serves salvation; read it by text, context and purpose."},
  hook:"\"Salus animarum suprema lex\": the salvation of souls is the supreme law.",
  analogy:{title:"The trellis",text:"A trellis does not make a vine grow, but without it the vine sprawls and rots. Canon law is the Church's trellis."},
  sort:{title:"Which book of the Code?",cats:["General norms","People of God","Sanctifying office","Processes"],items:[["Rules for interpreting laws",0],["Rights of the faithful",1],["Sacrament of marriage",2],["Nullity trials",3]]}}
});
Object.assign(DEPTH,{
 t11:{cases:[["\"Jesus never claimed to be God\"","A sceptic says this. Show John 8:58 (\"before Abraham was made, I am\"), John 10:30 and 20:28, and the synoptic claims to forgive sins (Mark 2:5–7)."],["Christ's suffering and ours","A patient with chronic pain asks whether God understands. Christ suffered in his human nature; Hebrews 4:15 says he can \"have compassion on our infirmities\"."],["Christmas sermon","\"God was born in Bethlehem\" is correct by the communication of idioms; \"the divine nature was born\" is not."]],
  allegory:["The author enters his book","C.S. Lewis's image: if Shakespeare wrote himself into Hamlet as a character, he would be both author and character. The Incarnation is more: the Word becomes truly human, not merely appearing in the story."],
  slow:{title:"ST III q.2 a.2: union in the person",steps:[["Person differs from nature: nature is the essence in the definition; the suppositum is the individual subsisting in it.","Not every individual is identical with its nature: \"this man is not his manhood\"."],["Whatever belongs to a person is united to it in person, whether it belongs to its nature or not.","My hand belongs to me, not to my definition as rational animal."],["If the human nature is not united to the Word in person, it is not united to him at all.","Then there is no Incarnation."],["So the union took place in the person of the Word, not in the nature.","One subject, two natures."]]},
  confusions:[["Did Christ have a human person?","No: a complete human nature, but no human person; the person is the Word."],["Did God change by becoming man?","No: the change is on the side of the human nature (III q.1 a.1 ad 1)."]],
  further:[["White, T.J. (2015) The Incarnate Lord.","Leading contemporary Thomist Christology; advanced but rewarding."],["Anselm, Cur Deus Homo.","The classic on satisfaction; compare with Thomas's q.48."]]},
 t12:{cases:[["A Protestant friend","\"Why call Mary Mother of God?\" Start with Christology: is Jesus God? Then his mother is Mother of God."],["The Rosary and Scripture","Many mysteries are scriptural; praying them is meditating on the Gospel."],["Catholic–Orthodox dialogue","The Orthodox honour Mary as All-Holy and celebrate the Dormition; differences on the Immaculate Conception are partly about how original sin is understood."]],
  allegory:["The ark","The Ark of the Covenant held the tablets, manna and Aaron's rod; Luke's Visitation narrative echoes David's bringing the ark (2 Sam 6). Mary carries the Word, the true Bread and the true Priest."],
  slow:{title:"ST III q.35 a.4: Mother of God",steps:[["A word for a nature in the concrete (\"God\", \"man\") can stand for any person having that nature.","Grammar of predication."],["The union took place in the person.","From q.2."],["Conception and birth are attributed to the person according to the nature in which he is conceived.","The Word was conceived according to his human nature."],["\"Therefore the Blessed Virgin is truly called the Mother of God.\"","Christological necessity."]]},
  confusions:[["Is the Immaculate Conception the same as the virgin birth?","No: it concerns Mary's own conception."],["Did Thomas deny the Immaculate Conception?","He held a different view, before the definition; his principle was kept."]],
  further:[["Ratzinger, J. and von Balthasar, H.U. (2005) Mary: The Church at the Source.","Short and profound."],["Newman, J.H. (1866) Letter to Pusey.","A classic defence of Marian doctrine to an Anglican friend."]]},
 t13:{cases:[["An invalid baptism?","A minister used \"in the name of the Creator, Redeemer and Sanctifier\". The CDF (2008) ruled such formulas invalid: the form must name Father, Son and Holy Spirit."],["The sinful priest","A parishioner learns her parish priest was in grave sin. Her confessions and Masses were valid (q.64 a.5)."],["Children's First Communion","Why teach children about transubstantiation? Because they must know what they receive (CIC c. 913)."]],
  allegory:["The king's seal","A king's seal on wax gives a letter the king's authority, whoever presses it. Christ's sacraments carry his power through the minister's hand."],
  slow:{title:"ST III q.75 a.4: conversion of bread into Christ's body",steps:[["Christ's body is truly in the sacrament.","Premise from q.75 a.1."],["It does not begin to be there by local motion, nor as in a place.","He does not leave heaven."],["So it begins to be there by conversion of the substance of bread into itself.","The only remaining way."],["This change is not natural but entirely supernatural, by God's power alone.","Not a formal change, but a substantial one: transubstantiation."]]},
  confusions:[["Is the Eucharist cannibalism?","No: Christ is present substantially, not in his natural dimensions or as flesh to be torn (q.76)."],["Do sacraments work like magic?","No: their fruit requires faith and disposition; magic claims to control God."]],
  further:[["Heschmeyer, J. The Eucharist Is Really Jesus.","Popular, well-argued defence of the real presence."],["Leeming, B. (1956) Principles of Sacramental Theology.","The best Thomist textbook on sacraments in general."]]},
 t14:{cases:[["\"I'm spiritual, not religious\"","Many South African students say this. Ecclesiology answers: Christ founded a body, not a self-help method; grace comes through the Church's sacraments and communion."],["A scandal in the Church","Abuse scandals wound faith. The Church's holiness comes from Christ and his means of grace; members, including clergy, sin. Justice for victims is an obligation."],["Ecumenical prayer","A Catholic attends an Anglican service. Permitted as ecumenical presence; reception of Communion is governed by c. 844."]],
  allegory:["Noah's ark","The Fathers compared the Church to the ark (1 Pet 3:20). Thomas uses it too (III q.73 a.3). Like the ark, she carries clean and unclean animals; holiness is in her purpose and her Lord."],
  slow:{title:"Lumen Gentium 8: subsistit in",steps:[["Christ established his holy Church as a visible structure.","Not a merely invisible community."],["The society and the Mystical Body are not two realities but one complex reality.","Visible and invisible together."],["This Church subsists in the Catholic Church, governed by Peter's successor and the bishops in communion with him.","The Church of Christ is concretely found there."],["Elements of sanctification and truth outside its visible structure impel toward catholic unity.","Ecumenism grounded in ecclesiology."]]},
  confusions:[["Does \"no salvation outside the Church\" damn all non-Catholics?","No (LG 16; 1949 letter); but it denies that the Church is optional for one who knows her necessity."]],
  further:[["Ratzinger, J. (1996) Called to Communion.","Clear, short ecclesiology."],["Heschmeyer, J. Pope Peter.","Popular defence of the papacy."]]},
 t15:{cases:[["A funeral homily","\"He's in a better place\" is a hope, not a certainty; the Church prays for the dead, entrusting them to God's mercy."],["Near-death experiences","Reports vary and are disputed; theology relies on revelation, not such reports, though they raise interesting questions."],["Fear of hell","A scrupulous student fears hell constantly. Hope rests on God's mercy; hell is chosen by deliberate refusal, not by anxious doubt (P4)."]],
  allegory:["The two cities","Augustine's City of God: two loves make two cities. Eschatology is the final separation and revelation of what each love chose."],
  slow:{title:"ST Suppl. q.99 a.1: eternal punishment",steps:[["Punishment is measured by severity and duration.","Two measures."],["Severity corresponds to the gravity of the fault.","\"As much as she hath glorified herself… so much torment\" (Apoc 18:7)."],["\"The duration of the punishment does not, however, correspond with the duration of the fault.\"","Even human law gives long sentences for brief crimes."],["Duration corresponds to the disposition of the sinner: a will fixed against God remains so.","Hence eternity."]]},
  confusions:[["Is purgatory a place in space?","The Church has not defined that; it is a state of purification."]],
  further:[["Ratzinger, J. (1988) Eschatology.","The best modern Catholic treatment."],["Garrigou-Lagrange, R. (1952) Life Everlasting.","Thomist and spiritual; read the chapters on purgatory and heaven."]]},
 t16:{cases:[["Marriage outside the Church","A Catholic marries in a civil ceremony without dispensation. The marriage is invalid for lack of canonical form (c. 1108); a convalidation can be arranged."],["Mass obligation and illness","A sick person is excused from Sunday Mass: the law does not oblige when observance is gravely burdensome (moral impossibility)."],["Parish finances","Book V requires accountability; a parish council for economic affairs is obligatory (c. 537)."]],
  allegory:["The rules of a game","Rules don't make a match beautiful, but without them there is no game. Canon law serves the life of grace, as rules serve the play."],
  slow:{title:"ST I-II q.96 a.6: acting beside the letter of the law",steps:[["Every law is directed to the common good.","Purpose of law."],["The lawgiver cannot foresee every case.","Laws are general."],["Where observance would harm the common good, the law should not be kept in that case.","The city gates example."],["In non-urgent cases, refer to those in authority to dispense.","Not every inconvenience justifies epikeia."]]},
  confusions:[["Is an annulment a divorce?","No: it declares that a valid marriage never existed."]],
  further:[["Beal, J.P. et al. (eds) (2000) New Commentary on the Code of Canon Law.","The standard English commentary; consult canon by canon."]]}
});

// ---------- Psychology & theology programme: P1 (foundations) completion and P2 (domains) ----------
// Evidence verified on PubMed (25 Oct 2026); Summa quotations checked by script against New Advent.
const ETIO_LEVELS=["Body and brain","Development and learning","Apprehension and passion","Will, habit and moral life","Relationships and society","Spiritual life"];
function ETIO(rows,title){return `<div class="co core"><div class="co-h">${title||"Where it comes from: an integrated causal map"}</div><div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>What contributes</th></tr></thead><tbody>${rows.map((r,i)=>`<tr><td><strong>${ETIO_LEVELS[i]}</strong></td><td>${r}</td></tr>`).join("")}</tbody></table></div><p class="ui muted" style="font-size:.78rem;margin-top:6px">Levels interact; no single level is the whole cause. See P1 lesson 2 for the framework.</p></div>`;}
addTerms([
 {id:"biopsychosocial",t:"Biopsychosocial-spiritual model",la:"—",d:"Psychology",b:"Understanding illness and suffering through biological, psychological, social and spiritual factors together.",p:"Engel's (1977) biopsychosocial model, extended by Sulmasy (2002) to include the person's relationship with transcendence; a framework for assessment and care rather than a single theory.",ex:"Assessing a depressed client's sleep and thyroid, thinking patterns, unemployment and loss of faith.",where:"Engel (1977); Sulmasy (2002)",ccc:"",conf:"Reductionism (only brain, only mind, only society).",rel:["integration","mental-disorder"],lesson:"p1b",alias:["biopsychosocial","biopsychosocial-spiritual"]},
 {id:"wounds-nature",t:"Wounds of nature",la:"vulnera naturae",d:"Anthropology",b:"Four lasting effects of original sin in the soul's powers: ignorance, malice, weakness and concupiscence.",p:"The loss of original justice leaves reason without its order to truth (ignorance), the will without its order to good (malice), the irascible without its order to the arduous (weakness), and the concupiscible without its order to the delectable moderated by reason (concupiscence) (ST I-II q.85 a.3); personal sins deepen them.",ex:"Knowing the good and finding it hard to do it.",where:"ST I-II q.85 a.3",ccc:"405, 1707",conf:"Total depravity.",rel:["passions","imputability"],lesson:"p1b",alias:["wounds of nature","four wounds"]},
 {id:"cogitative",t:"Cogitative power",la:"vis cogitativa",d:"Anthropology",b:"The interior sense by which we perceive the meaning of particular things for us (safe, threatening, mine), guided by reason.",p:"In animals the estimative power perceives intentions not given by the external senses (the sheep perceives the wolf as dangerous); in man it is the cogitative power, which compares particular intentions and is influenced by reason (ST I q.78 a.4).",ex:"Instantly sensing that a room is hostile.",where:"ST I q.78 a.4",ccc:"",conf:"The intellect, which grasps universals.",rel:["phantasm","passions"],lesson:"p7",alias:["cogitative power","estimative power","interior senses"]},
 {id:"attachment",t:"Attachment",la:"—",d:"Psychology",b:"The enduring emotional bond between a child and caregiver that shapes later relationships.",p:"Bowlby's theory of an evolved system seeking proximity to a protective figure; patterns (secure, avoidant, resistant, disorganised) assessed by Ainsworth's Strange Situation; insecure and especially disorganised attachment modestly predict later problems (Fearon et al., 2010).",ex:"A toddler using a parent as a secure base to explore.",where:"—",ccc:"2223",conf:"Dependency.",rel:["habitus"],lesson:"p5",alias:["attachment","attachment theory","secure attachment"]},
 {id:"heritability",t:"Heritability",la:"—",d:"Psychology",b:"The share of differences between people in a trait that is linked to genetic differences, in a given population.",p:"A population statistic (h²), not a statement about how much of one person's trait is genetic; about .40 for personality (Vukasović and Bratko, 2015), .37 for major depression (Sullivan et al., 2000).",ex:"Twin studies comparing identical and fraternal twins.",where:"—",ccc:"",conf:"Genetic determinism.",rel:["temperament"],lesson:"p10",alias:["heritability","heritable"]},
 {id:"temperament",t:"Temperament",la:"complexio",d:"Psychology",b:"Inborn tendencies of emotional reactivity and self-regulation.",p:"Early-appearing, biologically based differences in reactivity and regulation (e.g. behavioural inhibition); the medieval complexio of the body that disposes to certain passions (ST I-II q.17 a.7; q.46 a.5).",ex:"A child who is easily startled and slow to warm up.",where:"ST I-II q.17 a.7",ccc:"",conf:"Character, which is formed by choices.",rel:["heritability","virtue"],lesson:"p10",alias:["temperament","temperaments"]},
 {id:"heuristic",t:"Heuristic and bias",la:"—",d:"Psychology",b:"A mental shortcut that usually works but leads to predictable errors.",p:"Tversky and Kahneman (1974): representativeness, availability and anchoring yield systematic biases in judgment under uncertainty.",ex:"Judging plane travel more dangerous than driving after news of a crash (availability).",where:"Tversky and Kahneman (1974)",ccc:"",conf:"Irrationality as such.",rel:["prudence"],lesson:"p7",alias:["heuristic","heuristics","cognitive bias","biases"]},
 {id:"emotion-regulation",t:"Emotion regulation",la:"—",d:"Psychology",b:"The ways people influence which emotions they have, when, and how they experience and express them.",p:"Gross's process model: situation selection and modification, attentional deployment, cognitive change (reappraisal), response modulation; reappraisal of the stimulus and perspective-taking show the strongest effects (Webb et al., 2012).",ex:"Reframing a rejection letter as feedback.",where:"Webb, Miles and Sheeran (2012)",ccc:"",conf:"Suppression only.",rel:["passions"],lesson:"p8",alias:["emotion regulation","reappraisal"]},
 {id:"conformity",t:"Conformity and obedience",la:"—",d:"Psychology",b:"Changing behaviour to match a group or to follow an authority.",p:"Asch's line studies (conformity) and Milgram's obedience studies; a partial replication found obedience rates only slightly lower than Milgram's (Burger, 2009).",ex:"Laughing at a cruel joke because everyone else does.",where:"Burger (2009)",ccc:"1868–1869 (structures of sin)",conf:"Prudent docility.",rel:["fortitude"],lesson:"p11",alias:["conformity","obedience studies","Milgram"]},
 {id:"neuroplasticity",t:"Neuroplasticity",la:"—",d:"Neuroscience",b:"The brain's capacity to change its structure and function with experience and practice.",p:"Experience-dependent changes in synapses and networks across the lifespan; the biological correlate of learning and habit.",ex:"Practice strengthening the circuits for a skill.",where:"—",ccc:"",conf:"The claim that the brain is fixed after childhood.",rel:["habitus"],lesson:"p6",alias:["neuroplasticity","plasticity"]}
]);
