Object.assign(LESSONS,{
p1b:{id:"p1b",mod:"p1",ord:2,code:"P1 · Lesson 2",title:"Where psychological suffering comes from: a Thomistic map of causes",
 outcomes:["Explain the biopsychosocial model (Engel, 1977) and its spiritual extension (Sulmasy, 2002).","Use ST I-II q.17 a.7 to explain why bodily dispositions affect the passions and are not directly under reason's command.","Explain the four wounds of nature (I-II q.85 a.3) and how personal sin deepens them.","Distinguish illness, wound, vice, sin and spiritual affliction, including the Church's caution about the demonic (CCC 1673).","Build a six-level causal map for any presenting problem and use it to plan care."],
 prereq:["biopsychosocial","wounds-nature","passions","imputability","habitus"],
 reading:{primary:[["ST I-II q.17 a.7 (is the sensitive appetite commanded?)",NA("2017.htm#article7")],["ST I-II q.85 a.3 (the wounds of nature)",NA("2085.htm#article3")],["ST I-II q.77 a.3 (sins of weakness)",NA("2077.htm#article3")],["CCC 1673 (illness and possession)",'https://www.vatican.va/archive/ENG0015/__P4Z.HTM']],
  secondary:["Engel, G.L. (1977) 'The need for a new medical model: a challenge for biomedicine', Science, 196(4286), pp. 129–136. doi:10.1126/science.847460.","Sulmasy, D.P. (2002) 'A biopsychosocial-spiritual model for the care of patients at the end of life', The Gerontologist, 42(Suppl 3), pp. 24–33. doi:10.1093/geront/42.suppl_3.24.","Hughes, K. et al. (2017) 'The effect of multiple adverse childhood experiences on health: a systematic review and meta-analysis', Lancet Public Health, 2(8), pp. e356–e366. doi:10.1016/S2468-2667(17)30118-4.","Vitz, P.C., Nordling, W.J. and Titus, C.S. (eds) (2020) A Catholic Christian Meta-Model of the Person."]},
 scripture:[["John 9:1–3, \"neither hath this man sinned, nor his parents\"",null],["John 5:1–14, illness, healing and \"sin no more\"","j5-1"]],
 sections:[
 {id:"s1",h:"1. Why a single-cause explanation fails",html:`<p>Clients, families and pastors often ask one question: <em>\"Is it chemical, is it my upbringing, or is it spiritual?\"</em> The honest answer is nearly always: several, interacting.</p>
<p><strong>Engel's challenge.</strong> In 1977 the psychiatrist George Engel argued that the biomedical model \"leaves no room within its framework for the social, psychological, and behavioral dimensions of illness\" and proposed the <strong>biopsychosocial model</strong> (Engel, 1977, doi:10.1126/science.847460). Daniel Sulmasy extended it: persons are \"beings-in-relationship\", and holistic care must address \"the totality of the patient's relational existence—physical, psychological, social, and spiritual\" (Sulmasy, 2002, doi:10.1093/geront/42.suppl_3.24).</p>
<div class="co core"><div class="co-h">[Core] What the evidence shows about multiple causes</div><ul>
<li><strong>Genes matter, but as probabilities.</strong> Heritability of major depression is about 37% (twin studies; Sullivan, Neale and Kendler, 2000, doi:10.1176/appi.ajp.157.10.1552). Yet single \"depression genes\" have not held up: in samples of 62,138 to 443,264, none of 18 popular candidate genes or gene-environment interactions was supported (Border et al., 2019, doi:10.1176/appi.ajp.2018.18070881). Genetic risk is spread across thousands of small effects.</li>
<li><strong>Early adversity matters.</strong> Across 37 studies (N = 253,719), adults with four or more adverse childhood experiences had higher odds of every outcome studied: over three to six times for mental ill-health and problematic alcohol use, over seven times for problematic drug use and violence (Hughes et al., 2017, doi:10.1016/S2468-2667(17)30118-4). These are associations, not certainties: many people with high ACE scores flourish.</li>
<li><strong>Society matters.</strong> In South Africa, lifetime prevalence of any common mental disorder was 30.3% (SASH study, N = 4,351; Herman et al., 2009), with anxiety disorders the most common.</li></ul></div>`,
  check:Q("What did Border et al. (2019) find about popular candidate genes for depression?",[["They explained most cases","No."],["No support in very large samples","Correct."],["They caused depression only after trauma","The interaction hypotheses were also unsupported."]],1)},
 {id:"s2",h:"2. Thomas's two key texts: the body and the wounds",html:`<p><strong>(a) The body's part.</strong> Thomas asks whether the sensitive appetite is subject to reason's command, and answers: partly. Reason can direct it through imagination, but:</p>
<div class="quote">"Consequently the act of the sensitive appetite depends not only on the appetitive power, but also on the disposition of the body."<span class="src">ST I-II q.17 a.7</span></div>
<p>And \"condition or disposition of the body is not subject to the command of reason\" (same article). This is a medieval foundation for biological psychiatry: some emotional states arise from bodily dispositions that willpower alone cannot change. One may need medicine, sleep, food or rest, not only resolve.</p>
<p><strong>(b) The wounds of nature.</strong> Original sin removed original justice, so \"all the powers of the soul are left, as it were, destitute of their proper order\". Hence four wounds (I-II q.85 a.3):</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Power</th><th>Wound</th><th>Virtue that heals</th><th>Modern parallel (heuristic)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Reason</td><td>Ignorance</td><td>Prudence</td><td>cognitive distortions, biases</td></tr>
<tr><td>Will</td><td>Malice</td><td>Justice</td><td>self-centredness, low prosociality</td></tr>
<tr><td>Irascible</td><td>Weakness</td><td>Fortitude</td><td>avoidance, low distress tolerance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Concupiscible</td><td>Concupiscence</td><td>Temperance</td><td>impulsivity, compulsive reward-seeking</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Crucially, Thomas adds that these wounds \"are also the result of other sins, in so far as, through sin, the reason is obscured, especially in practical matters, the will hardened to evil, good actions become more difficult and concupiscence more impetuous\" (q.85 a.3). So personal history, including others' sins against us and our own, deepens the wounds.</p>`,
  check:Q("According to I-II q.17 a.7, is the disposition of the body subject to reason's command?",[["Yes, entirely","No."],["No, which is why passions partly escape direct control","Correct."],["Only in saints","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s3",h:"3. Six levels of cause",html:`<p>Combining the biopsychosocial-spiritual model with Thomas's anthropology gives six levels. Every later P3 lesson uses this map.</p>
<ol><li><strong>Body and brain</strong>: genes, temperament (<em>complexio</em>), neurochemistry, illness, sleep, nutrition, substances (I-II q.17 a.7).</li>
<li><strong>Development and learning</strong>: attachment, upbringing, trauma, conditioning, the habits we were given before we could choose (I-II qq.49–54).</li>
<li><strong>Apprehension and passion</strong>: how things are perceived (cogitative power, imagination) and the passions that follow (I q.78 a.4; I-II q.22–48). Cognitive therapy works here.</li>
<li><strong>Will, habit and moral life</strong>: choices, virtues and vices, sin and its effects, culpability (I-II q.6, q.77, q.85).</li>
<li><strong>Relationships and society</strong>: family, poverty, violence, discrimination, \"structures of sin\" (Sollicitudo Rei Socialis 36), scandal (II-II q.43).</li>
<li><strong>Spiritual life</strong>: relationship with God, grace, prayer, spiritual struggle, and, rarely, preternatural affliction.</li></ol>
<div class="co analogy"><div class="co-h">Analogy · The garden</div><p>A struggling plant may suffer from poor soil (body), early damage (development), wrong light (perception), neglect or bad pruning (habit and choice), crowding by other plants (society), and lack of rain (spiritual). A good gardener checks all six before deciding what to do.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("At which level does cognitive therapy chiefly work?",[["Body and brain","Medication works there."],["Apprehension and passion","Correct: it changes how things are perceived and judged."],["Spiritual life","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s4",h:"4. Illness, wound, vice, sin, and spiritual affliction",html:`<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Illness</th><th>Wound</th><th>Vice</th><th>Sin</th><th>Spiritual affliction</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Origin</td><td>dysfunction (often bodily)</td><td>suffered harm, original or personal</td><td>repeated choices</td><td>a free act</td><td>temptation, desolation; rarely the demonic</td></tr>
<tr><td>Responsibility</td><td>none for having it</td><td>none for receiving it</td><td>for forming it</td><td>full if knowing and free</td><td>for consent only</td></tr>
<tr><td>Remedy</td><td>treatment</td><td>healing, safety, time, grace</td><td>contrary acts, virtue</td><td>repentance, confession</td><td>prayer, direction, sacraments</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>The Church's caution about the demonic.</strong> \"Illness, especially psychological illness, is a very different matter; treating this is the concern of medical science. Therefore, before an exorcism is performed, it is important to ascertain that one is dealing with the presence of the Evil One, and not an illness\" (CCC 1673). The rite of exorcism (revised 1999) requires such discernment, often with medical and psychological consultation.</p>
<div class="co warn"><div class="co-h">[Warn] Two harmful errors</div><ul><li><strong>Spiritualising illness</strong>: treating psychosis or depression as demonic or as lack of faith. It delays treatment and adds shame.</li><li><strong>Medicalising everything</strong>: treating all moral struggle as symptom. It removes agency and hope of conversion.</li></ul></div>`,
  check:Q("What does CCC 1673 require before an exorcism?",[["Nothing: the priest decides","No."],["Ascertaining that it is not an illness","Correct."],["A psychiatric diagnosis of possession","No such diagnosis exists."]],1)},
 {id:"s5",h:"5. From map to care plan",html:`<p>For each level, a different helper may take the lead, but all should know the whole map.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Typical interventions</th><th>Typical helper</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Body</td><td>medical assessment, medication, sleep, exercise, nutrition</td><td>GP, psychiatrist</td></tr>
<tr><td>Development</td><td>trauma-focused therapy, attachment-informed therapy, safety</td><td>psychologist</td></tr>
<tr><td>Apprehension</td><td>CBT, cognitive restructuring, psychoeducation</td><td>psychologist, counsellor</td></tr>
<tr><td>Will and habit</td><td>behavioural activation, motivational interviewing, virtue practice, confession</td><td>psychologist; confessor</td></tr>
<tr><td>Society</td><td>social support, work, housing, safety, community</td><td>social worker, parish, family</td></tr>
<tr><td>Spiritual</td><td>prayer, sacraments, spiritual direction, reframing suffering</td><td>priest, spiritual director</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="co clinical"><div class="co-h">Clinical practicality (HPCSA)</div><p>A registered psychologist must work within the scope of their profession and competence. Addressing the spiritual level means assessing and respecting it, collaborating with clergy when the client wishes, and not imposing one's own beliefs.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Who typically leads care at the \"will and habit\" level?",[["A radiologist","No."],["A psychologist, and for sin, a confessor","Correct."],["Nobody","No."]],1)}
 ],
 quiz:[
  Q("Engel proposed the biopsychosocial model in…",[["Science, 1977","Correct."],["DSM-III, 1980","No."],["Lumen Gentium","No."],["1990","No."]],0,"doi:10.1126/science.847460",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["biopsychosocial"],diff:1}),
  Q("Sulmasy's extension adds which dimension?",[["Economic","No."],["Spiritual","Correct."],["Legal","No."],["Genetic","Already in 'bio'."]],1,"doi:10.1093/geront/42.suppl_3.24",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["biopsychosocial"],diff:1}),
  Q("Twin-study heritability of major depression is about…",[["5%","No."],["37%","Correct."],["90%","No."],["100%","No."]],1,"doi:10.1176/appi.ajp.157.10.1552",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["heritability"],diff:2}),
  Q("Adults with four or more ACEs had the strongest associations with…",[["obesity","Weak (OR < 2)."],["problematic drug use and violence","Correct: ORs above 7."],["diabetes","Weak."],["none","No."]],1,"Hughes et al. (2017)",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:2}),
  Q("Which wound corresponds to the irascible appetite?",[["Ignorance","Reason."],["Weakness","Correct."],["Malice","Will."],["Concupiscence","Concupiscible."]],1,"ST I-II q.85 a.3",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["wounds-nature"],diff:1}),
  Q("Thomas says personal sins…",[["have no effect on the wounds","No."],["deepen the wounds: reason obscured, will hardened, good harder, concupiscence more impetuous","Correct: q.85 a.3."],["heal the wounds","No."],["only affect the body","No."]],1,"ST I-II q.85 a.3",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["wounds-nature"],diff:2}),
  Q("Lifetime prevalence of any common mental disorder in the SASH study was…",[["3%","No."],["30.3%","Correct."],["60%","No."],["Unknown","No."]],1,"Herman et al. (2009)",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:1}),
  Q("Before an exorcism, CCC 1673 requires…",[["ascertaining it is not an illness","Correct."],["a vote of the parish","No."],["three witnesses","No."],["nothing","No."]],0,"CCC 1673",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:1}),
  Q("A man knows he should exercise but cannot get out of bed for weeks, has lost appetite and pleasure. The first level to assess is…",[["only the spiritual","No."],["body and brain (possible depression or illness), then the others","Correct."],["only his will","Moralising."],["society only","No."]],1,"P1 lesson 2",{bloom:"Apply",terms:["biopsychosocial"],diff:2}),
  Q("Which is an error the lesson warns against?",[["Integrating all six levels","That is the aim."],["Spiritualising illness","Correct."],["Referring to a GP","Good practice."],["Respecting faith","Good practice."]],1,"P1 lesson 2",{bloom:"Evaluate",terms:[],diff:1})
 ],
 construct:{q:"Whether the act of the sensitive appetite is commanded by reason?",ref:"ST I-II q.17 a.7",
  model:{obj:"It seems so entirely: the sensitive appetite obeys reason, as the Philosopher says, so every passion is under our command.",sc:"\"I see another law in my members, fighting against the law of my mind\" (Rom 7:23).",resp:"An act is commanded insofar as it is in our power. The sensitive appetite uses a bodily organ, so its act depends not only on the appetitive power but on the disposition of the body. Insofar as it follows the apprehension of imagination, which reason can direct, it is subject to reason. But the disposition of the body is not subject to reason's command; and passions sometimes arise suddenly before reason can judge. So the sensitive appetite is subject to reason, but not entirely.",rep:"Reason rules the passions politically, not despotically: they have something of their own, including bodily dispositions, which can resist reason."},
  rubric:["Objection: the appetite obeys reason entirely.","Sed contra: Romans 7:23.","Respondeo: depends on the disposition of the body.","Respondeo: partly subject via imagination; body not commanded.","Reply: political, not despotic, rule."]},
 drill:{title:"Order five of the six levels, from most bodily to most spiritual",parts:["Body and brain","Development and learning","Apprehension and passion","Will, habit and moral life","Spiritual life"]},
 connections:`<p><strong>Every P3 lesson</strong> begins with a causal map using these levels.</p><p><strong>KRM 320.</strong> Criminology's integrated theories (biosocial, life-course) are close cousins of this map; Thomas adds the will and the spiritual life explicitly.</p><p><strong>RES 320.</strong> Note the difference between association and cause in the ACE literature.</p>`},

p5:{id:"p5",ord:1,code:"P2 · Lesson 1",title:"Developmental psychology: attachment, stages and the formation of virtue",
 outcomes:["Summarise attachment theory (Bowlby, Ainsworth) and the evidence linking attachment to later problems (Fearon et al., 2010).","Compare Piaget, Vygotsky and Erikson on development.","Explain Thomas's view of virtue formation by habituation and the role of parents and teachers.","Relate the age of reason (CIC c. 97 §2) to developmental psychology.","Apply this to parenting, schooling and the formation of conscience in South Africa."],
 prereq:["attachment","habitus","virtue","conscience"],
 reading:{primary:[["ST I-II q.51 aa.2–3 (habits caused by acts)",NA("2051.htm#article2")],["ST I q.117 a.1 (can one man teach another?)",NA("1117.htm#article1")],["CCC 2221–2231 (duties of parents)",'https://www.vatican.va/archive/ENG0015/__P7Y.HTM']],
  secondary:["Fearon, R.P. et al. (2010) 'The significance of insecure attachment and disorganization in the development of children's externalizing behavior: a meta-analytic study', Child Development, 81(2), pp. 435–456. doi:10.1111/j.1467-8624.2009.01405.x.","Bowlby, J. (1969) Attachment and Loss, vol. 1: Attachment. London: Hogarth.","Berk, L.E. (2018) Development Through the Lifespan. 7th edn. Boston: Pearson.","Aristotle, Nicomachean Ethics II.1–4."]},
 scripture:[["Luke 2:52, Jesus advanced in wisdom and age",null],["John 1:35–39, \"Come and see\": learning by being with","j1-35"]],
 sections:[
 {id:"s1",h:"1. Attachment: the first school of love",html:`<p><strong>Bowlby</strong> proposed that infants have an evolved behavioural system that seeks proximity to a protective caregiver, forming \"internal working models\" of self and others. <strong>Ainsworth's</strong> Strange Situation classifies attachment as secure, avoidant, resistant (ambivalent), or (Main and Solomon) disorganised.</p>
<div class="co core"><div class="co-h">[Core] Evidence</div><p>Across 69 samples (N = 5,947), insecure attachment was associated with children's externalising problems, d = 0.31; disorganisation showed the largest risk (d = 0.34), with weaker effects for avoidance (d = 0.12) and resistance (d = 0.11, non-significant) (Fearon et al., 2010, doi:10.1111/j.1467-8624.2009.01405.x). Effects are moderate: attachment is a risk factor, not a destiny.</p></div>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>Thomas holds that \"knowledge is a cause of love\" (I-II q.27 a.2) and that love follows from likeness (q.27 a.3). A child first learns what love is by being loved: the parent's reliable care becomes the child's first image of goodness, and later of God (CCC 239, on the image of fatherhood and motherhood). Secure attachment is, in Thomist terms, a well-ordered first experience of the good.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Which attachment pattern carried the greatest risk for externalising problems in Fearon et al. (2010)?",[["Avoidant","Weaker."],["Disorganised","Correct."],["Secure","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s2",h:"2. Stage theories: Piaget, Vygotsky, Erikson",html:`<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Theorist</th><th>Core idea</th><th>Strength</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Piaget</td><td>Cognition develops through stages (sensorimotor, preoperational, concrete operational, formal operational) by assimilation and accommodation</td><td>Children as active knowers</td><td>Underestimates early competence; stages less uniform than claimed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vygotsky</td><td>Learning is social; the zone of proximal development; scaffolding</td><td>Role of teachers and culture</td><td>Less specific on mechanisms</td></tr>
<tr><td>Erikson</td><td>Eight psychosocial crises (trust vs mistrust … integrity vs despair)</td><td>Whole lifespan; identity</td><td>Hard to test empirically</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>Thomas's account of teaching anticipates Vygotsky: the teacher is like a physician who \"strengthens nature, and employs food and medicine, of which nature makes use\" (I q.117 a.1). The learner's own mind is the principal cause; the teacher assists by presenting examples and steps. This is scaffolding.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Thomas compares the teacher to…",[["a judge","No."],["a physician who strengthens nature","Correct: I q.117 a.1."],["a soldier","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s3",h:"3. Habituation: how virtue is formed",html:`<p>Aristotle: \"we become just by doing just acts\" (Ethics II.1). Thomas explains why: habits are caused by acts because in human acts there is both an active and a passive principle; reason, acting repeatedly on the appetite, gradually disposes it (I-II q.51 a.2).</p>
<p>And not usually by one act: just as fire \"cannot at once overcome the combustible\" but \"gradually expels contrary dispositions\", reason \"cannot entirely overcome the appetitive power in one act\" (q.51 a.3). Virtue takes many acts, which is why childhood formation matters so much.</p>
<div class="co apply"><div class="co-h">[Apply] Formation in practice</div><ol><li><strong>Early years:</strong> warmth, routine, consistent limits: the passions are trained before reasons are understood.</li><li><strong>Middle childhood:</strong> reasons given with rules; chores and responsibility (justice, fortitude).</li><li><strong>Adolescence:</strong> guided freedom; mentors; the passage from obeying rules to owning the good.</li></ol></div>`,
  check:Q("Why can't a virtue usually be formed by a single act, for Thomas?",[["Because virtue is innate","No."],["Because reason cannot overcome the appetite's varied inclinations in one act","Correct: q.51 a.3."],["Because grace forbids it","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s4",h:"4. Moral development and the age of reason",html:`<p><strong>Kohlberg</strong> described stages of moral reasoning (pre-conventional, conventional, post-conventional), criticised by Gilligan for neglecting care, and by others for focusing on reasoning rather than action and emotion. Contemporary research (e.g. Haidt) stresses fast intuitions, with reasoning often following.</p>
<p><strong>Canon law</strong> presumes the use of reason at seven (c. 97 §2), when children begin to be bound by merely ecclesiastical laws (c. 11) and usually make their first confession and Communion. Developmental psychology agrees that by around seven most children grasp rules, intentions and guilt in a basic way, though moral understanding continues to grow.</p>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>Haidt's intuitions correspond roughly to the passions and the cogitative power; reasoning to the intellect. Thomas would agree that well-trained passions incline us rightly before argument, and add that reason must still judge them.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Canon law presumes the use of reason at age…",[["7","Correct: c. 97 §2."],["12","No."],["18","Majority."]],0)},
 {id:"s5",h:"5. Wounds in development, and their healing",html:`${ETIO(["temperament, prenatal and early health, disability","attachment, parenting style, trauma, schooling, peers","the child's interpretations of events (\"it's my fault\")","the child's emerging choices; the sins of others against the child","poverty, violence, absent fathers, migrant labour, school quality (SA)","faith practised (or not) in the home; experience of God as father"],"Where developmental difficulties come from")}
<div class="co clinical"><div class="co-h">Clinical practicality</div><p>Attachment-informed interventions (e.g. video-feedback parenting programmes) can improve caregiver sensitivity. In South Africa, the Thula Sana home-visiting programme is an example studied locally. <span class="chip verify">verify outcomes before citing</span> Grace builds on nature: secure, loving relationships are a natural foundation for the life of grace.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Which is a social-level factor in South African child development?",[["Temperament","Body."],["Absent fathers due to migrant labour","Correct."],["Interpretations of events","Apprehension."]],1)}
 ],
 quiz:[
  Q("The Strange Situation was developed by…",[["Piaget","No."],["Ainsworth","Correct."],["Erikson","No."],["Skinner","No."]],1,"Attachment",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["attachment"],diff:1}),
  Q("Insecure attachment and externalising problems: overall d ≈",[["0.03","No."],["0.31","Correct."],["1.5","No."],["3.0","No."]],1,"Fearon et al. (2010)",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["attachment"],diff:2}),
  Q("The zone of proximal development is associated with…",[["Vygotsky","Correct."],["Piaget","No."],["Bowlby","No."],["Kohlberg","No."]],0,"Vygotsky",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:1}),
  Q("Erikson's first stage is…",[["identity vs role confusion","Adolescence."],["trust vs mistrust","Correct."],["integrity vs despair","Old age."],["industry vs inferiority","School age."]],1,"Erikson",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:1}),
  Q("For Thomas, habits are caused by acts because…",[["acts are physical","No."],["in human acts there is an active and a passive principle","Correct: q.51 a.2."],["God wills it arbitrarily","No."],["habits are innate","No."]],1,"ST I-II q.51 a.2",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["habitus"],diff:2}),
  Q("Gilligan criticised Kohlberg for neglecting…",[["reasoning","No."],["care and relationships","Correct."],["stages","No."],["culture","Partly, but care was her focus."]],1,"Kohlberg",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:2}),
  Q("Thomas's account of teaching is closest to…",[["passive transmission","No."],["scaffolding: the teacher helps the learner's own reason","Correct."],["rote learning","No."],["innate ideas","No."]],1,"ST I q.117 a.1",{bloom:"Analyse",terms:[],diff:2}),
  Q("\"Knowledge is a cause of love\" is from…",[["I-II q.27 a.2","Correct."],["I q.2 a.3","No."],["III q.75","No."],["II-II q.83","No."]],0,"ST I-II q.27 a.2",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:2}),
  Q("Attachment research supports saying that early insecurity…",[["determines adult life","No."],["is a moderate risk factor, not destiny","Correct."],["has no effect","No."],["is caused only by genes","No."]],1,"Fearon et al. (2010)",{bloom:"Evaluate",terms:["attachment"],diff:2}),
  Q("Children are bound by merely ecclesiastical laws from…",[["birth","No."],["seven, with use of reason","Correct: c. 11."],["fourteen always","Only some laws."],["eighteen","No."]],1,"CIC c. 11",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:1})
 ],
 construct:{q:"Whether one man can teach another?",ref:"ST I q.117 a.1",
  model:{obj:"It seems not: Augustine says only God teaches inwardly; the teacher's words only prompt the learner.",sc:"\"I am appointed a preacher… a doctor of the Gentiles\" (2 Tim 1:11).",resp:"Knowledge is acquired both from an interior principle (the light of the agent intellect) and from an exterior principle. As in healing, where nature is the principal cause and the physician strengthens nature and supplies food and medicine, so in learning the learner's reason is the principal cause and the teacher assists by presenting principles, examples and steps that lead from the known to the unknown. So one man can teach another, as an assisting cause.",rep:"God alone teaches as the giver of the interior light; the human teacher teaches as assisting the learner's own reason."},
  rubric:["Objection cites Augustine's De magistro.","Sed contra: Paul as teacher.","Respondeo: interior and exterior principles of knowledge.","Respondeo: the physician analogy.","Reply: God gives the light; the teacher assists."]},
 drill:{title:"Order Erikson's first five stages",parts:["Trust vs mistrust","Autonomy vs shame and doubt","Initiative vs guilt","Industry vs inferiority","Identity vs role confusion"]},
 connections:`<p><strong>SLK 320.</strong> Critical psychology's critique of universal stage models fits here: Thomas would agree that formation depends on culture and community, while insisting on a common human nature.</p><p><strong>Forward.</strong> P3 lessons on trauma and personality draw on attachment.</p>`},

p6:{id:"p6",mod:"p5",ord:2,code:"P2 · Lesson 2",title:"Biological psychology and neuroscience: brain, soul and freedom",
 outcomes:["Explain the relation of brain and mind in hylomorphism, avoiding both materialism and Cartesian dualism.","Interpret Libet's readiness-potential findings and Schurger's reinterpretation.","Explain neuroplasticity as the bodily correlate of habit.","Use I q.84 a.7 and I-II q.17 a.7 to interpret neuroscience theologically.","Evaluate claims that neuroscience disproves free will or the soul."],
 prereq:["soul-form","phantasm","neuroplasticity","free-choice"],
 reading:{primary:[["ST I q.75 a.2; q.76 a.1 (soul subsists; form of the body)",NA("1075.htm#article2")],["ST I q.84 a.7 (understanding needs phantasms)",NA("1084.htm#article7")]],
  secondary:["Libet, B. et al. (1983) 'Time of conscious intention to act in relation to onset of cerebral activity (readiness-potential)', Brain, 106(3), pp. 623–642. doi:10.1093/brain/106.3.623.","Schurger, A., Sitt, J.D. and Dehaene, S. (2012) 'An accumulator model for spontaneous neural activity prior to self-initiated movement', PNAS, 109(42), pp. E2904–E2913. doi:10.1073/pnas.1210467109.","Feser, E. (2024) Immortal Souls. Editiones Scholasticae.","Bennett, M.R. and Hacker, P.M.S. (2003) Philosophical Foundations of Neuroscience. Oxford: Blackwell."]},
 scripture:[["Genesis 2:7, dust and breath",null]],
 sections:[
 {id:"s1",h:"1. Three pictures of mind and brain",html:`<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>View</th><th>Claim</th><th>Problem</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Materialism (identity theory, eliminativism)</td><td>The mind is the brain's activity, nothing more</td><td>Struggles with meaning, universals, reasoning's normativity, and the unity of experience</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cartesian dualism</td><td>Mind and body are two substances that interact</td><td>Interaction problem; makes the body a machine; contradicts the unity of the person</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hylomorphism (Aristotle, Thomas)</td><td>The soul is the form of the body: one substance, the living human being</td><td>Requires a richer metaphysics than physics alone</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>For Thomas, sensing, imagining, remembering and feeling are acts of the whole person through bodily organs, so neuroscience studies them directly. Understanding universals and willing are not acts of an organ (I q.75 a.2), though in this life they depend on bodily powers for their materials (phantasms) (I q.84 a.7).</p>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">The mereological fallacy</div><p>Bennett and Hacker (2003) argue that saying \"the brain decides\" or \"the brain sees\" attributes to a part what belongs to the whole person. Thomas would agree: \"it is one and the same man who is conscious both that he understands, and that he senses\" (I q.76 a.1).</p></div>`,
  check:Q("For Thomas, which acts are not acts of a bodily organ?",[["Sensing and imagining","These use organs."],["Understanding universals and willing","Correct."],["Digestion","Bodily."]],1)},
 {id:"s2",h:"2. Does neuroscience disprove free will? Libet and after",html:`<p><strong>Libet (1983)</strong>: brain activity (the readiness potential) began several hundred milliseconds before subjects reported the conscious intention to flex a finger (\"by an average of about 350 ms, and by a minimum of about 150 ms\"; doi:10.1093/brain/106.3.623). Some concluded that the brain decides before \"we\" do.</p>
<p><strong>Schurger et al. (2012)</strong> proposed that the readiness potential may reflect spontaneous fluctuations in neural activity that accumulate toward a threshold, not a decision already made: \"when the imperative to produce a movement is weak, the precise moment at which the decision threshold is crossed… is largely determined by spontaneous subthreshold fluctuations\" (doi:10.1073/pnas.1210467109).</p>
<div class="co stance"><div class="co-h">[Stance] What a Thomist says</div><ol><li>Libet's task (an arbitrary finger flick with no reasons at stake) is not the paradigm of free choice, which is deliberation among goods (I q.83 a.1).</li><li>Free choice does not require that no bodily process precede it: the will acts through a body, and dispositions prepare action.</li><li>Libet himself held that consciousness could \"veto\" an initiated action.</li></ol><p>Conclusion: the experiments are interesting, but do not refute free choice as Thomas defines it.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Schurger et al. (2012) interpret the readiness potential as possibly reflecting…",[["a decision already made","Libet's popular reading."],["spontaneous neural fluctuations accumulating toward a threshold","Correct."],["the soul leaving the body","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s3",h:"3. Neuroplasticity and habit",html:`<p>Repeated experience and practice change synapses and networks: <strong>neuroplasticity</strong>. Skills, fears and addictions all have neural correlates that strengthen with use and weaken with disuse.</p>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>Thomas says habits are caused by repeated acts because the appetitive power is gradually disposed by reason (I-II q.51 aa.2–3). Since the sensitive powers use bodily organs, habits of the sensitive appetite (e.g. temperance, courage, or their opposites) have bodily correlates. Neuroplasticity is the body's side of habit formation. It also explains why vices are hard to break and virtues stable: both are written into the body as well as the soul.</p></div>
<div class="co apply"><div class="co-h">[Apply]</div><p>When a client says \"I can't change; my brain is wired this way\", neuroplasticity and Thomas agree: wiring changes with repeated contrary acts, slowly. This is a message of hope, not fatalism.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Neuroplasticity is best described, in Thomist terms, as…",[["the soul changing species","No."],["the bodily correlate of habit formation","Correct."],["proof of materialism","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s4",h:"4. Brain lesions, dementia and the person",html:`<p>Thomas: when imagination or memory is hindered by a bodily lesion, \"a man is hindered from actually understanding\" even what he knew (I q.84 a.7). Dementia, brain injury and severe mental illness impair the <em>use</em> of reason, not the <em>soul's</em> existence or dignity.</p>
<div class="co case"><div class="co-h">Case · The grandmother with Alzheimer's</div><p>She no longer recognises her son. Is she still \"there\"? Thomism: yes. She is the same person, a living human being whose rational soul cannot express itself through a damaged organ. Her dignity, and our duty of love, are undiminished (CCC 2276–2279).</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Brain injury, for Thomas, affects…",[["the existence of the soul","No."],["the use of reason through damaged bodily powers","Correct: I q.84 a.7."],["nothing","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s5",h:"5. Biology in the six-level map",html:`<p>Biological psychology fills the first level of the causal map: genes, neurotransmitters, hormones, inflammation, sleep, nutrition, and substances. Key points for integration:</p>
<ol><li><strong>Biological causes are real</strong> (I-II q.17 a.7) and justify medical treatment without shame.</li><li><strong>They are rarely sufficient</strong>: most disorders are multifactorial (P1 lesson 2).</li><li><strong>The biological is not the opposite of the moral</strong>: choices change brains (habit), and brains incline choices (temperament).</li><li><strong>Lifestyle is medicine</strong>: exercise, sleep and nutrition affect mood and cognition; Thomas recommends sleep and baths for sorrow (I-II q.38 a.5).</li></ol>`,
  check:Q("Which statement fits the lesson?",[["If a cause is biological, morality is irrelevant","No."],["Choices change brains, and brains incline choices","Correct."],["Biology never affects the passions","No."]],1)}
 ],
 quiz:[
  Q("Hylomorphism holds that the soul is…",[["a ghost in a machine","Cartesian caricature."],["the form of the body","Correct."],["the brain","Materialism."],["an illusion","Eliminativism."]],1,"ST I q.76 a.1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["soul-form"],diff:1}),
  Q("In Libet's study, brain activity preceded reported intention by about…",[["350 ms on average","Correct."],["10 seconds","No."],["0 ms","No."],["one hour","No."]],0,"Libet (1983)",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:2}),
  Q("The mereological fallacy attributes to the brain…",[["what belongs to the whole person","Correct."],["chemical activity","No."],["electrical activity","No."],["nothing","No."]],0,"Bennett and Hacker",{bloom:"Understand",terms:[],diff:2}),
  Q("Thomas's paradigm of free choice is…",[["an arbitrary finger movement","No."],["deliberate choice among goods","Correct."],["a reflex","No."],["a feeling","No."]],1,"ST I q.83",{bloom:"Analyse",terms:["free-choice"],diff:2}),
  Q("Neuroplasticity implies that…",[["the brain cannot change","No."],["repeated acts change neural networks","Correct."],["habits are purely spiritual","No."],["genes decide all","No."]],1,"Neuroplasticity",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["neuroplasticity"],diff:1}),
  Q("A person with advanced dementia, on Thomas's view…",[["has lost the soul","No."],["retains the soul and dignity, with impaired use of reason","Correct."],["is no longer human","No."],["is morally culpable for confusion","No."]],1,"ST I q.84 a.7",{bloom:"Apply",terms:[],diff:1}),
  Q("Which Thomist text recommends sleep and baths for sorrow?",[["I-II q.38 a.5","Correct."],["I q.2 a.3","No."],["III q.75","No."],["II-II q.64","No."]],0,"ST I-II q.38 a.5",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["sorrow"],diff:1}),
  Q("Libet himself held that consciousness could…",[["never affect action","No."],["veto an initiated action","Correct."],["predict lottery numbers","No."],["cause the readiness potential","Not his claim."]],1,"Libet",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:3})
 ],
 construct:{q:"Whether the intellect can actually understand through the intelligible species it possesses, without turning to phantasms?",ref:"ST I q.84 a.7",
  model:{obj:"It seems so: the intellect is not a bodily power, so it should not need bodily images.",sc:"The Philosopher: \"the soul understands nothing without a phantasm\".",resp:"In this life the intellect's proper object is the nature existing in corporeal matter, known through the senses and imagination. So it must turn to phantasms to understand actually. This is shown by experience: when the act of imagination is hindered by a bodily lesion, as in frenzy, or memory as in lethargy, a man is hindered from understanding even what he knew before.",rep:"The intellect is not an organ, but in this life it uses organs as instruments supplying its objects; damage to them hinders its use, not its existence."},
  rubric:["Objection: the intellect is immaterial.","Sed contra: Aristotle.","Respondeo: proper object in this life.","Respondeo: the lesion argument.","Reply: instrument vs organ."]},
 drill:{title:"Order the steps from sensation to understanding (Thomas)",parts:["External senses receive sensible forms","Common sense unifies them","Imagination retains the image (phantasm)","Agent intellect abstracts the intelligible species","Possible intellect understands, turning to the phantasm"]},
 connections:`<p><strong>Back to F5.</strong> This lesson applies Thomas's anthropology to neuroscience.</p><p><strong>Honours.</strong> Neuroscience of psychopathy (P3) must be interpreted with these distinctions.</p>`},

p7:{id:"p7",mod:"p5",ord:3,code:"P2 · Lesson 3",title:"Cognitive psychology: perception, bias, belief and the cogitative power",
 outcomes:["Explain heuristics and biases (Tversky and Kahneman, 1974) and dual-process theories.","Explain Thomas's interior senses, especially the cogitative power (I q.78 a.4).","Relate cognitive distortions in CBT to the wound of ignorance and to prudence.","Explain how beliefs form and change, and the role of the will in belief.","Apply this to clinical work and to apologetics."],
 prereq:["cogitative","heuristic","prudence","wounds-nature"],
 reading:{primary:[["ST I q.78 a.4 (interior senses)",NA("1078.htm#article4")],["ST II-II q.53 (imprudence: haste, thoughtlessness)",NA("3053.htm")]],
  secondary:["Tversky, A. and Kahneman, D. (1974) 'Judgment under uncertainty: heuristics and biases', Science, 185(4157), pp. 1124–1131. doi:10.1126/science.185.4157.1124.","Kahneman, D. (2011) Thinking, Fast and Slow. London: Allen Lane.","Beck, A.T. (1979) Cognitive Therapy of Depression. New York: Guilford.","Hofmann, S.G. et al. (2012) 'The efficacy of cognitive behavioral therapy: a review of meta-analyses', Cognitive Therapy and Research, 36(5), pp. 427–440. doi:10.1007/s10608-012-9476-1."]},
 scripture:[["John 7:24, judge not according to appearance",null]],
 sections:[
 {id:"s1",h:"1. Heuristics and biases",html:`<p>Tversky and Kahneman (1974) described three heuristics: <strong>representativeness</strong> (judging by resemblance), <strong>availability</strong> (judging by ease of recall), and <strong>anchoring</strong> (adjusting from a starting value). \"These heuristics are highly economical and usually effective, but they lead to systematic and predictable errors\" (doi:10.1126/science.185.4157.1124).</p>
<p><strong>Dual-process theories</strong> (Kahneman, 2011) distinguish fast, automatic, intuitive processing (\"System 1\") from slow, effortful, reflective processing (\"System 2\"). The distinction is a useful simplification; researchers debate whether there are really two systems.</p>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>System 1 corresponds roughly to the interior senses and passions (especially the cogitative power), System 2 to discursive reason. Thomas's parts of prudence include \"shrewdness\" (quick, accurate estimation) and \"reason\" (careful inference) (II-II q.49): good thinking needs both, trained.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Judging the frequency of something by how easily examples come to mind is the…",[["representativeness heuristic","No."],["availability heuristic","Correct."],["anchoring heuristic","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s2",h:"2. The interior senses and the cogitative power",html:`<p>Thomas distinguishes four interior senses (I q.78 a.4):</p>
<ol><li><strong>Common sense</strong>: unifies the data of the external senses.</li><li><strong>Imagination</strong> (phantasia): retains images.</li><li><strong>Estimative / cogitative power</strong>: perceives \"intentions\" not given by the senses, such as useful or harmful. In animals, instinctive (the sheep fleeing the wolf); in humans, the <em>cogitative</em> power compares particulars and is influenced by reason.</li><li><strong>Memory</strong>: retains such intentions with their pastness.</li></ol>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Why this matters clinically</div><p>Anxiety, trauma and depression often begin as distorted <em>estimations</em>: a harmless situation perceived as threatening, a neutral face read as rejecting. Because the human cogitative power is open to reason's influence, cognitive therapy can retrain it: this is the Thomist basis for CBT's success.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Which interior sense perceives a situation as dangerous or useful?",[["Common sense","No."],["Cogitative (estimative) power","Correct."],["Memory","It retains such perceptions."]],1)},
 {id:"s3",h:"3. Cognitive distortions and the wound of ignorance",html:`<p>Beck's cognitive model: situations trigger <strong>automatic thoughts</strong>, shaped by underlying beliefs (schemas), which produce emotions and behaviour. Common distortions: all-or-nothing thinking, catastrophising, mind-reading, overgeneralisation, personalisation, emotional reasoning.</p>
<div class="co core"><div class="co-h">[Core] Evidence</div><p>A review of 106 meta-analyses found CBT effective across many conditions, with especially strong support for anxiety disorders, somatoform disorders, bulimia, anger and general stress (Hofmann et al., 2012, doi:10.1007/s10608-012-9476-1).</p></div>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>Distortions are particular cases of the wound of ignorance: reason \"obscured, especially in practical matters\" (I-II q.85 a.3). Thomas's vices against prudence include <em>precipitation</em> (acting without counsel) and <em>thoughtlessness</em> (failing to consider rightly) (II-II q.53). CBT is, in effect, a disciplined practice of the parts of prudence: memory (what actually happened?), docility (what would a wise friend say?), circumspection (what are all the circumstances?).</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Which vice against prudence is closest to \"jumping to conclusions\"?",[["Precipitation","Correct: acting without due counsel."],["Docility","A part of prudence."],["Magnanimity","No."]],0)},
 {id:"s4",h:"4. Belief, motivated reasoning and the will",html:`<p>Psychology shows that people often believe what they want to believe (motivated reasoning) and seek confirming evidence (confirmation bias).</p>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>Thomas holds that when evidence is not compelling, assent can be moved by the will (II-II q.1 a.4; q.2 a.9). This is good in faith (moved by God's authority under grace) and dangerous in opinion (moved by self-love). The intellectual virtue of studiousness (II-II q.166) and the moral virtues guard against wishful thinking.</p></div>
<div class="co apply"><div class="co-h">[Apply] Apologetics</div><p>Objections to faith are not always intellectual. Ask gently what the person hopes or fears is true. Intellectual answers matter, but so do the will and the passions (see the Apologetics track).</p></div>`,
  check:Q("For Thomas, when evidence is not compelling, assent can be moved by…",[["the will","Correct."],["the senses alone","No."],["nothing","No."]],0)},
 {id:"s5",h:"5. Cognition in the six-level map",html:`${ETIO(["fatigue, intoxication, brain injury, attention disorders","learned schemas from family and school","automatic thoughts, biases, distorted estimation (cogitative power)","habits of thought: rashness, thoughtlessness, or studiousness","misinformation, propaganda, social media echo chambers","truth as a good loved for its own sake; humility before reality"],"Where errors of judgment come from")}`,
  check:Q("Social media echo chambers belong to which level?",[["Body","No."],["Relationships and society","Correct."],["Spiritual","No."]],1)}
 ],
 quiz:[
  Q("Which is NOT one of Tversky and Kahneman's three heuristics?",[["Availability","It is."],["Representativeness","It is."],["Anchoring","It is."],["Reciprocity","Correct: not one of the three."]],3,"Tversky and Kahneman (1974)",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["heuristic"],diff:1}),
  Q("Thomas's interior senses are…",[["sight, hearing, touch, taste","External."],["common sense, imagination, cogitative, memory","Correct."],["intellect and will","Spiritual powers."],["love and hate","Passions."]],1,"ST I q.78 a.4",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["cogitative"],diff:1}),
  Q("In humans, the estimative power is called…",[["the cogitative power","Correct."],["synderesis","No."],["the agent intellect","No."],["the common sense","No."]],0,"ST I q.78 a.4",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["cogitative"],diff:2}),
  Q("In Beck's model, emotions follow most directly from…",[["situations alone","No."],["automatic thoughts","Correct."],["genes alone","No."],["dreams","No."]],1,"Beck",{bloom:"Understand",terms:[],diff:1}),
  Q("Catastrophising corresponds best to which wound?",[["Ignorance (reason obscured in practical matters)","Correct."],["Malice","No."],["Concupiscence","No."],["None","No."]],0,"ST I-II q.85 a.3",{bloom:"Analyse",terms:["wounds-nature"],diff:2}),
  Q("Why can CBT retrain the cogitative power in humans?",[["Because it is open to reason's influence","Correct."],["Because it is the intellect","No."],["Because animals do CBT","No."],["It cannot","No."]],0,"ST I q.81 a.3; q.78 a.4",{bloom:"Analyse",terms:["cogitative"],diff:3}),
  Q("The virtue opposed to wishful or careless inquiry is…",[["studiousness","Correct: II-II q.166."],["gluttony","No."],["fortitude","No."],["meekness","No."]],0,"ST II-II q.166",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:2}),
  Q("Hofmann et al. (2012) reviewed how many meta-analyses in detail?",[["6","No."],["106","Correct."],["1,006","No."],["10","No."]],1,"doi:10.1007/s10608-012-9476-1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:2})
 ],
 construct:{q:"Whether the interior senses are suitably distinguished?",ref:"ST I q.78 a.4",
  model:{obj:"It seems not: the common sense and imagination are the same power; and there is no need for an estimative power perceiving intentions the senses do not perceive.",sc:"Avicenna assigns five interior powers.",resp:"An animal must not only receive sensible forms but retain them (imagination), unify them (common sense), apprehend intentions not given by the external senses, such as harmful or useful (estimative; in man, the cogitative, which compares particular intentions), and retain these intentions (memory). These require distinct powers.",rep:"Receiving and retaining require different principles; and perceiving intentions is a different act from perceiving sensible qualities."},
  rubric:["Objection reduces the number of powers.","Sed contra cites Avicenna.","Respondeo: receive, retain, unify, estimate, remember.","Respondeo: cogitative power in humans.","Reply: different acts require different powers."]},
 drill:{title:"Order a CBT thought record",parts:["Situation","Automatic thought","Emotion and its intensity","Evidence for and against","Balanced alternative thought"]},
 connections:`<p><strong>RES 320.</strong> Biases are why research methods exist: randomisation, blinding and replication correct our cognition.</p><p><strong>Forward.</strong> P3 lessons on anxiety and depression apply this lesson.</p>`},

p8:{id:"p8",mod:"p5",ord:4,code:"P2 · Lesson 4",title:"Emotion and motivation: the passions, affective science and self-determination",
 outcomes:["Compare modern theories of emotion with Thomas's eleven passions.","Summarise evidence on emotion-regulation strategies (Webb et al., 2012).","Explain motivation theories (self-determination theory) and relate them to the last end.","Distinguish antecedent and consequent passions and their moral significance.","Apply emotion regulation in a Thomistic key: reason rules the passions politically."],
 prereq:["passions","emotion-regulation","beatitude"],
 reading:{primary:[["ST I-II q.22–25 (the passions in general)",NA("2022.htm")],["ST I-II q.24 a.3 (passions and moral goodness)",NA("2024.htm#article3")]],
  secondary:["Webb, T.L., Miles, E. and Sheeran, P. (2012) 'Dealing with feeling: a meta-analysis of the effectiveness of strategies derived from the process model of emotion regulation', Psychological Bulletin, 138(4), pp. 775–808. doi:10.1037/a0027600.","Ryan, R.M. and Deci, E.L. (2017) Self-Determination Theory. New York: Guilford.","Lombardo, N.E. (2011) The Logic of Desire: Aquinas on Emotion. Washington, DC: CUA Press."]},
 scripture:[["John 11:35, Jesus wept",null],["John 2:13–17, zeal for thy house","j2-12"]],
 sections:[
 {id:"s1",h:"1. What emotions are: modern and Thomist accounts",html:`<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Theory</th><th>Emotion is…</th><th>Thomist parallel</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Basic emotions (Ekman)</td><td>a small set of universal, biologically based responses</td><td>the eleven passions as natural to human beings</td></tr>
<tr><td>Appraisal theories (Lazarus, Scherer)</td><td>a response to an evaluation of a situation's significance for me</td><td>passions follow apprehension (cogitative power)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Constructionist (Barrett)</td><td>constructed from core affect and concepts</td><td>partial: reason's concepts shape how passions are experienced</td></tr>
<tr><td>Thomas</td><td>a movement of the sensitive appetite in response to an apprehended good or evil, with bodily change (I-II q.22)</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>The eleven passions</strong> (F5): concupiscible (love, desire, joy; hatred, aversion, sorrow) and irascible (hope, despair, daring, fear, anger). Thomas's map includes appraisal (apprehension), physiology (bodily change), and action tendency (pursuit or avoidance).</p>`,
  check:Q("Appraisal theories are closest to which Thomist claim?",[["Passions follow apprehension","Correct."],["Passions are sins","No."],["Passions are purely bodily","No."]],0)},
 {id:"s2",h:"2. Regulating emotion: what works",html:`<div class="co core"><div class="co-h">[Core] Evidence (Webb, Miles and Sheeran, 2012; 306 comparisons)</div><ul><li><strong>Attentional deployment</strong> overall: no effect (d = 0.00); but <strong>distraction</strong> helped (d = 0.27) while <strong>concentrating on the emotion</strong> hurt (d = −0.26).</li><li><strong>Response modulation</strong>: small (d = 0.16). Suppressing <em>expression</em> helped (d = 0.32); suppressing the <em>experience</em> or the <em>thoughts</em> did not (d = −0.04, −0.12).</li><li><strong>Cognitive change</strong>: small-to-medium (d = 0.36): reappraising the stimulus (d = 0.36) and perspective-taking (d = 0.45) beat reappraising the response (d = 0.23).</li></ul><p>doi:10.1037/a0027600. These are short-term laboratory effects.</p></div>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>Reason rules the passions \"politically\" (I q.81 a.3 ad 2): not by suppressing the feeling, but by re-presenting the object differently (reappraisal) or turning attention (distraction). Perspective-taking, the strongest strategy, is close to the virtue of seeing a situation as a wise friend, or as God, would see it.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Which strategy had the largest effect in Webb et al. (2012)?",[["Concentrating on the emotion","Harmful."],["Perspective-taking","Correct: d = 0.45."],["Suppressing the experience","Ineffective."]],1)},
 {id:"s3",h:"3. Antecedent and consequent passions",html:`<p>Thomas distinguishes passions that arise <strong>before</strong> reason's judgment (antecedent) from those that <strong>follow</strong> it (consequent) (I-II q.24 a.3 ad 1; q.77 a.6).</p>
<ul><li><strong>Antecedent passion</strong> can reduce culpability, because it clouds judgment.</li><li><strong>Consequent passion</strong>, aroused by a judgment of the will, can increase merit: when a person, having judged rightly, also feels intensely, the act is more complete (\"it belongs to the perfection of moral good, that man should be moved unto good… also in respect of his sensitive appetite\", q.24 a.3).</li></ul>
<div class="co case"><div class="co-h">Case · Two kinds of anger</div><p>A father flares up instantly when his son is insulted (antecedent): his responsibility for what he blurts out may be reduced. A judge, having reviewed evidence of injustice, feels righteous anger that strengthens her resolve (consequent): the passion adds to the goodness of her act.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("A passion that follows a judgment of reason and strengthens a good act is…",[["antecedent","No."],["consequent","Correct."],["sinful","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s4",h:"4. Motivation: needs, goals and the last end",html:`<p><strong>Self-determination theory</strong> (Ryan and Deci) proposes three basic psychological needs: autonomy, competence and relatedness. Intrinsic motivation and internalised values predict well-being better than external rewards or pressure.</p>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>Thomas holds that every agent acts for an end and that the will naturally desires happiness (I-II q.1; q.5 a.8). SDT's three needs map loosely onto natural goods: freedom (the will's self-determination), knowledge and skill (perfection of powers), and friendship (communion). Thomas adds what SDT cannot: that these needs point beyond themselves to God, the universal good (I-II q.2 a.8). Purely intrinsic motivation without a true end can still be disordered.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("SDT's three basic needs are…",[["food, shelter, sex","No."],["autonomy, competence, relatedness","Correct."],["love, hate, fear","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s5",h:"5. When emotion goes wrong: a map",html:`${ETIO(["temperament, hormones, sleep, substances, brain changes","learned emotional responses; attachment; trauma","distorted estimation (threat, loss, insult)","vices: wrath, despair, envy; or virtues: meekness, hope","chronic stress, poverty, violence, isolation","hope or despair about God; sense of meaning"],"Where emotional disorder comes from")}`,
  check:Q("Chronic exposure to community violence is at which level?",[["Relationships and society","Correct."],["Spiritual","No."],["Body","Its effects reach the body, but the cause is social."]],0)}
 ],
 quiz:[
  Q("Thomas defines a passion as a movement of…",[["the intellect","No."],["the sensitive appetite with bodily change","Correct."],["the will only","No."],["the imagination","No."]],1,"ST I-II q.22",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["passions"],diff:1}),
  Q("Which is an irascible passion?",[["Joy","Concupiscible."],["Hope","Correct."],["Love","Concupiscible."],["Sorrow","Concupiscible."]],1,"F5",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["passions"],diff:1}),
  Q("Suppressing the experience of an emotion had what effect in Webb et al.?",[["Large benefit","No."],["No benefit (d = −0.04)","Correct."],["Medium benefit","No."],["Not studied","No."]],1,"doi:10.1037/a0027600",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["emotion-regulation"],diff:2}),
  Q("Reason rules the passions…",[["despotically","No."],["politically","Correct."],["not at all","No."],["only by grace","No."]],1,"ST I q.81 a.3 ad 2",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["passions"],diff:1}),
  Q("An antecedent passion can…",[["increase culpability","No."],["reduce culpability","Correct."],["never affect action","No."],["only occur in animals","No."]],1,"ST I-II q.77 a.6",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["imputability"],diff:2}),
  Q("SDT is associated with…",[["Ryan and Deci","Correct."],["Beck","CBT."],["Bowlby","Attachment."],["Skinner","Behaviourism."]],0,"SDT",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:1}),
  Q("What does Thomas add to SDT's needs?",[["Nothing","No."],["That human desire points beyond itself to God as the universal good","Correct."],["That needs are illusions","No."],["That autonomy is evil","No."]],1,"ST I-II q.2 a.8",{bloom:"Evaluate",terms:["beatitude"],diff:2}),
  Q("Perspective-taking corresponds to…",[["seeing a situation as a wise friend or God would","Correct."],["ignoring the situation","No."],["suppressing the emotion","No."],["concentrating on the feeling","No."]],0,"P2 lesson 4",{bloom:"Apply",terms:["emotion-regulation"],diff:2})
 ],
 construct:{q:"Whether passion increases or decreases the goodness or malice of an act?",ref:"ST I-II q.24 a.3",
  model:{obj:"It seems that every passion decreases the goodness of an act, since it clouds reason's judgment.",sc:"Augustine says the passions are good if love is good; and Psalm 83:3: \"My heart and my flesh have rejoiced in the living God.\"",resp:"Passions considered in themselves are neither good nor evil, but are good insofar as they are subject to reason. Antecedent passions, which cloud judgment, diminish the goodness of an act; consequent passions, which follow reason's judgment, either as overflowing from an intense will or as chosen, increase it. For it belongs to the perfection of moral good that man be moved to good not only by his will but also by his sensitive appetite.",rep:"The Stoics wrongly held all passion to be bad; passion clouds reason only when it precedes reason."},
  rubric:["Objection: passion clouds judgment.","Sed contra: Augustine or Psalm 83.","Respondeo: passions good when subject to reason.","Respondeo: antecedent vs consequent.","Reply: against the Stoics."]},
 drill:{title:"Order Gross's emotion-regulation process",parts:["Situation selection","Situation modification","Attentional deployment","Cognitive change","Response modulation"]},
 connections:`<p><strong>Back to F5 and T8.</strong> Temperance and fortitude are habits of rightly ordered emotion.</p><p><strong>Forward.</strong> P3 lessons on depression and anxiety apply emotion regulation.</p>`},

p9:{id:"p9",mod:"p5",ord:5,code:"P2 · Lesson 5",title:"Learning and habit: conditioning, behaviour change and the virtues",
 outcomes:["Explain classical and operant conditioning and their clinical uses.","Explain habit formation research and how habits change.","Relate behaviourism to Thomas's account of habitus without reducing the person to conditioning.","Explain self-control and the evidence linking religion to self-regulation (McCullough and Willoughby, 2009).","Design a virtue-building plan using learning principles."],
 prereq:["habitus","virtue","neuroplasticity"],
 reading:{primary:[["ST I-II qq.49–54 (habits)",NA("2049.htm")],["ST I-II q.63 a.2 (virtue by habituation)",NA("2063.htm#article2")]],
  secondary:["McCullough, M.E. and Willoughby, B.L.B. (2009) 'Religion, self-regulation, and self-control', Psychological Bulletin, 135(1), pp. 69–93. doi:10.1037/a0014213.","Wood, W. and Rünger, D. (2016) 'Psychology of habit', Annual Review of Psychology, 67, pp. 289–314.","Skinner, B.F. (1953) Science and Human Behavior. New York: Macmillan."]},
 scripture:[["Romans 7:15–25, the law in my members",null]],
 sections:[
 {id:"s1",h:"1. Conditioning",html:`<p><strong>Classical conditioning</strong> (Pavlov): a neutral stimulus paired with an unconditioned stimulus comes to elicit a response (fear of dentists from painful visits). <strong>Operant conditioning</strong> (Thorndike, Skinner): behaviour followed by reinforcement increases; followed by punishment, decreases. <strong>Extinction</strong>: without reinforcement, responses weaken, though they can return (spontaneous recovery).</p>
<p>Clinical uses: exposure therapy (extinction of fear), contingency management (rewarding abstinence), behavioural activation (scheduling rewarding activities).</p>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>Thomas agrees that the sensitive appetite is trained by experience, as animals are trained (I-II q.50 a.3 ad 2). But in humans, the sensitive appetite can also be moved by reason, so conditioning is not the whole story: the person can understand the pattern and choose to act against it.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Exposure therapy works chiefly by…",[["reinforcement","No."],["extinction of conditioned fear","Correct."],["punishment","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s2",h:"2. Habits: what psychology knows",html:`<p>Habits are behaviours cued automatically by context through repeated performance (Wood and Rünger, 2016). Key findings: habits persist even when intentions change; changing contexts (moving house, a new term) disrupts habits and creates windows for change; implementation intentions (\"when X, I will Y\") help start new habits.</p>
<div class="co extra"><div class="co-h">Note on a popular figure</div><p>The often-quoted \"66 days to form a habit\" comes from a small study of 96 people with wide variation (18 to 254 days) (Lally et al., 2010, European Journal of Social Psychology). Treat it as an average, not a rule.</p></div>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>Thomas's <em>habitus</em> is richer than automaticity: a habit is a quality disposing a power to act well or badly, readily and with pleasure (I-II q.49). Virtue makes acting well easy and delightful; it is not mere routine but \"second nature\" that serves freedom.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Habits differ from virtues, for Thomas, because virtues are…",[["unconscious routines","No."],["good habits perfecting a power to act well, readily and with pleasure, under reason","Correct."],["innate","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s3",h:"3. Self-control and religion",html:`<div class="co core"><div class="co-h">[Core] Evidence</div><p>McCullough and Willoughby (2009) reviewed evidence that religion promotes self-control: it influences how goals are chosen and organised, facilitates self-monitoring, builds self-regulatory strength, and prescribes self-regulatory behaviours (prayer, fasting, examination of conscience). They suggest that some of religion's links with health and well-being may run through self-control (doi:10.1037/a0014213).</p></div>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>Fasting, the examen and regular prayer are classic exercises of temperance, prudence and religion. Their psychological effect is real; their primary purpose is God. Note also the marshmallow test: a large conceptual replication found the link between early delay of gratification and later achievement was half the original size and reduced by two-thirds after controlling for background (Watts, Duncan and Quan, 2018, doi:10.1177/0956797618761661). Self-control matters, but environment matters too.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Watts et al. (2018) found the marshmallow-test association with later achievement was…",[["twice as large as claimed","No."],["about half the original size and much reduced after controls","Correct."],["exactly as Mischel found","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s4",h:"4. Continence, incontinence and temperance",html:`<p>Aristotle and Thomas distinguish four characters:</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Character</th><th>Desires</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Temperate</td><td>ordered</td><td>good, with ease</td></tr>
<tr><td>Continent</td><td>disordered, resisted</td><td>good, with struggle (II-II q.155)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Incontinent</td><td>disordered, followed against judgment</td><td>bad, with regret (II-II q.156)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Intemperate</td><td>disordered, endorsed</td><td>bad, without regret</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Thomas notes that bodily dispositions are only the occasion of incontinence, \"since it is owing to a bodily disposition that vehement passions can arise in the sensitive appetite\"; the will's yielding is the cause (II-II q.156 a.1).</p>`,
  check:Q("The continent person…",[["has ordered desires","Temperate."],["resists disordered desires and acts well with struggle","Correct."],["follows disordered desires with regret","Incontinent."]],1)},
 {id:"s5",h:"5. A virtue-building plan using learning principles",html:`<ol><li><strong>Choose one virtue</strong> and one behaviour (e.g. temperance: no phone in bed).</li><li><strong>Change the context</strong>: charge the phone outside the room (situation selection).</li><li><strong>Implementation intention</strong>: \"When I get into bed, I will read for ten minutes.\"</li><li><strong>Track</strong> it (the particular examen, Ignatius).</li><li><strong>Reinforce</strong>: notice and thank God for small successes; confess and restart after failures.</li><li><strong>Seek grace</strong>: sacraments heal and strengthen beyond natural habit (I-II q.63 a.3).</li></ol>
${ETIO(["fatigue, reward circuitry, substances","what was modelled and reinforced at home","cues and expectations","repeated choices: virtue or vice","social norms, peer pressure, availability","grace, prayer, sacraments"],"Where habits come from")}`,
  check:Q("Ignatius's tool for tracking one fault is…",[["the general confession","No."],["the particular examen","Correct."],["the Rosary","No."]],1)}
 ],
 quiz:[
  Q("Operant conditioning is associated with…",[["Pavlov","Classical."],["Skinner","Correct."],["Bowlby","No."],["Beck","No."]],1,"Skinner",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:1}),
  Q("Behavioural activation uses…",[["scheduling rewarding activities","Correct."],["punishment","No."],["hypnosis","No."],["dream analysis","No."]],0,"BA",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:1}),
  Q("The \"66 days\" habit figure…",[["is a fixed rule","No."],["is an average with wide variation (18–254 days) from a small study","Correct."],["comes from Thomas","No."],["was disproved entirely","No."]],1,"Lally et al. (2010)",{bloom:"Evaluate",terms:["habitus"],diff:2}),
  Q("McCullough and Willoughby (2009) propose that religion promotes…",[["impulsivity","No."],["self-control and self-regulation","Correct."],["anxiety","No."],["conformity only","No."]],1,"doi:10.1037/a0014213",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:1}),
  Q("The person who acts badly against his own judgment, with regret, is…",[["intemperate","No regret."],["incontinent","Correct."],["continent","Acts well."],["temperate","No."]],1,"ST II-II q.156",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["temperance"],diff:2}),
  Q("For Thomas, bodily disposition in incontinence is…",[["the sufficient cause","No."],["only the occasion","Correct: II-II q.156 a.1."],["irrelevant","No."],["a sin","No."]],1,"ST II-II q.156 a.1",{bloom:"Understand",terms:[],diff:2}),
  Q("Changing context helps habit change because…",[["habits are cued by context","Correct."],["willpower is unlimited","No."],["habits are innate","No."],["context is irrelevant","No."]],0,"Wood and Rünger (2016)",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["habitus"],diff:2}),
  Q("Infused virtues differ from acquired ones in that they…",[["come with grace","Correct."],["require no acts","They issue in acts."],["are bodily","No."],["are temporary","No."]],0,"ST I-II q.63 a.3",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["virtue"],diff:2})
 ],
 construct:{q:"Whether a habit can be caused by one act?",ref:"ST I-II q.51 a.3",
  model:{obj:"It seems so: demonstration causes science, which is a habit, by one act.",sc:"The Philosopher: \"one swallow does not make a spring, nor one day; so neither does one day or a short time make a man blessed or happy\".",resp:"A habit is caused when the active principle overcomes the passive one. Reason cannot entirely overcome the appetitive power in one act, because the appetite is inclined variously to many things; so habits of virtue need many acts. In the intellect, a single demonstration can sometimes cause science of one conclusion, but lower powers, like memory, need repetition.",rep:"Some intellectual habits can arise from one act; moral habits need many."},
  rubric:["Objection: science from one demonstration.","Sed contra: one swallow.","Respondeo: active must overcome passive.","Respondeo: appetite inclined variously.","Reply: intellect vs appetite."]},
 drill:{title:"Order the steps of a habit-change plan",parts:["Choose one virtue and one behaviour","Change the context","Form an implementation intention","Track with the particular examen","Reinforce, repent and restart, with grace"]},
 connections:`<p><strong>Back to F7 and T8.</strong> Virtue as habit.</p><p><strong>Forward.</strong> P3 addiction lesson.</p>`},

p10:{id:"p10",mod:"p5",ord:6,code:"P2 · Lesson 6",title:"Personality: traits, temperament, character and virtue",
 outcomes:["Explain the Big Five and their heritability (Vukasović and Bratko, 2015).","Distinguish temperament, personality traits, character and virtue.","Relate the medieval temperaments and Thomas's complexio to modern temperament research.","Explain the DSM-5-TR alternative model of personality disorders.","Explain how grace and virtue relate to stable traits."],
 prereq:["temperament","heritability","virtue","habitus"],
 reading:{primary:[["ST I-II q.46 a.5 (anger and bodily disposition)",NA("2046.htm#article5")],["ST I-II q.63 a.1 (virtue in us by nature?)",NA("2063.htm#article1")]],
  secondary:["Vukasović, T. and Bratko, D. (2015) 'Heritability of personality: a meta-analysis of behavior genetic studies', Psychological Bulletin, 141(4), pp. 769–785. doi:10.1037/bul0000017.","McCrae, R.R. and Costa, P.T. (2003) Personality in Adulthood: A Five-Factor Theory Perspective. 2nd edn. New York: Guilford.","Peterson, C. and Seligman, M.E.P. (2004) Character Strengths and Virtues. New York: OUP."]},
 scripture:[["John 1:47, a true Israelite in whom there is no guile","j1-47"]],
 sections:[
 {id:"s1",h:"1. The Big Five",html:`<p>The five-factor model: <strong>Openness</strong>, <strong>Conscientiousness</strong>, <strong>Extraversion</strong>, <strong>Agreeableness</strong>, <strong>Neuroticism</strong> (OCEAN). Traits are relatively stable, moderately heritable, and change gradually across life (people tend to become more conscientious and agreeable, less neurotic, with age).</p>
<div class="co core"><div class="co-h">[Core] Heritability</div><p>A meta-analysis of 62 independent effect sizes (over 100,000 participants) found average heritability of .40: \"40% of individual differences in personality were due to genetic, while 60% are due to environmental influences\"; twin studies gave higher estimates (.47) than family and adoption studies (.22) (Vukasović and Bratko, 2015, doi:10.1037/bul0000017).</p></div>
<p><strong>Remember</strong>: heritability describes differences in a population, not how much of one person's personality is \"genetic\".</p>`,
  check:Q("Average heritability of personality in Vukasović and Bratko (2015) was…",[["0.04","No."],["0.40","Correct."],["0.90","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s2",h:"2. Temperament, personality, character, virtue",html:`<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Origin</th><th>Moral weight</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Temperament</td><td>largely inborn, biological</td><td>none in itself: a starting point</td></tr>
<tr><td>Personality traits</td><td>temperament + experience</td><td>morally neutral patterns</td></tr>
<tr><td>Character</td><td>formed by choices over time</td><td>morally significant</td></tr>
<tr><td>Virtue</td><td>good habit by repeated good acts (or infused by grace)</td><td>the perfection of character</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>Thomas: virtue is in us by nature only as an aptitude and \"beginning\"; some individuals, by bodily disposition, \"are disposed either well or ill to certain virtues\" (I-II q.63 a.1). A naturally agreeable person may find meekness easier; a naturally anxious one may find fortitude harder. Merit lies in the use of freedom, not in the starting temperament.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Which carries moral weight in itself?",[["Temperament","No."],["Character formed by choices","Correct."],["Eye colour","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s3",h:"3. The four temperaments, then and now",html:`<p>The ancient theory (Hippocrates, Galen) of four humours yielded four temperaments: sanguine, choleric, melancholic, phlegmatic. Thomas uses the language of bodily <em>complexio</em>: for instance, choleric persons are quicker to anger because of bodily disposition (I-II q.46 a.5).</p>
<p>The humoral physiology is obsolete, but the observation that bodily dispositions incline us to certain passions is confirmed by temperament research (e.g. behavioural inhibition in infancy predicting later anxiety) and by heritability studies. Modern Catholic writers sometimes use the four temperaments as a practical tool for spiritual direction; treat them as heuristic labels, not science.</p>`,
  check:Q("The four humours theory is…",[["confirmed by neuroscience","No."],["obsolete physiology, though its observation of bodily dispositions has modern support","Correct."],["a dogma","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s4",h:"4. Personality disorders: categories and dimensions",html:`<p>DSM-5-TR Section II lists ten categorical personality disorders in three clusters: A (odd: paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal), B (dramatic: antisocial, borderline, histrionic, narcissistic), C (anxious: avoidant, dependent, obsessive-compulsive). Section III offers an <strong>Alternative Model</strong>: impairment in self and interpersonal functioning plus pathological traits (negative affectivity, detachment, antagonism, disinhibition, psychoticism). ICD-11 moved fully to a dimensional model (severity plus trait qualifiers).</p>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>Personality disorders are enduring patterns of perceiving, feeling and acting: in Thomist terms, deep habits of the cogitative power and the appetites, often formed early by wounds (development level) and temperament (body level). Some traits resemble vices (antagonism and pride; disinhibition and intemperance), but having a disorder is not the same as culpable vice: imputability depends on knowledge and freedom (P1 lesson 4).</p></div>`,
  check:Q("ICD-11's model of personality disorder is…",[["ten categories","DSM Section II."],["dimensional: severity plus trait qualifiers","Correct."],["abolished","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s5",h:"5. Grace, traits and sanctity",html:`<p>Does grace change personality? Grace perfects nature (I q.1 a.8 ad 2): it does not erase temperament but orders it. Saints show every temperament: the fiery Jerome, the gentle Francis de Sales, the anxious Thérèse of Lisieux in her youth.</p>
<div class="co analogy"><div class="co-h">Analogy · The instruments of an orchestra</div><p>A trumpet does not become a violin when it joins a good orchestra; it plays its own part in tune. Grace does not turn a choleric into a phlegmatic; it teaches each to play rightly.</p></div>
${ETIO(["genes (h² ≈ .40), temperament, brain","attachment and upbringing","characteristic ways of perceiving self and others","habits of choice: virtues and vices","culture, family roles, peers","grace ordering nature"],"Where personality comes from")}`,
  check:Q("Grace and temperament: which is true?",[["Grace erases temperament","No."],["Grace orders temperament without erasing it","Correct."],["Grace has nothing to do with personality","No."]],1)}
 ],
 quiz:[
  Q("OCEAN stands for…",[["Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, Neuroticism","Correct."],["Obedience, Charity, Energy, Anger, Need","No."],["Order, Courage, Empathy, Attention, Nerve","No."],["None","No."]],0,"Big Five",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:1}),
  Q("Twin studies gave heritability estimates of about…",[["0.47","Correct."],["0.22","Family and adoption studies."],["0.90","No."],["0.05","No."]],0,"doi:10.1037/bul0000017",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["heritability"],diff:2}),
  Q("Heritability tells us…",[["how much of one person's trait is genetic","Common misconception."],["how much of the variation in a population is associated with genetic variation","Correct."],["that traits cannot change","No."],["nothing","No."]],1,"Heritability",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["heritability"],diff:2}),
  Q("Thomas says some people are naturally better disposed to certain virtues because of…",[["bodily disposition","Correct: I-II q.63 a.1."],["merit before birth","No."],["astrology","No."],["baptism alone","No."]],0,"ST I-II q.63 a.1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["temperament"],diff:2}),
  Q("Which personality disorder is in Cluster B?",[["Avoidant","Cluster C."],["Borderline","Correct."],["Schizoid","Cluster A."],["Dependent","Cluster C."]],1,"DSM-5-TR",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:1}),
  Q("The DSM-5-TR Alternative Model's trait domains include…",[["negative affectivity, detachment, antagonism, disinhibition, psychoticism","Correct."],["the four humours","No."],["OCEAN exactly","Related, but different labels."],["sins","No."]],0,"DSM-5-TR Section III",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:2}),
  Q("Having a personality disorder is…",[["automatically a culpable vice","No."],["not the same as culpable vice; imputability depends on knowledge and freedom","Correct."],["impossible for Christians","No."],["always demonic","No."]],1,"P1 lesson 4",{bloom:"Evaluate",terms:["imputability"],diff:2}),
  Q("Character Strengths and Virtues (2004) was written by…",[["Peterson and Seligman","Correct."],["Beck and Ellis","No."],["Ryan and Deci","No."],["Tversky and Kahneman","No."]],0,"Peterson and Seligman",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:1})
 ],
 construct:{q:"Whether virtue is in us by nature?",ref:"ST I-II q.63 a.1",
  model:{obj:"It seems so: Damascene says virtues are natural, and some people are born gentle or brave.",sc:"Virtue is acquired by practice; what is natural is not acquired.",resp:"Virtue is natural to man inchoately, both on the part of the species (reason has seeds of virtue: first principles and natural inclinations) and on the part of the individual (bodily dispositions incline some to certain virtues). But virtue in its perfection is not from nature, except in the case of the infused virtues, which are from God; acquired virtue is perfected by practice.",rep:"Natural gentleness is a disposition toward meekness, not yet the virtue, which needs reason's ordering."},
  rubric:["Objection: born gentle or brave.","Sed contra: virtue acquired by practice.","Respondeo: inchoate by nature (species and individual).","Respondeo: perfect by practice or infusion.","Reply: disposition vs virtue."]},
 drill:{title:"Order from least to most morally significant",parts:["Temperament","Personality trait","Habit of action","Character","Virtue"]},
 connections:`<p><strong>SLK 320.</strong> Critical psychology questions the cultural universality of the Big Five; cross-cultural studies (including in South Africa) suggest broad but not perfect replication.</p><p><strong>Forward.</strong> P3 lesson on personality disorders and psychopathy.</p>`},

p11:{id:"p11",mod:"p5",ord:7,code:"P2 · Lesson 7",title:"Social psychology: conformity, obedience, prejudice, and structures of sin",
 outcomes:["Explain the classic findings on conformity and obedience and their replications (Burger, 2009).","Explain the bystander effect, prejudice and intergroup conflict.","Relate these to Thomas on scandal, human respect and fortitude.","Explain \"structures of sin\" (Sollicitudo Rei Socialis 36) and social sin.","Apply to South African history and present challenges."],
 prereq:["conformity","fortitude","common-good"],
 reading:{primary:[["ST II-II q.43 (scandal)","https://www.newadvent.org/summa/3043.htm"],["John Paul II (1987) Sollicitudo Rei Socialis 36–37",'https://www.vatican.va/content/john-paul-ii/en/encyclicals/documents/hf_jp-ii_enc_30121987_sollicitudo-rei-socialis.html'],["CCC 1868–1869",'https://www.vatican.va/archive/ENG0015/__P6E.HTM']],
  secondary:["Burger, J.M. (2009) 'Replicating Milgram: would people still obey today?', American Psychologist, 64(1), pp. 1–11. doi:10.1037/a0010932.","Allport, G.W. (1954) The Nature of Prejudice. Cambridge, MA: Addison-Wesley.","Tajfel, H. and Turner, J.C. (1979) 'An integrative theory of intergroup conflict', in Austin and Worchel (eds) The Social Psychology of Intergroup Relations. Monterey: Brooks/Cole, pp. 33–47."]},
 scripture:[["John 9:22, fear of being put out of the synagogue",null],["John 4:9, Jews and Samaritans","j4-1"]],
 sections:[
 {id:"s1",h:"1. Conformity and obedience",html:`<p><strong>Asch</strong> (1950s): many participants conformed at least once to a group's obviously wrong judgment of line length. <strong>Milgram</strong> (1963): about two-thirds of participants continued to the maximum shock when instructed by an experimenter.</p>
<div class="co core"><div class="co-h">[Core] Replication</div><p>Burger (2009) partially replicated Milgram, stopping at 150 volts for ethical reasons (79% of Milgram's participants who passed that point went to the end). \"Obedience rates in the 2006 replication were only slightly lower than those Milgram found 45 years earlier\"; seeing a confederate refuse did not reduce obedience (doi:10.1037/a0010932).</p></div>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>Thomas treats obedience as a virtue, but never to commands against God or justice: \"we ought to obey God rather than men\" (Acts 5:29; II-II q.104 a.5). Disobeying an unjust command requires fortitude against fear of men's disapproval (II-II q.125, on fear as a sin when it makes us forsake good).</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Burger (2009) found obedience rates were…",[["far lower than Milgram's","No."],["only slightly lower than Milgram's","Correct."],["higher","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s2",h:"2. Bystanders, prejudice and groups",html:`<ul><li><strong>Bystander effect</strong> (Darley and Latané, 1968): the presence of others reduces individual helping, through diffusion of responsibility.</li>
<li><strong>Prejudice</strong> (Allport, 1954): negative attitudes toward groups; the <em>contact hypothesis</em>: equal-status, cooperative contact with institutional support reduces prejudice.</li>
<li><strong>Social identity theory</strong> (Tajfel and Turner): people derive self-esteem from groups and favour their in-group.</li></ul>
<div class="co apply"><div class="co-h">[Apply] South Africa</div><p>Apartheid is a case study in institutionalised prejudice, legal structures and conformity, including by churches. The Dutch Reformed Church's theological justification and later repentance, and the Catholic bishops' and Anglican leaders' opposition, illustrate both conformity and fortitude. Post-1994, contact across groups remains limited by residential and economic separation.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Allport's contact hypothesis requires contact that is…",[["brief and competitive","No."],["equal-status, cooperative, institutionally supported","Correct."],["online only","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s3",h:"3. Scandal and human respect",html:`<p>Thomas defines <strong>scandal</strong> as \"something less rightly said or done that occasions spiritual downfall\" (II-II q.43 a.1, following Jerome). Social psychology shows how powerfully others' example shapes behaviour, which is why scandal is serious, especially by those in authority (CCC 2285).</p>
<p><strong>Human respect</strong> is the traditional name for doing or omitting good out of fear of others' opinion: a failure of fortitude.</p>
<div class="co case"><div class="co-h">Case · The office joke</div><p>Colleagues mock a coworker's accent. A Catholic employee laughs along (conformity). Not laughing, or speaking up kindly, is a small act of fortitude that may also give others permission to stop (Asch found one dissenter reduces conformity sharply).</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Asch found that one dissenter in the group…",[["increased conformity","No."],["sharply reduced conformity","Correct."],["had no effect","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s4",h:"4. Structures of sin",html:`<p>John Paul II: structures of sin are \"rooted in personal sin, and thus always linked to the concrete acts of individuals who introduce these structures, consolidate them and make them difficult to remove\" (Sollicitudo Rei Socialis 36). They \"grow stronger, spread, and become the source of other sins\".</p>
<p>CCC 1869: \"Sins give rise to social situations and institutions that are contrary to the divine goodness.\" They are \"an analogical sense\" of sin: only persons sin properly, but the effects of sin become embedded in institutions and cultures.</p>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>This connects to Thomas's view that secondary precepts of the natural law can be blotted out \"by evil persuasions… or by vicious customs and corrupt habits\" (I-II q.94 a.6): cultures can make wrong seem normal. Social psychology describes the mechanisms; theology names the moral reality.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("According to SRS 36, structures of sin are rooted in…",[["nature","No."],["personal sin","Correct."],["chance","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s5",h:"5. The social level in the causal map",html:`${ETIO(["stress physiology from chronic adversity","socialisation into norms and prejudices","in-group/out-group perception","conformity or fortitude; complicity or courage","institutions, laws, economic structures, media","conversion of hearts and of structures; the Church as counter-culture"],"Where social evils come from")}
<div class="co clinical"><div class="co-h">Clinical practicality</div><p>Clients may carry guilt for structural injustices or wounds from them (racism, xenophobia, gender-based violence). Assess the social level; avoid both individualising structural harm and removing personal agency.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("A Thomist response to structures of sin includes…",[["only personal conversion","Both are needed."],["conversion of hearts and of structures","Correct."],["ignoring structures","No."]],1)}
 ],
 quiz:[
  Q("In Milgram's classic study, about how many went to the maximum shock?",[["one in ten","No."],["about two-thirds","Correct."],["none","No."],["all","No."]],1,"Milgram",{bloom:"Remember",terms:["conformity"],diff:1}),
  Q("Burger stopped at 150 volts because…",[["of ethical concerns, and it predicts continuation","Correct."],["no one went further","No."],["of law","No."],["of cost","No."]],0,"doi:10.1037/a0010932",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["conformity"],diff:2}),
  Q("Diffusion of responsibility explains…",[["the bystander effect","Correct."],["attachment","No."],["the Big Five","No."],["habit","No."]],0,"Darley and Latané",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:1}),
  Q("Thomas defines scandal as…",[["any embarrassment","No."],["something less rightly said or done that occasions spiritual downfall","Correct."],["criticism of authority","No."],["a lie","No."]],1,"ST II-II q.43 a.1",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:2}),
  Q("Obedience is never owed to…",[["just authority","It is owed."],["commands against God or justice","Correct."],["parents","Owed in lawful matters."],["teachers","Owed in lawful matters."]],1,"ST II-II q.104 a.5",{bloom:"Apply",terms:[],diff:1}),
  Q("\"Structures of sin\" is a phrase from…",[["Sollicitudo Rei Socialis (1987)","Correct."],["Trent","No."],["Thomas","No."],["Nicaea","No."]],0,"SRS 36",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:1}),
  Q("Social identity theory was developed by…",[["Tajfel and Turner","Correct."],["Beck","No."],["Ainsworth","No."],["Skinner","No."]],0,"Tajfel and Turner",{bloom:"Remember",terms:[],diff:1}),
  Q("Human respect is a failure of…",[["temperance","No."],["fortitude","Correct."],["chastity","No."],["faith only","No."]],1,"II-II q.125",{bloom:"Understand",terms:["fortitude"],diff:2})
 ],
 construct:{q:"Whether scandal is fittingly defined as something less rightly said or done that occasions spiritual downfall?",ref:"ST II-II q.43 a.1",
  model:{obj:"It seems not: scandal is a sin, but not every occasion of another's downfall is a sin of the one who gives it.",sc:"Jerome's definition, which Thomas adopts.",resp:"The word means a stumbling block in the way. In the spiritual path, a person may be disposed to fall by another's word or deed that is less right, either because it is evil in itself or has the appearance of evil. Such a word or deed, as occasioning (not causing) another's fall, is scandal.",rep:"Scandal is 'given' (active) when the act is less right; it is 'taken' (passive) when another falls through his own malice at a right act; the latter is not the giver's sin."},
  rubric:["Objection: not every occasion of downfall is sin.","Sed contra: Jerome.","Respondeo: stumbling block in the spiritual path.","Respondeo: occasion, not cause.","Reply: active vs passive scandal."]},
 drill:{title:"Order the steps by which a structure of sin grows (SRS 36)",parts:["Personal sins","Introduced into institutions","Consolidated by others' acts","Become difficult to remove","Become the source of further sins"]},
 connections:`<p><strong>SLK 320.</strong> Critical psychology's attention to power and structures meets Catholic social doctrine here.</p><p><strong>KRM 320.</strong> Criminology's social learning and strain theories are social-level causes in the map.</p>`}
});
["p1b","p5","p6","p7","p8","p9","p10","p11"].forEach(k=>shuffleLesson(LESSONS[k]));
